id,category,subcategory,title,content,tags,source_url,source_group,record_type
1,Electronics,Fundamentals,Ohm's Law,"Ohm's Law relates voltage, current, and resistance in a circuit: V = I x R. Given any two values you can solve for the third — for example, a 9V supply across a 470 ohm resistor draws about 19.1 mA.",electronics;fundamentals,https://github.com/munsakad/DMHardware-Chat-Bot/blob/main/generate_dataset.py,original:Ohm's Law,original_reference
2,Electronics,Fundamentals,Power Dissipation,"Electrical power in a resistive element is P = V x I, which can also be written as P = I^2 x R or P = V^2 / R. Exceeding a resistor's rated wattage causes overheating and drift or failure.",electronics;fundamentals,https://github.com/munsakad/DMHardware-Chat-Bot/blob/main/generate_dataset.py,original:Power Dissipation,original_reference
3,Electronics,Fundamentals,Kirchhoff's Current Law (KCL),KCL states that the sum of currents entering a node equals the sum of currents leaving it — charge cannot accumulate at a node in steady state. It's the basis for nodal analysis.,electronics;fundamentals,https://github.com/munsakad/DMHardware-Chat-Bot/blob/main/generate_dataset.py,original:Kirchhoff's Current Law (KCL),original_reference
4,Electronics,Fundamentals,Kirchhoff's Voltage Law (KVL),KVL states that the sum of voltage rises and drops around any closed loop in a circuit equals zero. It's the basis for mesh/loop analysis.,electronics;fundamentals,https://github.com/munsakad/DMHardware-Chat-Bot/blob/main/generate_dataset.py,original:Kirchhoff's Voltage Law (KVL),original_reference
5,Electronics,Fundamentals,Series vs Parallel Resistors,"Resistors in series add directly: Rtotal = R1 + R2 + ... In parallel, conductances add: 1/Rtotal = 1/R1 + 1/R2 + ... Series connections divide voltage; parallel connections divide current.",electronics;fundamentals,https://github.com/munsakad/DMHardware-Chat-Bot/blob/main/generate_dataset.py,original:Series vs Parallel Resistors,original_reference
6,Electronics,Fundamentals,Thevenin Equivalent,Any linear two-terminal network can be reduced to a single voltage source (Vth) in series with a single resistance (Rth) as seen from those terminals — useful for simplifying load analysis.,electronics;fundamentals,https://github.com/munsakad/DMHardware-Chat-Bot/blob/main/generate_dataset.py,original:Thevenin Equivalent,original_reference
7,Electronics,Fundamentals,Norton Equivalent,The Norton equivalent represents a linear network as a current source (In) in parallel with a resistance (Rn). Vth = In x Rn relates it directly to the Thevenin equivalent.,electronics;fundamentals,https://github.com/munsakad/DMHardware-Chat-Bot/blob/main/generate_dataset.py,original:Norton Equivalent,original_reference
8,Electronics,Passive Components,Resistor Color Code (4-band),"4-band resistors encode value as: band1-band2 = significant digits, band3 = multiplier (power of ten), band4 = tolerance. Example: brown-black-red-gold = 10 x 100 = 1kOhm +/-5%.",electronics;passive_components,https://github.com/munsakad/DMHardware-Chat-Bot/blob/main/generate_dataset.py,original:Resistor Color Code (4-band),original_reference
9,Electronics,Passive Components,Resistor Color Code (5-band),"5-band precision resistors use three significant digit bands, then a multiplier band, then a tolerance band, giving finer resolution than the 4-band scheme (commonly +/-1% or better).",electronics;passive_components,https://github.com/munsakad/DMHardware-Chat-Bot/blob/main/generate_dataset.py,original:Resistor Color Code (5-band),original_reference
10,Electronics,Passive Components,Capacitor Basics,"A capacitor stores energy in an electric field between two conductive plates separated by a dielectric. Capacitance C = Q/V, measured in farads; typical circuit values range from picofarads to thousands of microfarads.",electronics;passive_components,https://github.com/munsakad/DMHardware-Chat-Bot/blob/main/generate_dataset.py,original:Capacitor Basics,original_reference
11,Electronics,Passive Components,Capacitor Types,"Ceramic capacitors are small, cheap, and good for decoupling/high frequency. Electrolytic capacitors offer large capacitance in small volume but are polarized and have limited lifetime. Film and tantalum capacitors offer stability and low leakage for precision analog work.",electronics;passive_components,https://github.com/munsakad/DMHardware-Chat-Bot/blob/main/generate_dataset.py,original:Capacitor Types,original_reference
12,Electronics,Passive Components,Decoupling Capacitors,"Small ceramic capacitors (commonly 0.1uF) placed close to an IC's power pins suppress high-frequency noise and supply transients, keeping the local supply rail stable during fast switching.",electronics;passive_components,https://github.com/munsakad/DMHardware-Chat-Bot/blob/main/generate_dataset.py,original:Decoupling Capacitors,original_reference
13,Electronics,Passive Components,Inductor Basics,"An inductor stores energy in a magnetic field and opposes changes in current. Inductance L relates voltage and current rate of change: V = L x (dI/dt). Common uses include filtering, energy storage in switching regulators, and RF tuning.",electronics;passive_components,https://github.com/munsakad/DMHardware-Chat-Bot/blob/main/generate_dataset.py,original:Inductor Basics,original_reference
14,Electronics,Passive Components,RC Time Constant,"In a resistor-capacitor circuit, the time constant tau = R x C describes how quickly the capacitor charges or discharges — after one tau the voltage has moved about 63% of the way to its final value.",electronics;passive_components,https://github.com/munsakad/DMHardware-Chat-Bot/blob/main/generate_dataset.py,original:RC Time Constant,original_reference
15,Electronics,Passive Components,RL Time Constant,"In a resistor-inductor circuit, tau = L / R describes the rate of current rise or decay — after one tau the current has moved about 63% toward its steady-state value.",electronics;passive_components,https://github.com/munsakad/DMHardware-Chat-Bot/blob/main/generate_dataset.py,original:RL Time Constant,original_reference
16,Electronics,Semiconductors,Diode Basics,A diode conducts current primarily in one direction. Silicon diodes have roughly a 0.6-0.7V forward voltage drop; germanium diodes are lower (~0.3V); Schottky diodes are lower still (~0.2-0.3V) with faster switching.,electronics;semiconductors,https://github.com/munsakad/DMHardware-Chat-Bot/blob/main/generate_dataset.py,original:Diode Basics,original_reference
17,Electronics,Semiconductors,Zener Diode,"A Zener diode is designed to operate in reverse breakdown at a well-defined voltage, making it useful for simple voltage regulation or reference circuits when paired with a series current-limiting resistor.",electronics;semiconductors,https://github.com/munsakad/DMHardware-Chat-Bot/blob/main/generate_dataset.py,original:Zener Diode,original_reference
18,Electronics,Semiconductors,LED Basics,"LEDs emit light when forward biased, with forward voltage depending on color/material (roughly 1.8V for red up to 3.2V+ for blue/white). They require a current-limiting resistor or driver to avoid exceeding rated current.",electronics;semiconductors,https://github.com/munsakad/DMHardware-Chat-Bot/blob/main/generate_dataset.py,original:LED Basics,original_reference
19,Electronics,Semiconductors,BJT Overview,"Bipolar Junction Transistors (NPN/PNP) are current-controlled devices where a small base current controls a larger collector-emitter current. Common configurations are common-emitter (gain), common-collector (buffer), and common-base (high-frequency).",electronics;semiconductors,https://github.com/munsakad/DMHardware-Chat-Bot/blob/main/generate_dataset.py,original:BJT Overview,original_reference
20,Electronics,Semiconductors,MOSFET Overview,MOSFETs are voltage-controlled switches/amplifiers. N-channel MOSFETs turn on when gate-source voltage exceeds a threshold; they dominate modern switching power supplies and digital logic due to low gate drive power.,electronics;semiconductors,https://github.com/munsakad/DMHardware-Chat-Bot/blob/main/generate_dataset.py,original:MOSFET Overview,original_reference
21,Electronics,Semiconductors,Transistor as a Switch,"When used as a digital switch, a transistor is driven fully into saturation (BJT) or fully enhanced (MOSFET) for the 'on' state, and fully off (cutoff) for the 'off' state, minimizing power dissipation in both states.",electronics;semiconductors,https://github.com/munsakad/DMHardware-Chat-Bot/blob/main/generate_dataset.py,original:Transistor as a Switch,original_reference
22,Electronics,Semiconductors,Op-Amp Basics,"An ideal operational amplifier has infinite gain, infinite input impedance, and zero output impedance, driving its two inputs to the same voltage via negative feedback (the 'virtual short' concept).",electronics;semiconductors,https://github.com/munsakad/DMHardware-Chat-Bot/blob/main/generate_dataset.py,original:Op-Amp Basics,original_reference
23,Electronics,Semiconductors,Op-Amp Inverting Amplifier,"In an inverting amplifier, gain = -Rf/Rin, where Rin connects the source to the inverting input and Rf feeds back from output to inverting input. The non-inverting input is typically grounded or biased.",electronics;semiconductors,https://github.com/munsakad/DMHardware-Chat-Bot/blob/main/generate_dataset.py,original:Op-Amp Inverting Amplifier,original_reference
24,Electronics,Semiconductors,Op-Amp Non-Inverting Amplifier,"In a non-inverting amplifier, gain = 1 + Rf/Rin. The signal drives the non-inverting input directly, giving high input impedance and a positive (non-inverted) gain.",electronics;semiconductors,https://github.com/munsakad/DMHardware-Chat-Bot/blob/main/generate_dataset.py,original:Op-Amp Non-Inverting Amplifier,original_reference
25,Electronics,Circuits,Voltage Divider,A voltage divider made of two series resistors produces Vout = Vin x (R2 / (R1 + R2)). Loading effects from whatever connects to the output can significantly change this ratio if the load impedance is comparable to R2.,electronics;circuits,https://github.com/munsakad/DMHardware-Chat-Bot/blob/main/generate_dataset.py,original:Voltage Divider,original_reference
26,Electronics,Circuits,Low-Pass Filter (RC),A simple RC low-pass filter passes frequencies below its cutoff f_c = 1/(2*pi*R*C) and attenuates higher frequencies at roughly 20 dB/decade beyond that point.,electronics;circuits,https://github.com/munsakad/DMHardware-Chat-Bot/blob/main/generate_dataset.py,original:Low-Pass Filter (RC),original_reference
27,Electronics,Circuits,High-Pass Filter (RC),"A simple RC high-pass filter blocks frequencies below cutoff f_c = 1/(2*pi*R*C) and passes higher frequencies, useful for removing DC offset or low-frequency noise from a signal.",electronics;circuits,https://github.com/munsakad/DMHardware-Chat-Bot/blob/main/generate_dataset.py,original:High-Pass Filter (RC),original_reference
28,Electronics,Circuits,Linear Voltage Regulator,"Linear regulators (e.g., 78xx series) drop excess voltage as heat to produce a stable output, offering low noise and simplicity but poor efficiency when the input-output differential is large.",electronics;circuits,https://github.com/munsakad/DMHardware-Chat-Bot/blob/main/generate_dataset.py,original:Linear Voltage Regulator,original_reference
29,Electronics,Circuits,Switching Voltage Regulator,"Switching (buck/boost) regulators use an inductor/capacitor and a switching element to convert voltage with high efficiency (often >85%), at the cost of more complex design and higher output ripple/noise than linear regulators.",electronics;circuits,https://github.com/munsakad/DMHardware-Chat-Bot/blob/main/generate_dataset.py,original:Switching Voltage Regulator,original_reference
30,Electronics,Circuits,Buck Converter,"A buck converter steps voltage down by rapidly switching an input source across an inductor and filtering with a capacitor, with output roughly Vout = Vin x duty_cycle for continuous conduction mode.",electronics;circuits,https://github.com/munsakad/DMHardware-Chat-Bot/blob/main/generate_dataset.py,original:Buck Converter,original_reference
31,Electronics,Circuits,Boost Converter,"A boost converter steps voltage up by storing energy in an inductor during the switch-on phase and releasing it in series with the input during the switch-off phase, giving Vout > Vin.",electronics;circuits,https://github.com/munsakad/DMHardware-Chat-Bot/blob/main/generate_dataset.py,original:Boost Converter,original_reference
32,Electronics,Measurement,Multimeter Basics,"A digital multimeter measures voltage (parallel across a component), current (in series with the circuit), and resistance (with the circuit unpowered), and is the primary hand tool for verifying circuit behavior.",electronics;measurement,https://github.com/munsakad/DMHardware-Chat-Bot/blob/main/generate_dataset.py,original:Multimeter Basics,original_reference
33,Electronics,Measurement,Oscilloscope Basics,"An oscilloscope displays voltage over time, letting you observe waveform shape, frequency, rise/fall time, noise, and timing relationships between signals — essential for debugging anything with fast-changing signals.",electronics;measurement,https://github.com/munsakad/DMHardware-Chat-Bot/blob/main/generate_dataset.py,original:Oscilloscope Basics,original_reference
34,Electronics,Sensors,Analog vs Digital Sensors,"Analog sensors (e.g., a thermistor or potentiometer) output a continuous voltage/current proportional to a measured quantity and typically need an ADC; digital sensors output discrete values or communicate over a protocol like I2C/SPI/UART.",electronics;sensors,https://github.com/munsakad/DMHardware-Chat-Bot/blob/main/generate_dataset.py,original:Analog vs Digital Sensors,original_reference
35,Electronics,Sensors,I2C Protocol,"I2C is a two-wire (SDA/SCL) synchronous serial bus supporting multiple devices on the same lines via unique addresses, commonly used to connect low-speed sensors and peripherals to a microcontroller.",electronics;sensors,https://github.com/munsakad/DMHardware-Chat-Bot/blob/main/generate_dataset.py,original:I2C Protocol,original_reference
36,Electronics,Sensors,SPI Protocol,"SPI is a synchronous serial protocol using separate clock, data-in, data-out, and chip-select lines, offering higher throughput than I2C at the cost of more wires and typically point-to-point wiring per device.",electronics;sensors,https://github.com/munsakad/DMHardware-Chat-Bot/blob/main/generate_dataset.py,original:SPI Protocol,original_reference
37,Electronics,Sensors,UART Protocol,"UART is an asynchronous serial protocol using separate TX/RX lines with an agreed baud rate rather than a shared clock, commonly used for simple point-to-point communication like a microcontroller talking to a PC or GPS module.",electronics;sensors,https://github.com/munsakad/DMHardware-Chat-Bot/blob/main/generate_dataset.py,original:UART Protocol,original_reference
38,Robotics,Actuators,DC Motor Basics,"A brushed DC motor converts electrical energy to rotational motion via a magnetic field and commutator; speed is roughly proportional to applied voltage and torque relates to current, making them simple to control with a single H-bridge.",robotics;actuators,https://github.com/munsakad/DMHardware-Chat-Bot/blob/main/generate_dataset.py,original:DC Motor Basics,original_reference
39,Robotics,Actuators,Brushless DC Motor (BLDC),"BLDC motors move the commutation electronics outside the motor, using an electronic speed controller to sequence current through stator coils. They offer higher efficiency and longevity than brushed motors but need more complex control.",robotics;actuators,https://github.com/munsakad/DMHardware-Chat-Bot/blob/main/generate_dataset.py,original:Brushless DC Motor (BLDC),original_reference
40,Robotics,Actuators,Stepper Motor Basics,"Stepper motors move in discrete angular steps by energizing coil pairs in sequence, allowing open-loop position control without an encoder, at the cost of lower top speed and possible step loss under excessive load.",robotics;actuators,https://github.com/munsakad/DMHardware-Chat-Bot/blob/main/generate_dataset.py,original:Stepper Motor Basics,original_reference
41,Robotics,Actuators,Servo Motor Basics,"An RC-style servo combines a small DC motor, gearbox, and feedback potentiometer with onboard control electronics that accept a PWM position command, making it convenient for simple positional control in robotics.",robotics;actuators,https://github.com/munsakad/DMHardware-Chat-Bot/blob/main/generate_dataset.py,original:Servo Motor Basics,original_reference
42,Robotics,Actuators,H-Bridge Motor Driver,"An H-bridge uses four switching elements arranged so current can flow through a motor in either direction, enabling forward/reverse control and, combined with PWM, variable speed control of a DC motor.",robotics;actuators,https://github.com/munsakad/DMHardware-Chat-Bot/blob/main/generate_dataset.py,original:H-Bridge Motor Driver,original_reference
43,Robotics,Actuators,PWM for Motor Control,"Pulse-width modulation varies the average power delivered to a motor by rapidly switching full voltage on and off at a fixed frequency, with duty cycle controlling effective speed or position command.",robotics;actuators,https://github.com/munsakad/DMHardware-Chat-Bot/blob/main/generate_dataset.py,original:PWM for Motor Control,original_reference
44,Robotics,Kinematics,Forward Kinematics,"Forward kinematics computes the position and orientation of a robot's end effector given known joint angles/lengths, typically via a chain of transformation matrices (e.g., Denavit-Hartenberg parameters).",robotics;kinematics,https://github.com/munsakad/DMHardware-Chat-Bot/blob/main/generate_dataset.py,original:Forward Kinematics,original_reference
45,Robotics,Kinematics,Inverse Kinematics,"Inverse kinematics computes the joint angles needed to reach a desired end-effector position and orientation — often having multiple valid solutions (elbow-up/elbow-down) and sometimes no closed-form solution, requiring numerical methods.",robotics;kinematics,https://github.com/munsakad/DMHardware-Chat-Bot/blob/main/generate_dataset.py,original:Inverse Kinematics,original_reference
46,Robotics,Kinematics,Degrees of Freedom (DOF),Degrees of freedom describe the number of independent ways a robot or joint can move. A typical robotic arm for full 3D position and orientation control needs at least 6 DOF.,robotics;kinematics,https://github.com/munsakad/DMHardware-Chat-Bot/blob/main/generate_dataset.py,original:Degrees of Freedom (DOF),original_reference
47,Robotics,Kinematics,Denavit-Hartenberg Parameters,"DH parameters (link length, link twist, link offset, joint angle) provide a standardized way to describe the geometry of a serial robotic arm's links and joints for kinematic modeling.",robotics;kinematics,https://github.com/munsakad/DMHardware-Chat-Bot/blob/main/generate_dataset.py,original:Denavit-Hartenberg Parameters,original_reference
48,Robotics,Control,PID Controller,"A PID controller computes a control output from Proportional (current error), Integral (accumulated past error), and Derivative (rate of error change) terms, widely used for motor speed/position and other feedback control loops.",robotics;control,https://github.com/munsakad/DMHardware-Chat-Bot/blob/main/generate_dataset.py,original:PID Controller,original_reference
49,Robotics,Control,Tuning a PID Loop,"Common tuning approaches include starting with proportional gain only, then adding integral gain to remove steady-state error, then adding derivative gain to reduce overshoot/oscillation. Ziegler-Nichols is a classic systematic tuning method.",robotics;control,https://github.com/munsakad/DMHardware-Chat-Bot/blob/main/generate_dataset.py,original:Tuning a PID Loop,original_reference
50,Robotics,Control,Open-Loop vs Closed-Loop Control,"Open-loop control applies a command without checking the actual result (e.g., basic stepper motion), while closed-loop control uses sensor feedback to correct errors in real time (e.g., encoder-based position control).",robotics;control,https://github.com/munsakad/DMHardware-Chat-Bot/blob/main/generate_dataset.py,original:Open-Loop vs Closed-Loop Control,original_reference
51,Robotics,Control,Feedback and Encoders,"Rotary encoders (optical or magnetic) report shaft position or speed, enabling closed-loop control. Incremental encoders report relative motion; absolute encoders report a unique position value at all times.",robotics;control,https://github.com/munsakad/DMHardware-Chat-Bot/blob/main/generate_dataset.py,original:Feedback and Encoders,original_reference
52,Robotics,Sensing,IMU (Inertial Measurement Unit),"An IMU typically combines an accelerometer (linear acceleration) and gyroscope (angular velocity), and sometimes a magnetometer (heading), used together for orientation estimation and motion tracking in mobile robots and drones.",robotics;sensing,https://github.com/munsakad/DMHardware-Chat-Bot/blob/main/generate_dataset.py,original:IMU (Inertial Measurement Unit),original_reference
53,Robotics,Sensing,Ultrasonic Distance Sensor,"Ultrasonic sensors measure distance by timing an emitted sound pulse's echo return, offering low-cost obstacle detection but limited accuracy on soft/angled surfaces compared to optical methods.",robotics;sensing,https://github.com/munsakad/DMHardware-Chat-Bot/blob/main/generate_dataset.py,original:Ultrasonic Distance Sensor,original_reference
54,Robotics,Sensing,LiDAR Basics,"LiDAR measures distance by timing reflected laser pulses (or measuring phase shift), producing precise point clouds used for mapping, obstacle avoidance, and localization in mobile robotics and autonomous vehicles.",robotics;sensing,https://github.com/munsakad/DMHardware-Chat-Bot/blob/main/generate_dataset.py,original:LiDAR Basics,original_reference
55,Robotics,Sensing,Wheel Odometry,"Wheel odometry estimates robot position by integrating wheel rotation counts over time, which is simple but accumulates drift error from wheel slip and needs periodic correction from other sensors (IMU, vision, LiDAR).",robotics;sensing,https://github.com/munsakad/DMHardware-Chat-Bot/blob/main/generate_dataset.py,original:Wheel Odometry,original_reference
56,Robotics,Navigation,Path Planning Overview,"Path planning algorithms compute a route from a start to a goal while avoiding obstacles. Common approaches include grid-search methods (A*, Dijkstra) and sampling-based methods (RRT, PRM) for higher-dimensional configuration spaces.",robotics;navigation,https://github.com/munsakad/DMHardware-Chat-Bot/blob/main/generate_dataset.py,original:Path Planning Overview,original_reference
57,Robotics,Navigation,A* Search Algorithm,"A* finds a shortest path on a graph/grid by combining the actual cost so far with a heuristic estimate of remaining cost, expanding the most promising nodes first — widely used in grid-based robot path planning.",robotics;navigation,https://github.com/munsakad/DMHardware-Chat-Bot/blob/main/generate_dataset.py,original:A* Search Algorithm,original_reference
58,Robotics,Navigation,SLAM (Simultaneous Localization and Mapping),"SLAM lets a robot build a map of an unknown environment while simultaneously tracking its own position within that map, typically fusing LiDAR/camera data with odometry and probabilistic filtering (e.g., particle filters, Kalman filters).",robotics;navigation,https://github.com/munsakad/DMHardware-Chat-Bot/blob/main/generate_dataset.py,original:SLAM (Simultaneous Localization and Mapping),original_reference
59,Robotics,Navigation,Kalman Filter Basics,"A Kalman filter fuses noisy sensor measurements with a motion model to produce an optimal estimate of a system's state (e.g., position, velocity), widely used in robot localization and sensor fusion.",robotics;navigation,https://github.com/munsakad/DMHardware-Chat-Bot/blob/main/generate_dataset.py,original:Kalman Filter Basics,original_reference
60,Robotics,Mechanisms,End Effectors and Grippers,"End effectors are the tools mounted at a robot arm's tip — parallel-jaw grippers, vacuum suction cups, and specialized tool changers are common choices depending on the object being manipulated.",robotics;mechanisms,https://github.com/munsakad/DMHardware-Chat-Bot/blob/main/generate_dataset.py,original:End Effectors and Grippers,original_reference
61,Robotics,Mechanisms,Differential Drive Robot,"A differential drive robot steers by varying the relative speed of two independently driven wheels (often with passive casters for stability), making it mechanically simple compared to Ackermann or omnidirectional drive systems.",robotics;mechanisms,https://github.com/munsakad/DMHardware-Chat-Bot/blob/main/generate_dataset.py,original:Differential Drive Robot,original_reference
62,Robotics,Software,ROS (Robot Operating System) Overview,"ROS provides a middleware framework of nodes, topics, and services for building modular robotics software, letting sensor drivers, control algorithms, and planning modules communicate without being tightly coupled.",robotics;software,https://github.com/munsakad/DMHardware-Chat-Bot/blob/main/generate_dataset.py,original:ROS (Robot Operating System) Overview,original_reference
63,Robotics,Software,Microcontroller vs Single-Board Computer,"Microcontrollers (e.g., an ATmega or STM32) are efficient for real-time, low-level control (motors, sensors), while single-board computers (e.g., Raspberry Pi) suit higher-level tasks like vision processing and planning that benefit from a full OS.",robotics;software,https://github.com/munsakad/DMHardware-Chat-Bot/blob/main/generate_dataset.py,original:Microcontroller vs Single-Board Computer,original_reference
64,PCB Design,Basics,PCB Layer Stack-up,A PCB stack-up defines how copper and dielectric layers are arranged through the board's thickness. A simple 2-layer board has top/bottom copper; 4+ layer boards add internal power/ground planes for better signal integrity and EMI performance.,pcb design;basics,https://github.com/munsakad/DMHardware-Chat-Bot/blob/main/generate_dataset.py,original:PCB Layer Stack-up,original_reference
65,PCB Design,Basics,Traces and Copper Weight,A PCB trace is a copper conductor connecting components. Copper weight (commonly 1oz or 2oz per square foot) and trace width together determine current-carrying capacity and resistance/voltage drop along the trace.,pcb design;basics,https://github.com/munsakad/DMHardware-Chat-Bot/blob/main/generate_dataset.py,original:Traces and Copper Weight,original_reference
66,PCB Design,Basics,Vias,"A via is a plated hole connecting copper on different layers. Through-hole vias span the full board; blind vias connect an outer layer to an inner layer; buried vias connect only internal layers, used in denser boards.",pcb design;basics,https://github.com/munsakad/DMHardware-Chat-Bot/blob/main/generate_dataset.py,original:Vias,original_reference
67,PCB Design,Basics,Pads and Footprints,"A footprint defines the copper pad pattern, silkscreen outline, and mechanical keep-out for a specific component package, ensuring the physical part will solder correctly and fit as intended.",pcb design;basics,https://github.com/munsakad/DMHardware-Chat-Bot/blob/main/generate_dataset.py,original:Pads and Footprints,original_reference
68,PCB Design,Basics,Silkscreen Layer,"The silkscreen layer prints reference designators, polarity marks, and outlines onto the board surface for assembly and debugging, and carries no electrical function.",pcb design;basics,https://github.com/munsakad/DMHardware-Chat-Bot/blob/main/generate_dataset.py,original:Silkscreen Layer,original_reference
69,PCB Design,Basics,Solder Mask,"Solder mask is the protective coating (commonly green, though many colors exist) that covers copper areas not meant to be soldered, preventing bridges and oxidation while leaving pads exposed.",pcb design;basics,https://github.com/munsakad/DMHardware-Chat-Bot/blob/main/generate_dataset.py,original:Solder Mask,original_reference
70,PCB Design,Component Packages,Through-Hole vs Surface Mount,"Through-hole components mount via leads passing through drilled holes, offering mechanical strength for connectors and large parts. Surface-mount (SMT) components solder directly onto pads, enabling smaller size and automated assembly.",pcb design;component_packages,https://github.com/munsakad/DMHardware-Chat-Bot/blob/main/generate_dataset.py,original:Through-Hole vs Surface Mount,original_reference
71,PCB Design,Component Packages,SOIC Package,"Small Outline Integrated Circuit (SOIC) is a common surface-mount IC package with gull-wing leads on two sides, easy to hand-solder and widely used for op-amps, logic ICs, and small microcontrollers.",pcb design;component_packages,https://github.com/munsakad/DMHardware-Chat-Bot/blob/main/generate_dataset.py,original:SOIC Package,original_reference
72,PCB Design,Component Packages,QFN Package,"Quad Flat No-lead (QFN) packages have pads flush with the package bottom (plus often a thermal pad), giving a compact, low-profile footprint but requiring reflow soldering and good thermal via design under the package.",pcb design;component_packages,https://github.com/munsakad/DMHardware-Chat-Bot/blob/main/generate_dataset.py,original:QFN Package,original_reference
73,PCB Design,Component Packages,BGA Package,"Ball Grid Array (BGA) packages place solder balls in a grid under the chip, enabling very high pin counts in a small footprint, but requiring precise reflow profiles and X-ray inspection since joints are hidden underneath.",pcb design;component_packages,https://github.com/munsakad/DMHardware-Chat-Bot/blob/main/generate_dataset.py,original:BGA Package,original_reference
