141,Electronics,Basics of Electricity & Electronics,Series & Parallel Circuits — Parallel circuits [b707e426],"In a parallel circuit, components share the same two nodes, so the same voltage appears across every branch, while the total current splits between the branches.",electricity-electronics-basics;series-parallel-circuits;lesson_passage,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=electricity-electronics-basics&lesson=series-parallel-circuits,lesson:electricity-electronics-basics/series-parallel-circuits,lesson_passage
142,Electronics,Basics of Electricity & Electronics,Series & Parallel Circuits — Parallel circuits [410539c0],Series vs. parallel at a glance | Property | Series | Parallel | Current | Same through all components | Splits between branches | Voltage | Splits across components | Same across all branches | Equivalent resistance | Always larger than any single resistor | Always smaller than the smallest resistor | If one component fails open | Whole circuit stops | Other branches keep working,electricity-electronics-basics;series-parallel-circuits;lesson_passage,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=electricity-electronics-basics&lesson=series-parallel-circuits,lesson:electricity-electronics-basics/series-parallel-circuits,lesson_passage
143,Electronics,Basics of Electricity & Electronics,Kirchhoff&prime;s Laws — Kirchhoff′s Current Law (KCL) [4279e98b],KCL states that the total current flowing into any node (junction) must equal the total current flowing out of it -- charge cannot pile up or vanish at a point in a circuit.,electricity-electronics-basics;kirchhoffs-laws;lesson_passage,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=electricity-electronics-basics&lesson=kirchhoffs-laws,lesson:electricity-electronics-basics/kirchhoffs-laws,lesson_passage
144,Electronics,Basics of Electricity & Electronics,Kirchhoff&prime;s Laws — Kirchhoff′s Current Law (KCL) [8b833ae3],"Equivalently, if current flowing into a node is treated as positive and current flowing out as negative, the sum of all currents at a node is zero.",electricity-electronics-basics;kirchhoffs-laws;lesson_passage,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=electricity-electronics-basics&lesson=kirchhoffs-laws,lesson:electricity-electronics-basics/kirchhoffs-laws,lesson_passage
145,Electronics,Basics of Electricity & Electronics,Kirchhoff&prime;s Laws — Kirchhoff′s Voltage Law (KVL) [866b63c1],KVL states that the sum of all voltage rises and drops around any closed loop in a circuit must equal zero -- energy gained from sources around a loop must exactly equal energy given up across the loop′s resistances and other elements.,electricity-electronics-basics;kirchhoffs-laws;lesson_passage,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=electricity-electronics-basics&lesson=kirchhoffs-laws,lesson:electricity-electronics-basics/kirchhoffs-laws,lesson_passage
146,Electronics,Basics of Electricity & Electronics,Kirchhoff&prime;s Laws — Worked example: a two-resistor loop [d1332412],"KVL: 9V = I · R1 + I · R2 = I(100 + 200)
I = 9 / 300 = 0.03 A = 30 mA

Voltage across R1: V1 = I × R1 = 0.03 × 100 = 3 V
Voltage across R2: V2 = I × R2 = 0.03 × 200 = 6 V
Check: V1 + V2 = 3 + 6 = 9 V = battery voltage ✓",electricity-electronics-basics;kirchhoffs-laws;code,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=electricity-electronics-basics&lesson=kirchhoffs-laws,lesson:electricity-electronics-basics/kirchhoffs-laws,code
147,Electronics,Basics of Electricity & Electronics,Kirchhoff&prime;s Laws — Why these laws matter [298e09c2],"KCL and KVL are the two fundamental rules that every other circuit analysis technique -- series/parallel reduction, voltage/current dividers, Thevenin/Norton equivalents, mesh and nodal analysis -- is ultimately built from. Any circuit, no matter how complex, must satisfy both laws at every node and around every loop.",electricity-electronics-basics;kirchhoffs-laws;lesson_passage,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=electricity-electronics-basics&lesson=kirchhoffs-laws,lesson:electricity-electronics-basics/kirchhoffs-laws,lesson_passage
148,Electronics,Basics of Electricity & Electronics,Circuit Analysis Techniques — Voltage divider [e52c7b6f],Two resistors in series split an input voltage in proportion to their resistance -- the larger resistor gets the larger share of the voltage.,electricity-electronics-basics;circuit-analysis-techniques;lesson_passage,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=electricity-electronics-basics&lesson=circuit-analysis-techniques,lesson:electricity-electronics-basics/circuit-analysis-techniques,lesson_passage
149,Electronics,Basics of Electricity & Electronics,Circuit Analysis Techniques — Voltage divider [e675e100],"Voltage dividers are simple, but ""loading"" (connecting anything else to Vout that draws current) changes the ratio -- they work best when whatever reads Vout draws very little current compared to what flows through R1 and R2.",electricity-electronics-basics;circuit-analysis-techniques;lesson_passage,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=electricity-electronics-basics&lesson=circuit-analysis-techniques,lesson:electricity-electronics-basics/circuit-analysis-techniques,lesson_passage
150,Electronics,Basics of Electricity & Electronics,Circuit Analysis Techniques — Current divider [4ee10d3d],Two resistors in parallel split an input current inversely to their resistance -- the smaller resistor carries the larger share of the current.,electricity-electronics-basics;circuit-analysis-techniques;lesson_passage,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=electricity-electronics-basics&lesson=circuit-analysis-techniques,lesson:electricity-electronics-basics/circuit-analysis-techniques,lesson_passage
151,Electronics,Basics of Electricity & Electronics,Circuit Analysis Techniques — Thevenin′s theorem [65b56ff9],"Any network of resistors and sources, viewed from just two terminals, behaves exactly like one ideal voltage source (V_TH) in series with one resistor (R_TH):",electricity-electronics-basics;circuit-analysis-techniques;lesson_passage,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=electricity-electronics-basics&lesson=circuit-analysis-techniques,lesson:electricity-electronics-basics/circuit-analysis-techniques,lesson_passage
152,Electronics,Basics of Electricity & Electronics,Circuit Analysis Techniques — Thevenin′s theorem [b71228c4],"V_TH is the open-circuit voltage measured across the two terminals, with nothing else connected.",electricity-electronics-basics;circuit-analysis-techniques;lesson_passage,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=electricity-electronics-basics&lesson=circuit-analysis-techniques,lesson:electricity-electronics-basics/circuit-analysis-techniques,lesson_passage
153,Electronics,Basics of Electricity & Electronics,Circuit Analysis Techniques — Thevenin′s theorem [5e6a6fe0],"R_TH is the resistance measured across the terminals with all independent sources turned off (voltage sources replaced by short circuits, current sources replaced by open circuits).",electricity-electronics-basics;circuit-analysis-techniques;lesson_passage,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=electricity-electronics-basics&lesson=circuit-analysis-techniques,lesson:electricity-electronics-basics/circuit-analysis-techniques,lesson_passage
154,Electronics,Basics of Electricity & Electronics,Circuit Analysis Techniques — Thevenin′s theorem [2e1c3972],"Reducing a complicated network to its Thevenin equivalent makes it easy to see how it will respond to whatever load is eventually connected, without re-analyzing the whole original circuit every time the load changes.",electricity-electronics-basics;circuit-analysis-techniques;lesson_passage,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=electricity-electronics-basics&lesson=circuit-analysis-techniques,lesson:electricity-electronics-basics/circuit-analysis-techniques,lesson_passage
155,Electronics,Basics of Electricity & Electronics,Circuit Analysis Techniques — Norton′s theorem [06063bc4],Norton′s theorem is the dual of Thevenin′s: the same two-terminal network can equally be modeled as one ideal current source (I_N) in parallel with one resistor (R_N = R_TH).,electricity-electronics-basics;circuit-analysis-techniques;lesson_passage,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=electricity-electronics-basics&lesson=circuit-analysis-techniques,lesson:electricity-electronics-basics/circuit-analysis-techniques,lesson_passage
156,Electronics,Basics of Electricity & Electronics,Circuit Analysis Techniques — Norton′s theorem [2f818753],Thevenin and Norton equivalents of the same network are always interchangeable using this relationship.,electricity-electronics-basics;circuit-analysis-techniques;lesson_passage,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=electricity-electronics-basics&lesson=circuit-analysis-techniques,lesson:electricity-electronics-basics/circuit-analysis-techniques,lesson_passage
157,Electronics,Basics of Electricity & Electronics,Circuit Analysis Techniques — Superposition [a39abbf4],"In any linear circuit with multiple independent sources, the voltage or current anywhere in the circuit is the sum of the contributions from each source acting alone, with every other independent source turned off (voltage sources shorted, current sources opened) one at a time.",electricity-electronics-basics;circuit-analysis-techniques;lesson_passage,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=electricity-electronics-basics&lesson=circuit-analysis-techniques,lesson:electricity-electronics-basics/circuit-analysis-techniques,lesson_passage
158,Electronics,Basics of Electricity & Electronics,Capacitors & Capacitance — What a capacitor does [a2378331],"A capacitor stores electrical energy in an electric field between two conductive plates separated by an insulator (a dielectric). Capacitance C, measured in farads (F), describes how much charge a capacitor stores per volt across it:",electricity-electronics-basics;capacitors-and-capacitance;lesson_passage,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=electricity-electronics-basics&lesson=capacitors-and-capacitance,lesson:electricity-electronics-basics/capacitors-and-capacitance,lesson_passage
159,Electronics,Basics of Electricity & Electronics,Capacitors & Capacitance — What a capacitor does [ca3da523],"A farad is a very large unit for most electronics -- practical capacitors are usually specified in microfarads (µF), nanofarads (nF), or picofarads (pF).",electricity-electronics-basics;capacitors-and-capacitance;lesson_passage,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=electricity-electronics-basics&lesson=capacitors-and-capacitance,lesson:electricity-electronics-basics/capacitors-and-capacitance,lesson_passage
