161,Electronics,Basics of Electricity & Electronics,Capacitors & Capacitance — Capacitors in series and parallel [e827708e],"Capacitors combine the opposite way resistors do: parallel capacitors add directly (more plate area, more stored charge for the same voltage), while series capacitors combine like parallel resistors.",electricity-electronics-basics;capacitors-and-capacitance;lesson_passage,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=electricity-electronics-basics&lesson=capacitors-and-capacitance,lesson:electricity-electronics-basics/capacitors-and-capacitance,lesson_passage
162,Electronics,Basics of Electricity & Electronics,Capacitors & Capacitance — Charging and discharging: the RC time constant [d4ef9273],"Charging a capacitor through a resistor does not happen instantly -- the voltage across the capacitor rises (or falls) exponentially, characterized by the time constant τ (tau):",electricity-electronics-basics;capacitors-and-capacitance;lesson_passage,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=electricity-electronics-basics&lesson=capacitors-and-capacitance,lesson:electricity-electronics-basics/capacitors-and-capacitance,lesson_passage
163,Electronics,Basics of Electricity & Electronics,Capacitors & Capacitance — Charging and discharging: the RC time constant [75a4c646],"τ is the time it takes the capacitor voltage to reach about 63% of its final value when charging (or fall to about 37% of its starting value when discharging). After roughly 5τ, a capacitor is considered fully charged or discharged for most practical purposes.",electricity-electronics-basics;capacitors-and-capacitance;lesson_passage,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=electricity-electronics-basics&lesson=capacitors-and-capacitance,lesson:electricity-electronics-basics/capacitors-and-capacitance,lesson_passage
164,Electronics,Basics of Electricity & Electronics,Capacitors & Capacitance — Charging and discharging: the RC time constant [ba177264],"Capacitor voltage during charging (through a resistor from a fixed supply) | Time elapsed | % of final voltage reached | 1τ | ≈ 63% | 2τ | ≈ 86% | 3τ | ≈ 95% | 5τ | ≈ 99% (considered ""fully charged"")",electricity-electronics-basics;capacitors-and-capacitance;lesson_passage,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=electricity-electronics-basics&lesson=capacitors-and-capacitance,lesson:electricity-electronics-basics/capacitors-and-capacitance,lesson_passage
165,Electronics,Basics of Electricity & Electronics,Capacitors & Capacitance — Common uses [c0f2c552],Common capacitor applications | Application | Why a capacitor helps | Decoupling / bulk supply filtering | Supplies quick bursts of current and smooths voltage dips near a chip | Timing circuits | The RC time constant sets a predictable delay | Blocking DC / passing AC | A charged capacitor blocks steady DC but still lets a changing AC signal through,electricity-electronics-basics;capacitors-and-capacitance;lesson_passage,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=electricity-electronics-basics&lesson=capacitors-and-capacitance,lesson:electricity-electronics-basics/capacitors-and-capacitance,lesson_passage
166,Electronics,Basics of Electricity & Electronics,Inductors & Inductance — What an inductor does [207a57dc],"An inductor stores energy in a magnetic field created by current flowing through a coil of wire. Inductance L, measured in henries (H), describes how strongly a changing current induces an opposing voltage:",electricity-electronics-basics;inductors-and-inductance;lesson_passage,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=electricity-electronics-basics&lesson=inductors-and-inductance,lesson:electricity-electronics-basics/inductors-and-inductance,lesson_passage
167,Electronics,Basics of Electricity & Electronics,Inductors & Inductance — What an inductor does [2874691e],"In words: the voltage across an inductor is proportional to how fast its current is changing, not to the current itself. An inductor carrying a perfectly steady current (dI/dt = 0) has zero voltage across it, no matter how large that current is.",electricity-electronics-basics;inductors-and-inductance;lesson_passage,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=electricity-electronics-basics&lesson=inductors-and-inductance,lesson:electricity-electronics-basics/inductors-and-inductance,lesson_passage
