170,Electronics,Basics of Electricity & Electronics,Inductors & Inductance — RL circuits and the time constant [318cf765],"Current through an inductor rises (or falls) exponentially toward its final value with this same time constant, following the same 63%/86%/95%/99% pattern as capacitor voltage.",electricity-electronics-basics;inductors-and-inductance;lesson_passage,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=electricity-electronics-basics&lesson=inductors-and-inductance,lesson:electricity-electronics-basics/inductors-and-inductance,lesson_passage
171,Electronics,Basics of Electricity & Electronics,Inductors & Inductance — An inductor resists sudden current changes [a589e8bc],"Because V = L(dI/dt), forcing an inductor′s current to change instantly would require infinite voltage -- in practice, an inductor resists sudden current changes, the opposite behavior from a capacitor (which resists sudden voltage changes). This is why abruptly switching off current through an inductor (e.g. a relay coil) can produce a large voltage spike, usually suppressed with a flyback diode.",electricity-electronics-basics;inductors-and-inductance;lesson_passage,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=electricity-electronics-basics&lesson=inductors-and-inductance,lesson:electricity-electronics-basics/inductors-and-inductance,lesson_passage
