181,Electronics,Basics of Electricity & Electronics,AC Circuit Analysis: Reactance & Impedance — Impedance [c66d01a3],"For a resistor and capacitor in series: Z = R - jX_C. For a resistor and inductor in series: Z = R + jX_L. Ohm′s Law generalizes directly: V = I × Z, using complex (phasor) values for V, I, and Z.",electricity-electronics-basics;ac-circuit-analysis-impedance;lesson_passage,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=electricity-electronics-basics&lesson=ac-circuit-analysis-impedance,lesson:electricity-electronics-basics/ac-circuit-analysis-impedance,lesson_passage
182,Electronics,Basics of Electricity & Electronics,AC Circuit Analysis: Reactance & Impedance — Series RLC resonance [049882ab],"In a series RLC circuit, X_L and X_C work against each other because of their opposite signs. At one specific frequency, called the resonant frequency, they cancel exactly (X_L = X_C), leaving only R to oppose current -- current is at its maximum at resonance.",electricity-electronics-basics;ac-circuit-analysis-impedance;lesson_passage,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=electricity-electronics-basics&lesson=ac-circuit-analysis-impedance,lesson:electricity-electronics-basics/ac-circuit-analysis-impedance,lesson_passage
183,Electronics,Basics of Electricity & Electronics,AC Circuit Analysis: Reactance & Impedance — Series RLC resonance [2f6cba89],"Resonance is the underlying principle behind radio tuning circuits, which use an RLC-like network to select one desired frequency out of many.",electricity-electronics-basics;ac-circuit-analysis-impedance;lesson_passage,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=electricity-electronics-basics&lesson=ac-circuit-analysis-impedance,lesson:electricity-electronics-basics/ac-circuit-analysis-impedance,lesson_passage
184,Electronics,Basics of Electricity & Electronics,Diodes & Transistors — Diodes [9347ed44],A diode is a two-terminal component that lets current flow easily in one direction (forward bias) and blocks it in the other (reverse bias). A silicon diode typically needs about 0.7 V across it before it conducts; an LED needs roughly 1.8-3.3 V depending on its color.,electricity-electronics-basics;diodes-transistors;lesson_passage,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=electricity-electronics-basics&lesson=diodes-transistors,lesson:electricity-electronics-basics/diodes-transistors,lesson_passage
185,Electronics,Basics of Electricity & Electronics,Diodes & Transistors — Diodes [65d87271],Diode behavior by bias direction | Bias direction | Behavior | Forward bias | Conducts current once the forward voltage threshold is reached | Reverse bias | Blocks current (until its reverse breakdown voltage is exceeded),electricity-electronics-basics;diodes-transistors;lesson_passage,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=electricity-electronics-basics&lesson=diodes-transistors,lesson:electricity-electronics-basics/diodes-transistors,lesson_passage
186,Electronics,Basics of Electricity & Electronics,Diodes & Transistors — Transistors [54a62808],"A transistor is a three-terminal device used as an electronic switch or amplifier. The most common types are the Bipolar Junction Transistor (BJT), with terminals Base, Collector, and Emitter, and the MOSFET, with terminals Gate, Drain, and Source.",electricity-electronics-basics;diodes-transistors;lesson_passage,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=electricity-electronics-basics&lesson=diodes-transistors,lesson:electricity-electronics-basics/diodes-transistors,lesson_passage
187,Electronics,Basics of Electricity & Electronics,Diodes & Transistors — Transistors [6b3c832e],"BJT operating regions (NPN transistor) | Region | Condition | Behavior | Cutoff | V_BE below ~0.7 V | Acts like an open switch, no current flows | Active | V_BE ≈ 0.7 V, V_CE above saturation | Acts as an amplifier, I_C proportional to I_B | Saturation | V_CE very low (~0.2 V) | Acts like a closed switch, fully ""on""",electricity-electronics-basics;diodes-transistors;lesson_passage,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=electricity-electronics-basics&lesson=diodes-transistors,lesson:electricity-electronics-basics/diodes-transistors,lesson_passage
188,Electronics,Basics of Electricity & Electronics,Diodes & Transistors — Transistors [a37a87f3],"In digital circuits, transistors are almost always used as switches: fully off (cutoff) represents a logic 0, and fully on (saturation) represents a logic 1. This is the foundation that logic gates are built from.",electricity-electronics-basics;diodes-transistors;lesson_passage,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=electricity-electronics-basics&lesson=diodes-transistors,lesson:electricity-electronics-basics/diodes-transistors,lesson_passage
189,Electronics,Basics of Electricity & Electronics,Power Supplies: Rectifiers & Regulation — From AC mains to usable DC [2633b633],"Most electronics need a steady DC voltage, but wall power is AC. A power supply converts AC to DC in stages: rectification, filtering, and regulation.",electricity-electronics-basics;power-supplies-and-voltage-regulation;lesson_passage,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=electricity-electronics-basics&lesson=power-supplies-and-voltage-regulation,lesson:electricity-electronics-basics/power-supplies-and-voltage-regulation,lesson_passage
190,Electronics,Basics of Electricity & Electronics,Power Supplies: Rectifiers & Regulation — Rectifiers [4bccbe51],"Half-wave vs. full-wave rectification | Type | Diodes needed | Output | Half-wave | 1 | Passes only the positive half of each AC cycle; the negative half is simply blocked, wasting half the input power | Full-wave (bridge) | 4 (in a bridge arrangement) | Flips the negative half of each cycle positive instead of discarding it, so both halves of every cycle contribute to the output",electricity-electronics-basics;power-supplies-and-voltage-regulation;lesson_passage,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=electricity-electronics-basics&lesson=power-supplies-and-voltage-regulation,lesson:electricity-electronics-basics/power-supplies-and-voltage-regulation,lesson_passage
191,Electronics,Basics of Electricity & Electronics,Power Supplies: Rectifiers & Regulation — Rectifiers [319be3f7],"A full-wave bridge rectifier is by far the more common choice in real power supplies, since it uses the input power far more efficiently and produces a smoother, more easily filtered output.",electricity-electronics-basics;power-supplies-and-voltage-regulation;lesson_passage,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=electricity-electronics-basics&lesson=power-supplies-and-voltage-regulation,lesson:electricity-electronics-basics/power-supplies-and-voltage-regulation,lesson_passage
192,Electronics,Basics of Electricity & Electronics,Power Supplies: Rectifiers & Regulation — Filtering [b223bafd],"A rectifier alone produces a bumpy, pulsing DC output, not a flat one. A large filter capacitor placed across the output smooths this out: it charges toward the peak voltage on each pulse and then discharges slowly through the load between pulses, filling in the gaps. The remaining up-and-down wobble is called ripple, and it shrinks as the filter capacitor gets larger or the load current gets smaller.",electricity-electronics-basics;power-supplies-and-voltage-regulation;lesson_passage,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=electricity-electronics-basics&lesson=power-supplies-and-voltage-regulation,lesson:electricity-electronics-basics/power-supplies-and-voltage-regulation,lesson_passage
193,Electronics,Basics of Electricity & Electronics,Power Supplies: Rectifiers & Regulation — Regulation [7ce7c637],Even a well-filtered supply still sags under changing load and still has some ripple -- a voltage regulator holds the output at a fixed voltage despite both.,electricity-electronics-basics;power-supplies-and-voltage-regulation;lesson_passage,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=electricity-electronics-basics&lesson=power-supplies-and-voltage-regulation,lesson:electricity-electronics-basics/power-supplies-and-voltage-regulation,lesson_passage
194,Electronics,Basics of Electricity & Electronics,Power Supplies: Rectifiers & Regulation — Regulation [d19bdc62],"Linear vs. switching regulators | Aspect | Linear regulator | Switching regulator | How it works | Acts like a variable resistor, dropping the excess voltage as heat | Rapidly switches a transistor on/off and filters the result with an inductor and capacitor | Efficiency | Lower -- wastes power proportional to the voltage it drops | Higher -- typically 85-95% | Noise / complexity | Very clean output, simple to use | Some switching noise, more complex, but far less wasted heat | Typical use | Low-power, noise-sensitive analog circuits | Battery-powered devices, anywhere efficiency matters",electricity-electronics-basics;power-supplies-and-voltage-regulation;lesson_passage,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=electricity-electronics-basics&lesson=power-supplies-and-voltage-regulation,lesson:electricity-electronics-basics/power-supplies-and-voltage-regulation,lesson_passage
195,Electronics,Basics of Electricity & Electronics,Operational Amplifiers — The ideal op-amp [aeb4be7e],"An operational amplifier (""op-amp"") is a high-gain amplifier with two inputs (inverting, marked ""-"", and non-inverting, marked ""+"") and one output. Circuit analysis is made simple by two ideal assumptions:",electricity-electronics-basics;operational-amplifiers;lesson_passage,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=electricity-electronics-basics&lesson=operational-amplifiers,lesson:electricity-electronics-basics/operational-amplifiers,lesson_passage
196,Electronics,Basics of Electricity & Electronics,Operational Amplifiers — The ideal op-amp [141dc509],"In a stable circuit with feedback, the op-amp drives its output so that the voltage difference between its two inputs is zero (infinite open-loop gain).",electricity-electronics-basics;operational-amplifiers;lesson_passage,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=electricity-electronics-basics&lesson=operational-amplifiers,lesson:electricity-electronics-basics/operational-amplifiers,lesson_passage
197,Electronics,Basics of Electricity & Electronics,Operational Amplifiers — Inverting amplifier [3a154d8c],"The negative sign means the output is an inverted (flipped) and scaled copy of the input. The gain magnitude is set purely by the ratio of two resistors, Rf and Rin -- not by anything intrinsic to the op-amp itself, which is what makes op-amp circuits so predictable and easy to design.",electricity-electronics-basics;operational-amplifiers;lesson_passage,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=electricity-electronics-basics&lesson=operational-amplifiers,lesson:electricity-electronics-basics/operational-amplifiers,lesson_passage
198,Electronics,Basics of Electricity & Electronics,Operational Amplifiers — Non-inverting amplifier [5c7f3d93],"Here the output is in phase with the input, and the gain is always at least 1 (setting Rf = 0 gives a gain of exactly 1 -- a unity-gain buffer).",electricity-electronics-basics;operational-amplifiers;lesson_passage,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=electricity-electronics-basics&lesson=operational-amplifiers,lesson:electricity-electronics-basics/operational-amplifiers,lesson_passage
199,Electronics,Basics of Electricity & Electronics,Operational Amplifiers — Comparator [873fe74f],"Used without feedback, an op-amp simply drives its output fully to one supply rail or the other, depending on which input is higher -- a comparator, useful for detecting when a signal crosses a threshold.",electricity-electronics-basics;operational-amplifiers;lesson_passage,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=electricity-electronics-basics&lesson=operational-amplifiers,lesson:electricity-electronics-basics/operational-amplifiers,lesson_passage
200,Electronics,Basics of Electricity & Electronics,Operational Amplifiers — Comparator [0f39f567],Op-amp used as a comparator | Condition | Output | V+ > V- | Output swings to the positive supply rail | V+ < V- | Output swings to the negative supply rail (or ground),electricity-electronics-basics;operational-amplifiers;lesson_passage,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=electricity-electronics-basics&lesson=operational-amplifiers,lesson:electricity-electronics-basics/operational-amplifiers,lesson_passage
201,Electronics,Basics of Electricity & Electronics,Passive Filters — Filtering by frequency [7cab65f6],A filter passes some frequencies while attenuating (weakening) others. The simplest filters combine just one resistor and one capacitor.,electricity-electronics-basics;passive-filters;lesson_passage,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=electricity-electronics-basics&lesson=passive-filters,lesson:electricity-electronics-basics/passive-filters,lesson_passage
202,Electronics,Basics of Electricity & Electronics,Passive Filters — Low-pass RC filter [a6899a40],"Taking the output across the capacitor in a series RC circuit passes low frequencies (where the capacitor′s reactance is large, so little signal is dropped across it) and attenuates high frequencies (where reactance is small, so most of the signal is dropped across the resistor instead).",electricity-electronics-basics;passive-filters;lesson_passage,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=electricity-electronics-basics&lesson=passive-filters,lesson:electricity-electronics-basics/passive-filters,lesson_passage
203,Electronics,Basics of Electricity & Electronics,Passive Filters — High-pass RC filter [8fc94d77],"Taking the output across the resistor instead gives the opposite behavior: high frequencies pass through relatively unattenuated, while low frequencies are blocked (the capacitor′s large reactance at low frequency drops most of the signal there instead).",electricity-electronics-basics;passive-filters;lesson_passage,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=electricity-electronics-basics&lesson=passive-filters,lesson:electricity-electronics-basics/passive-filters,lesson_passage
204,Electronics,Basics of Electricity & Electronics,Passive Filters — The cutoff frequency [6a9eb939],"At f_cutoff, the output power is reduced to half of its input value (the voltage drops to about 70.7% of the input, or -3 dB) -- this is the conventional dividing line between a filter′s ""passband"" and its ""stopband,"" even though the roll-off beyond that point is gradual rather than a hard cutoff.",electricity-electronics-basics;passive-filters;lesson_passage,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=electricity-electronics-basics&lesson=passive-filters,lesson:electricity-electronics-basics/passive-filters,lesson_passage
205,Electronics,Basics of Electricity & Electronics,Passive Filters — RL filters [fdd0b7a4],"Resistor-inductor pairs form filters the same way, just with the roles reversed compared to RC, since inductive reactance rises with frequency instead of falling: a resistor-inductor low-pass filter takes its output across the resistor, and a high-pass filter takes its output across the inductor.",electricity-electronics-basics;passive-filters;lesson_passage,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=electricity-electronics-basics&lesson=passive-filters,lesson:electricity-electronics-basics/passive-filters,lesson_passage
206,Electronics,Basics of Electricity & Electronics,Electromagnetism & Induction — Current creates a magnetic field [12779f64],"Any current-carrying wire produces a magnetic field around it; coiling the wire concentrates and strengthens that field, which is exactly what an inductor is. Wrapping the coil around an iron core concentrates it further still -- the basis of electromagnets, relays, and motor windings.",electricity-electronics-basics;electromagnetism-and-induction;lesson_passage,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=electricity-electronics-basics&lesson=electromagnetism-and-induction,lesson:electricity-electronics-basics/electromagnetism-and-induction,lesson_passage
207,Electronics,Basics of Electricity & Electronics,Electromagnetism & Induction — Faraday′s law of induction [f51323d1],"A changing magnetic field through a coil induces a voltage in that coil, proportional to how fast the magnetic flux is changing:",electricity-electronics-basics;electromagnetism-and-induction;lesson_passage,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=electricity-electronics-basics&lesson=electromagnetism-and-induction,lesson:electricity-electronics-basics/electromagnetism-and-induction,lesson_passage
208,Electronics,Basics of Electricity & Electronics,Electromagnetism & Induction — Faraday′s law of induction [1ad41412],"where N is the number of turns in the coil and Φ is the magnetic flux through it. This is the principle behind generators (a changing magnetic field, produced by moving a magnet or coil, creates electricity) and transformers (a changing current in one coil induces a voltage in a nearby coil).",electricity-electronics-basics;electromagnetism-and-induction;lesson_passage,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=electricity-electronics-basics&lesson=electromagnetism-and-induction,lesson:electricity-electronics-basics/electromagnetism-and-induction,lesson_passage
209,Electronics,Basics of Electricity & Electronics,Electromagnetism & Induction — Transformers [5b241da4],"A transformer uses two coils sharing a common magnetic core to step voltage up or down between its primary and secondary windings, with essentially no direct electrical connection between the two sides.",electricity-electronics-basics;electromagnetism-and-induction;lesson_passage,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=electricity-electronics-basics&lesson=electromagnetism-and-induction,lesson:electricity-electronics-basics/electromagnetism-and-induction,lesson_passage
210,Electronics,Basics of Electricity & Electronics,Electromagnetism & Induction — Transformers [f135e45c],"An ideal transformer conserves power, so as voltage steps up, current steps down by the same ratio (and vice versa) -- it cannot deliver more power out than is put in.",electricity-electronics-basics;electromagnetism-and-induction;lesson_passage,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=electricity-electronics-basics&lesson=electromagnetism-and-induction,lesson:electricity-electronics-basics/electromagnetism-and-induction,lesson_passage
211,Electronics,Basics of Electricity & Electronics,"Electromagnetism & Induction — Motors and generators: the same principle, run in opposite directions [576c68ce]","A current-carrying conductor in a magnetic field experiences a force -- the basis of every electric motor. Running that same physical arrangement in reverse, mechanically spinning a conductor through a magnetic field, induces a current instead -- the basis of every generator. This connects directly to the Motor Control lesson in the Robotics course: a spinning DC motor is always also acting a little like a generator, producing a ""back-EMF"" that opposes the current driving it.",electricity-electronics-basics;electromagnetism-and-induction;lesson_passage,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=electricity-electronics-basics&lesson=electromagnetism-and-induction,lesson:electricity-electronics-basics/electromagnetism-and-induction,lesson_passage
212,Electronics,Basics of Electricity & Electronics,Measurement Instruments — The multimeter [18a889b0],"A multimeter is the single most useful tool in electronics, typically able to measure voltage, current, resistance, and continuity.",electricity-electronics-basics;measurement-instruments;lesson_passage,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=electricity-electronics-basics&lesson=measurement-instruments,lesson:electricity-electronics-basics/measurement-instruments,lesson_passage
213,Electronics,Basics of Electricity & Electronics,Measurement Instruments — The multimeter [5fea195e],"How to connect a multimeter | Measurement | Connection | Common mistake | Voltage (V) | In parallel across the two points being measured | None major -- a voltmeter has very high input resistance, so it barely disturbs the circuit | Current (A) | In series, breaking the circuit so current flows through the meter | Leaving the meter in current mode while measuring voltage can short the circuit through the meter′s very low resistance, often blowing its internal fuse | Resistance (Ω) | Across an isolated component, with power removed from the circuit | Measuring resistance in a live, powered circuit gives a wrong reading and can damage the meter",electricity-electronics-basics;measurement-instruments;lesson_passage,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=electricity-electronics-basics&lesson=measurement-instruments,lesson:electricity-electronics-basics/measurement-instruments,lesson_passage
214,Electronics,Basics of Electricity & Electronics,Measurement Instruments — The oscilloscope [464a8832],"An oscilloscope plots voltage on the vertical axis against time on the horizontal axis, which a multimeter cannot show -- it is the tool for seeing waveform shape, frequency, timing relationships between signals, and glitches that a single numeric reading would completely miss.",electricity-electronics-basics;measurement-instruments;lesson_passage,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=electricity-electronics-basics&lesson=measurement-instruments,lesson:electricity-electronics-basics/measurement-instruments,lesson_passage
215,Electronics,Basics of Electricity & Electronics,Measurement Instruments — The oscilloscope [d93bd5ec],"Key oscilloscope controls | Control | What it sets | Volts/division | Vertical scale -- how many volts each grid square represents | Time/division | Horizontal scale -- how much time each grid square represents | Trigger | The condition (e.g. a rising edge crossing a set voltage) that tells the scope when to start each new sweep, so a repeating waveform appears stable on screen instead of scrolling",electricity-electronics-basics;measurement-instruments;lesson_passage,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=electricity-electronics-basics&lesson=measurement-instruments,lesson:electricity-electronics-basics/measurement-instruments,lesson_passage
216,Electronics,Basics of Electricity & Electronics,Circuit Protection & Safety — Fuses and circuit breakers [e8b035cb],"Both protect a circuit from excessive current, which can overheat wires and start fires, by breaking the circuit before that happens.",electricity-electronics-basics;circuit-protection-and-safety;lesson_passage,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=electricity-electronics-basics&lesson=circuit-protection-and-safety,lesson:electricity-electronics-basics/circuit-protection-and-safety,lesson_passage
217,Electronics,Basics of Electricity & Electronics,Circuit Protection & Safety — Fuses and circuit breakers [841a275e],Fuses vs. circuit breakers | Aspect | Fuse | Circuit breaker | How it works | A thin metal element melts and breaks the circuit when current exceeds its rating | A mechanical (or electronic) switch trips open when current exceeds its rating | After tripping | Must be replaced | Can simply be reset and reused | Typical use | Individual devices and circuit boards | Household electrical panels,electricity-electronics-basics;circuit-protection-and-safety;lesson_passage,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=electricity-electronics-basics&lesson=circuit-protection-and-safety,lesson:electricity-electronics-basics/circuit-protection-and-safety,lesson_passage
218,Electronics,Basics of Electricity & Electronics,Circuit Protection & Safety — Grounding [b6bd7340],"""Ground"" (or ""earth"") is a common reference point that circuit voltages are measured relative to, and, in mains wiring, a dedicated safety conductor. A properly grounded metal appliance chassis gives a fault current (e.g. a wire touching the case) a low-resistance path back to the source, tripping a breaker or fuse quickly instead of leaving the case dangerously energized and waiting for a person to complete the circuit instead.",electricity-electronics-basics;circuit-protection-and-safety;lesson_passage,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=electricity-electronics-basics&lesson=circuit-protection-and-safety,lesson:electricity-electronics-basics/circuit-protection-and-safety,lesson_passage
219,Electronics,Basics of Electricity & Electronics,Circuit Protection & Safety — Electric shock and safe practice [7daaeb8c],"Approximate effects of AC current through the body (illustrative only -- actual thresholds vary by person and current path) | Current | Typical effect | ≈ 1 mA | Threshold of perception (a slight tingle) | ≈ 10-20 mA | Painful shock; muscles may not be able to let go (""let-go threshold"") | ≈ 100-200 mA | Can cause ventricular fibrillation and be fatal",electricity-electronics-basics;circuit-protection-and-safety;lesson_passage,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=electricity-electronics-basics&lesson=circuit-protection-and-safety,lesson:electricity-electronics-basics/circuit-protection-and-safety,lesson_passage
220,Electronics,Basics of Electricity & Electronics,Circuit Protection & Safety — Electric shock and safe practice [c835c2f1],"A few practical habits matter far more than memorizing thresholds: always assume a circuit is live until it has been verified otherwise with a meter, discharge capacitors before touching a circuit (they can hold a dangerous charge long after power is removed), work with one hand where practical to avoid a current path across the chest, and never work alone on anything connected to mains voltage.",electricity-electronics-basics;circuit-protection-and-safety;lesson_passage,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=electricity-electronics-basics&lesson=circuit-protection-and-safety,lesson:electricity-electronics-basics/circuit-protection-and-safety,lesson_passage
221,Electronics,Basics of Electricity & Electronics,Circuit Protection & Safety — Electrostatic discharge (ESD) [1780e11b],"Static electricity built up on a person or surface can silently destroy sensitive semiconductors (especially MOSFETs) with a discharge far too small to feel. Anti-static wrist straps, ESD-safe mats, and grounded soldering equipment are standard precautions when handling bare ICs and other sensitive components.",electricity-electronics-basics;circuit-protection-and-safety;lesson_passage,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=electricity-electronics-basics&lesson=circuit-protection-and-safety,lesson:electricity-electronics-basics/circuit-protection-and-safety,lesson_passage
222,Digital Logic Design,Digital Logic Design,Number Systems — Why digital systems use binary [47af6a56],"Digital circuits represent information using only two voltage levels: ""high"" and ""low"", corresponding to the digits 1 and 0. This is called the binary (base-2) number system. Because raw binary is hard for humans to read, engineers also use octal (base-8) and hexadecimal (base-16) as compact shorthand for groups of bits.",digital-logic-design;number-systems;lesson_passage,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=digital-logic-design&lesson=number-systems,lesson:digital-logic-design/number-systems,lesson_passage
223,Digital Logic Design,Digital Logic Design,Number Systems — Why digital systems use binary [eea45623],Decimal 0-15 across the common number systems | Decimal | Binary | Octal | Hexadecimal | 0 | 0000 | 0 | 0 | 1 | 0001 | 1 | 1 | 2 | 0010 | 2 | 2 | 3 | 0011 | 3 | 3 | 4 | 0100 | 4 | 4 | 5 | 0101 | 5 | 5 | 6 | 0110 | 6 | 6 | 7 | 0111 | 7 | 7 | 8 | 1000 | 10 | 8 | 9 | 1001 | 11 | 9 | 10 | 1010 | 12 | A | 11 | 1011 | 13 | B | 12 | 1100 | 14 | C | 13 | 1101 | 15 | D | 14 | 1110 | 16 | E | 15 | 1111 | 17 | F,digital-logic-design;number-systems;lesson_passage,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=digital-logic-design&lesson=number-systems,lesson:digital-logic-design/number-systems,lesson_passage
224,Digital Logic Design,Digital Logic Design,Number Systems — Converting binary to decimal [f7659a3d],"Each bit represents a power of two. To convert, add up the powers of two where the bit is 1:",digital-logic-design;number-systems;lesson_passage,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=digital-logic-design&lesson=number-systems,lesson:digital-logic-design/number-systems,lesson_passage
