236,Digital Logic Design,Digital Logic Design,Binary Arithmetic & Signed Numbers — Overflow [e4f7cfc4],"Overflow occurs when adding two numbers with the same sign produces a result with the opposite sign -- meaning the true result did not fit in the available bits. Hardware detects this by comparing the carry into the sign bit with the carry out of the sign bit: if they differ, overflow occurred.",digital-logic-design;binary-arithmetic-signed-numbers;lesson_passage,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=digital-logic-design&lesson=binary-arithmetic-signed-numbers,lesson:digital-logic-design/binary-arithmetic-signed-numbers,lesson_passage
237,Digital Logic Design,Digital Logic Design,Binary Arithmetic & Signed Numbers — Binary-coded decimal (BCD) [5a2fd598],"BCD encodes each decimal digit (0-9) separately in 4 bits, rather than converting the whole number to pure binary. It wastes some bit patterns (1010-1111 are unused) but makes conversion to and from human-readable decimal trivial -- useful for displays and calculators.",digital-logic-design;binary-arithmetic-signed-numbers;lesson_passage,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=digital-logic-design&lesson=binary-arithmetic-signed-numbers,lesson:digital-logic-design/binary-arithmetic-signed-numbers,lesson_passage
238,Digital Logic Design,Digital Logic Design,Binary Arithmetic & Signed Numbers — Gray code [9f9e1c11],"Gray code orders binary patterns so that only one bit changes between consecutive values, which avoids glitches when a counter or sensor value is read while it is changing. It is widely used in rotary encoders and in Karnaugh map layouts.",digital-logic-design;binary-arithmetic-signed-numbers;lesson_passage,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=digital-logic-design&lesson=binary-arithmetic-signed-numbers,lesson:digital-logic-design/binary-arithmetic-signed-numbers,lesson_passage
