240,Digital Logic Design,Digital Logic Design,Boolean Algebra Basics — What Boolean algebra is [9cb64530],"Boolean algebra is a mathematical system, introduced by George Boole and later applied to switching circuits by Claude Shannon, that works with variables which can only take one of two values: 0 (false) and 1 (true). It gives a formal way to write, manipulate, and simplify the same logic that a truth table or a gate diagram describes.",digital-logic-design;boolean-algebra-basics;lesson_passage,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=digital-logic-design&lesson=boolean-algebra-basics,lesson:digital-logic-design/boolean-algebra-basics,lesson_passage
241,Digital Logic Design,Digital Logic Design,Boolean Algebra Basics — The three basic operations [2897cb91],"Boolean operations and their notations | Operation | Also called | Common notations | Gate equivalent | Complement | NOT, inversion | A′, ¬A, an overbar over A | NOT gate | Logical product | AND, conjunction | A · B, AB, A ∧ B | AND gate | Logical sum | OR, disjunction | A + B, A ∨ B | OR gate",digital-logic-design;boolean-algebra-basics;lesson_passage,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=digital-logic-design&lesson=boolean-algebra-basics,lesson:digital-logic-design/boolean-algebra-basics,lesson_passage
242,Digital Logic Design,Digital Logic Design,Boolean Algebra Basics — The three basic operations [3decfde9],"Despite the ""+"" and ""·"" symbols borrowed from ordinary algebra, Boolean addition and multiplication do not behave exactly like arithmetic -- most importantly, 1 + 1 = 1, not 2, because the result can only ever be 0 or 1.",digital-logic-design;boolean-algebra-basics;lesson_passage,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=digital-logic-design&lesson=boolean-algebra-basics,lesson:digital-logic-design/boolean-algebra-basics,lesson_passage
243,Digital Logic Design,Digital Logic Design,Boolean Algebra Basics — Operator precedence [411fb06d],"Just like arithmetic, Boolean expressions have an order of operations: complement first, then AND, then OR (parentheses override this order). For example:",digital-logic-design;boolean-algebra-basics;lesson_passage,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=digital-logic-design&lesson=boolean-algebra-basics,lesson:digital-logic-design/boolean-algebra-basics,lesson_passage
244,Digital Logic Design,Digital Logic Design,Boolean Algebra Basics — Three equivalent descriptions of the same logic [3d43595d],"Any Boolean function can be described in three completely equivalent ways -- a truth table, a Boolean expression, and a gate-level logic diagram -- and a good digital designer moves freely between all three.",digital-logic-design;boolean-algebra-basics;lesson_passage,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=digital-logic-design&lesson=boolean-algebra-basics,lesson:digital-logic-design/boolean-algebra-basics,lesson_passage
