282,Digital Logic Design,Digital Logic Design,Canonical Forms: SOP & POS — Converting between SOP and POS [99017590],"Given the canonical SOP as Σm(...), the maxterm indices for the POS form are simply all the indices not in that list, with De Morgan′s theorem justifying the switch between AND and OR. Canonical forms are rarely the most efficient circuit to actually build -- the next lesson covers how to minimize them.",digital-logic-design;canonical-forms-sop-pos;lesson_passage,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=digital-logic-design&lesson=canonical-forms-sop-pos,lesson:digital-logic-design/canonical-forms-sop-pos,lesson_passage
283,Digital Logic Design,Digital Logic Design,Karnaugh Maps & Minimization — Why Karnaugh maps [b4b1ee1c],"Algebraic simplification works, but it is easy to miss a possible simplification by hand. A Karnaugh map (K-map) rearranges a truth table into a grid where physically adjacent cells differ by exactly one bit, so the groups you can simplify are visually obvious as rectangular blocks of 1s.",digital-logic-design;karnaugh-maps;lesson_passage,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=digital-logic-design&lesson=karnaugh-maps,lesson:digital-logic-design/karnaugh-maps,lesson_passage
