302,Digital Logic Design,Digital Logic Design,Combinational Circuit Design — Design trade-offs [b8176a95],"Common trade-offs in combinational design | Trade-off | Why it matters | Gate count vs. speed | Fewer levels of logic between input and output means shorter propagation delay, even at the cost of more gates | Fan-in limits | Real gates only accept a limited number of inputs; a 6-input AND may need to be built from smaller gates | Fan-out limits | A single gate output can only reliably drive a limited number of downstream gate inputs before needing a buffer | Reusability | Building from standard blocks (muxes, decoders, adders) is easier to design, verify, and reuse than one giant minimized expression",digital-logic-design;combinational-circuit-design;lesson_passage,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=digital-logic-design&lesson=combinational-circuit-design,lesson:digital-logic-design/combinational-circuit-design,lesson_passage
