383,Digital Logic Design,Digital Logic Design,Counters — Designing a mod-N counter [1af9464f],"A counter that must count through only N states (rather than a full power of two) is designed with the standard sequential design process from the next lesson: write the state table for the N desired states, use flip-flop excitation tables to work out the required J/K (or D, or T) input equations for each transition, minimize those equations, and add a reset path for any unused states so the counter cannot get stuck outside its intended cycle.",digital-logic-design;counters;lesson_passage,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=digital-logic-design&lesson=counters,lesson:digital-logic-design/counters,lesson_passage
384,Digital Logic Design,Digital Logic Design,Counters — BCD (decade) counter [844aa652],"A decade counter counts 0000 through 1001 (0-9 in BCD) and then resets to 0000 on the next clock, instead of continuing to 1010-1111. It is commonly built either by adding reset-on-1010 detection logic to a 4-bit binary counter, or by designing the toggle equations directly for exactly those 10 states, using the excitation-table method above.",digital-logic-design;counters;lesson_passage,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=digital-logic-design&lesson=counters,lesson:digital-logic-design/counters,lesson_passage
385,Digital Logic Design,Digital Logic Design,Finite State Machines — Finite state machines [76d9becf],"A finite state machine (FSM) is the general model behind every sequential circuit: a system that is always in exactly one of a finite number of states, and moves between states based on its current state and current inputs, on each clock edge.",digital-logic-design;sequential-circuit-design-fsm;lesson_passage,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=digital-logic-design&lesson=sequential-circuit-design-fsm,lesson:digital-logic-design/sequential-circuit-design-fsm,lesson_passage
386,Digital Logic Design,Digital Logic Design,Finite State Machines — Moore vs. Mealy machines [dc575f8b],"Moore vs. Mealy machines | Aspect | Moore machine | Mealy machine | Output depends on | Current state only | Current state and current input | Output changes | Only on a clock edge, synchronized with the state | Can change as soon as the input changes, between clock edges | State diagram notation | Output labeled inside each state circle | Output labeled on each transition arrow, alongside the input | States needed | Often needs more states to separate outputs | Often needs fewer states | Timing behavior | Glitch-free, easier to reason about | Can react one clock cycle sooner, but outputs can glitch",digital-logic-design;sequential-circuit-design-fsm;lesson_passage,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=digital-logic-design&lesson=sequential-circuit-design-fsm,lesson:digital-logic-design/sequential-circuit-design-fsm,lesson_passage
387,Digital Logic Design,Digital Logic Design,Finite State Machines — State diagrams and state tables [beb4e134],"A state diagram is a graph: circles are states, and arrows are transitions labeled with the input value(s) that cause them (and, for a Mealy machine, the output produced). A state table lists the same information as rows -- current state, input, next state, and output -- and is simply the truth table for a sequential circuit.",digital-logic-design;sequential-circuit-design-fsm;lesson_passage,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=digital-logic-design&lesson=sequential-circuit-design-fsm,lesson:digital-logic-design/sequential-circuit-design-fsm,lesson_passage
