525,Robotics,Robotics,Robot Locomotion Types — Wheeled locomotion [8c125705],"Most beginner and intermediate robots use wheels, since they are mechanically simple and easy to control in software, at the cost of not handling rough terrain as well as legs or tracks.",robotics;robot-locomotion-types;lesson_passage,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=robotics&lesson=robot-locomotion-types,lesson:robotics/robot-locomotion-types,lesson_passage
526,Robotics,Robotics,Robot Locomotion Types — Wheeled locomotion [ef08efb8],"Common wheeled drive configurations | Configuration | How it steers | Trade-offs | Differential drive | Two independently driven wheels (plus one or more passive casters); turning comes from spinning the two wheels at different speeds | Simple, cheap, can turn in place, but slips sideways easily on smooth floors | Ackermann steering | Front wheels physically pivot, like a car; rear wheels are usually the driven ones | Mechanically more complex, but tracks more predictably at speed -- the standard choice for RC-car-based robots | Omni-wheel / mecanum | Wheels with small rollers around their circumference, allowing sideways slip | Can move in any direction (including sideways) without turning first, at the cost of more complex wheels and motor control | Tracked (tank-style) | Continuous tracks driven like a differential drive | Excellent traction and rough-terrain ability, but higher friction and power use during turns",robotics;robot-locomotion-types;lesson_passage,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=robotics&lesson=robot-locomotion-types,lesson:robotics/robot-locomotion-types,lesson_passage
527,Robotics,Robotics,Robot Locomotion Types — Legged locomotion [a5feda4c],"Legged robots (from simple hexapods to complex bipeds) trade mechanical and control complexity for the ability to cross terrain wheels cannot -- steps, gaps, and loose or uneven ground. Even a basic hexapod typically needs independent control of 2-3 servo motors per leg (12-18 total), making it dramatically more code- and hardware-intensive than a differential-drive robot.",robotics;robot-locomotion-types;lesson_passage,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=robotics&lesson=robot-locomotion-types,lesson:robotics/robot-locomotion-types,lesson_passage
528,Robotics,Robotics,Robot Locomotion Types — Choosing a configuration [c40821ac],"For a first robotics project, differential drive is almost always the right starting point: it needs only two motors and one motor driver, the kinematics (covered in the next lesson) are the simplest of any configuration, and it is directly compatible with the DC motor and encoder techniques used throughout the rest of this course.",robotics;robot-locomotion-types;lesson_passage,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=robotics&lesson=robot-locomotion-types,lesson:robotics/robot-locomotion-types,lesson_passage
529,Robotics,Robotics,Kinematics & Odometry Math — Forward kinematics: from wheel speeds to robot motion [3bcaf2a8],"For a differential-drive robot, the robot′s linear velocity v (straight-ahead speed) and angular velocity ω (turning rate) are determined by the speeds of the left and right wheels:",robotics;kinematics-and-odometry-math;lesson_passage,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=robotics&lesson=kinematics-and-odometry-math,lesson:robotics/kinematics-and-odometry-math,lesson_passage
530,Robotics,Robotics,Kinematics & Odometry Math — Forward kinematics: from wheel speeds to robot motion [eba9777b],where L is the distance between the two wheels (the wheelbase). Equal wheel speeds (v_left = v_right) give pure straight-line motion (ω = 0); opposite speeds (v_right = -v_left) give a pure in-place rotation.,robotics;kinematics-and-odometry-math;lesson_passage,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=robotics&lesson=kinematics-and-odometry-math,lesson:robotics/kinematics-and-odometry-math,lesson_passage
531,Robotics,Robotics,Kinematics & Odometry Math — Inverse kinematics: from desired motion to wheel speeds [75c32bb4],"Robot control code more often needs the opposite: given a desired v and ω, what should each wheel′s speed be? Solving the equations above:",robotics;kinematics-and-odometry-math;lesson_passage,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=robotics&lesson=kinematics-and-odometry-math,lesson:robotics/kinematics-and-odometry-math,lesson_passage
532,Robotics,Robotics,Kinematics & Odometry Math — Implementing it in C++ [12abf63d],"const float WHEELBASE = 0.15;   // meters between the two wheels
const float MAX_SPEED_MPS = 0.5;

void driveDifferential(float linearVel, float angularVel, int &leftPwm, int &rightPwm) {
    float vLeft  = linearVel - (angularVel * WHEELBASE) / 2.0;
    float vRight = linearVel + (angularVel * WHEELBASE) / 2.0;

    leftPwm  = constrain((int)(vLeft  / MAX_SPEED_MPS * 255), -255, 255);
    rightPwm = constrain((int)(vRight / MAX_SPEED_MPS * 255), -255, 255);
}",robotics;kinematics-and-odometry-math;code,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=robotics&lesson=kinematics-and-odometry-math,lesson:robotics/kinematics-and-odometry-math,code
533,Robotics,Robotics,Kinematics & Odometry Math — Implementing it in C++ [32914930],"constrain() is an Arduino built-in that clamps a value into a given range -- essential here, since the raw math can easily ask for a PWM value outside the range the motor driver actually accepts.",robotics;kinematics-and-odometry-math;lesson_passage,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=robotics&lesson=kinematics-and-odometry-math,lesson:robotics/kinematics-and-odometry-math,lesson_passage
534,Robotics,Robotics,Kinematics & Odometry Math — Odometry: estimating position from wheel motion [f5146c6b],"Running the forward kinematics equations continuously, using measured wheel speeds from encoders (covered later in this course) instead of commanded ones, lets a robot estimate its own position and heading over time -- a technique called odometry:",robotics;kinematics-and-odometry-math;lesson_passage,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=robotics&lesson=kinematics-and-odometry-math,lesson:robotics/kinematics-and-odometry-math,lesson_passage
535,Robotics,Robotics,Kinematics & Odometry Math — Odometry: estimating position from wheel motion [2a08ea84],"float odoX = 0, odoY = 0, heading = 0;   // robot pose estimate

void updateOdometry(float vLeft, float vRight, float dt) {
    float v = (vLeft + vRight) / 2.0;
    float omega = (vRight - vLeft) / WHEELBASE;

    odoX += v * cos(heading) * dt;
    odoY += v * sin(heading) * dt;
    heading += omega * dt;
}",robotics;kinematics-and-odometry-math;code,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=robotics&lesson=kinematics-and-odometry-math,lesson:robotics/kinematics-and-odometry-math,code
536,Robotics,Robotics,Kinematics & Odometry Math — Odometry: estimating position from wheel motion [d622966b],"Odometry alone always drifts over time -- small errors in wheel speed measurement and wheel slip accumulate, so the estimated position slowly diverges from the robot′s true position. The Localization & Dead Reckoning lesson later in this course covers how odometry is combined with other sensors to reduce this drift.",robotics;kinematics-and-odometry-math;lesson_passage,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=robotics&lesson=kinematics-and-odometry-math,lesson:robotics/kinematics-and-odometry-math,lesson_passage
