553,Robotics,Robotics,Stepper Motor Control — Using the Stepper and AccelStepper libraries [6c7088f7],"The built-in Stepper library handles the step sequencing shown above internally. The third-party AccelStepper library goes further, adding acceleration/deceleration ramps and non-blocking stepping (compatible with the millis()-based patterns from the Arduino course), which matters once a stepper needs to move smoothly alongside other robot behavior instead of blocking everything else while it steps.",robotics;stepper-motor-control;lesson_passage,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=robotics&lesson=stepper-motor-control,lesson:robotics/stepper-motor-control,lesson_passage
554,Robotics,Robotics,Servo Motors & Robotic Arm Control — Multi-servo control [20016bb8],"#include <Servo.h>

Servo baseServo, shoulderServo, elbowServo, gripperServo;

void setup() {
    baseServo.attach(3);
    shoulderServo.attach(5);
    elbowServo.attach(6);
    gripperServo.attach(9);
}

void moveArm(int base, int shoulder, int elbow, int gripper) {
    baseServo.write(base);
    shoulderServo.write(shoulder);
    elbowServo.write(elbow);
    gripperServo.write(gripper);
}

void loop() {
    moveArm(90, 45, 120, 30);   // move to a ""pick"" pose
    delay(1000);
    moveArm(90, 45, 120, 90);   // close the gripper
    delay(500);
    moveArm(0, 90, 60, 90);     // move to a ""place"" pose, gripper still closed
    delay(1000);
}",robotics;servo-and-robotic-arm-control;code,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=robotics&lesson=servo-and-robotic-arm-control,lesson:robotics/servo-and-robotic-arm-control,code
555,Robotics,Robotics,Servo Motors & Robotic Arm Control — Multi-servo control [c377d57a],"Sending all four write() calls back to back like this moves every joint toward its target angle at roughly the same time, since each servo independently tracks toward whatever angle it was last told, rather than the Arduino needing to sequence them one at a time.",robotics;servo-and-robotic-arm-control;lesson_passage,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=robotics&lesson=servo-and-robotic-arm-control,lesson:robotics/servo-and-robotic-arm-control,lesson_passage
556,Robotics,Robotics,Servo Motors & Robotic Arm Control — Smooth motion: interpolating between angles [9a8c444a],"void moveServoSmoothly(Servo &servo, int fromAngle, int toAngle, int stepDelayMs) {
    if (toAngle > fromAngle) {
        for (int angle = fromAngle; angle <= toAngle; angle++) {
            servo.write(angle);
            delay(stepDelayMs);
        }
    } else {
        for (int angle = fromAngle; angle >= toAngle; angle--) {
            servo.write(angle);
            delay(stepDelayMs);
        }
    }
}",robotics;servo-and-robotic-arm-control;code,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=robotics&lesson=servo-and-robotic-arm-control,lesson:robotics/servo-and-robotic-arm-control,code
557,Robotics,Robotics,Servo Motors & Robotic Arm Control — Two-link arm inverse kinematics [a6a828bf],"For a simple two-link arm (an upper arm of length L1 and a forearm of length L2), computing the shoulder and elbow angles needed to reach a target point (x, y) uses the law of cosines:",robotics;servo-and-robotic-arm-control;lesson_passage,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=robotics&lesson=servo-and-robotic-arm-control,lesson:robotics/servo-and-robotic-arm-control,lesson_passage
558,Robotics,Robotics,Servo Motors & Robotic Arm Control — Two-link arm inverse kinematics [87db4489],"float elbowAngle(float x, float y, float L1, float L2) {
    float dist2 = x * x + y * y;
    float cosElbow = (dist2 - L1 * L1 - L2 * L2) / (2 * L1 * L2);
    cosElbow = constrain(cosElbow, -1.0, 1.0);   // guard against tiny floating-point overshoot
    return acos(cosElbow);
}

float shoulderAngle(float x, float y, float L1, float L2, float elbow) {
    float k1 = L1 + L2 * cos(elbow);
    float k2 = L2 * sin(elbow);
    return atan2(y, x) - atan2(k2, k1);
}",robotics;servo-and-robotic-arm-control;code,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=robotics&lesson=servo-and-robotic-arm-control,lesson:robotics/servo-and-robotic-arm-control,code
559,Robotics,Robotics,Servo Motors & Robotic Arm Control — Two-link arm inverse kinematics [926b75bf],"This is the same inverse-kinematics idea introduced for wheeled robots in the Kinematics & Odometry lesson, applied to a rotating-joint arm instead of wheels: given a desired end-effector position, solve backward for the joint angles that produce it.",robotics;servo-and-robotic-arm-control;lesson_passage,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=robotics&lesson=servo-and-robotic-arm-control,lesson:robotics/servo-and-robotic-arm-control,lesson_passage
560,Robotics,Robotics,Encoders & Speed Measurement — Why encoders [090fa786],"Commanding a certain PWM value to a motor does not guarantee a certain speed -- battery voltage sags, friction varies, and identical motors never behave perfectly identically. An encoder measures actual shaft rotation directly, which is what makes closed-loop speed and position control (and accurate odometry) possible at all.",robotics;encoders-and-speed-measurement;lesson_passage,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=robotics&lesson=encoders-and-speed-measurement,lesson:robotics/encoders-and-speed-measurement,lesson_passage
561,Robotics,Robotics,Encoders & Speed Measurement — Quadrature encoders [f90eb907],"A quadrature encoder outputs two square-wave signals (commonly labeled A and B), offset from each other by 90° -- which of the two signals leads the other reveals the direction of rotation, not just that rotation is happening.",robotics;encoders-and-speed-measurement;lesson_passage,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=robotics&lesson=encoders-and-speed-measurement,lesson:robotics/encoders-and-speed-measurement,lesson_passage
