590,Robotics,Robotics,Obstacle Avoidance Algorithms — Potential fields: a smoother alternative [1340f49c],"A more advanced technique treats obstacles as generating a virtual repulsive force and the goal as generating a virtual attractive force, and steers the robot along the combined (vector-summed) direction of both -- producing smoother, more continuous avoidance behavior than a discrete stop-and-turn sequence, though it can get trapped in local minima (e.g. a U-shaped obstacle) where the forces cancel out with no clear way forward.",robotics;obstacle-avoidance-algorithms;lesson_passage,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=robotics&lesson=obstacle-avoidance-algorithms,lesson:robotics/obstacle-avoidance-algorithms,lesson_passage
591,Robotics,Robotics,Control Loops — Open-loop vs. closed-loop control [ce9afd8c],"Open-loop control sends a command and hopes for the best, with no feedback (e.g. running a motor at a fixed PWM value). Closed-loop control measures the actual result with a sensor and continuously adjusts the command to reduce the error between the desired and actual state.",robotics;control-loops;lesson_passage,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=robotics&lesson=control-loops,lesson:robotics/control-loops,lesson_passage
