595,Robotics,Robotics,Control Loops — PID control [0d00d0ce],"Effect of increasing each PID gain | Gain increased | Typical effect | P (Kp) | Faster response, but too high causes oscillation | I (Ki) | Removes steady-state error, but too high causes overshoot/instability | D (Kd) | Reduces overshoot and damps oscillation, but too high amplifies sensor noise",robotics;control-loops;lesson_passage,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=robotics&lesson=control-loops,lesson:robotics/control-loops,lesson_passage
596,Robotics,Robotics,Control Loops — PID control [57d20725],"A common tuning approach is to start with only P, increase it until the system oscillates, then back it off and add I to remove offset, and finally add D to smooth out overshoot. The next lesson turns this into a reusable C++ class.",robotics;control-loops;lesson_passage,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=robotics&lesson=control-loops,lesson:robotics/control-loops,lesson_passage
597,Robotics,Robotics,Implementing PID Control in C++ — Recap: the three PID terms [3f278f56],The Control Loops lesson introduces the three PID terms conceptually. Turning that into working code just means computing each term from the error every control cycle and adding them together.,robotics;pid-control-implementation-in-cpp;lesson_passage,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=robotics&lesson=pid-control-implementation-in-cpp,lesson:robotics/pid-control-implementation-in-cpp,lesson_passage
598,Robotics,Robotics,Implementing PID Control in C++ — A reusable PID class [9b93a340],"class PIDController {
public:
    PIDController(float kp, float ki, float kd)
        : Kp(kp), Ki(ki), Kd(kd), integral(0), lastError(0) {}

    float update(float setpoint, float measured, float dt) {
        float error = setpoint - measured;

        integral += error * dt;
        float derivative = (error - lastError) / dt;
        lastError = error;

        return (Kp * error) + (Ki * integral) + (Kd * derivative);
    }

private:
    float Kp, Ki, Kd;
    float integral;
    float lastError;
};",robotics;pid-control-implementation-in-cpp;code,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=robotics&lesson=pid-control-implementation-in-cpp,lesson:robotics/pid-control-implementation-in-cpp,code
599,Robotics,Robotics,Implementing PID Control in C++ — Using it to hold a motor at a target speed [284de415],"PIDController speedPID(2.0, 0.5, 0.1);
unsigned long lastPidTime = 0;

void loop() {
    unsigned long now = millis();
    float dt = (now - lastPidTime) / 1000.0;
    lastPidTime = now;

    float targetSpeed = 0.3;                // meters per second
    float currentSpeed = getWheelSpeed();   // from the Encoders & Speed Measurement lesson

    float output = speedPID.update(targetSpeed, currentSpeed, dt);
    int pwm = constrain((int)(output * 255), -255, 255);

    setMotor(LEFT_IN1, LEFT_IN2, LEFT_EN, pwm);
}",robotics;pid-control-implementation-in-cpp;code,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=robotics&lesson=pid-control-implementation-in-cpp,lesson:robotics/pid-control-implementation-in-cpp,code
600,Robotics,Robotics,Implementing PID Control in C++ — Integral windup [37dc1e50],"If the motor genuinely cannot reach the target speed (e.g. it is being commanded faster than the battery can drive it), the error never goes to zero, and the integral term keeps growing without bound -- called integral windup. When the target finally becomes reachable again, that oversized accumulated integral term causes a large overshoot before it can unwind. Clamping the integral term to a sensible range is a standard, simple fix:",robotics;pid-control-implementation-in-cpp;lesson_passage,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=robotics&lesson=pid-control-implementation-in-cpp,lesson:robotics/pid-control-implementation-in-cpp,lesson_passage
601,Robotics,Robotics,Implementing PID Control in C++ — Tuning: Ziegler-Nichols as a starting point [f022a9bd],"One common systematic tuning method: set Ki and Kd to zero, and increase Kp alone until the system just barely starts to oscillate continuously at a constant amplitude. Note the critical gain (K_u) at which this happens and the oscillation period (T_u), then use them to compute starting values for all three gains:",robotics;pid-control-implementation-in-cpp;lesson_passage,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=robotics&lesson=pid-control-implementation-in-cpp,lesson:robotics/pid-control-implementation-in-cpp,lesson_passage
602,Robotics,Robotics,Implementing PID Control in C++ — Tuning: Ziegler-Nichols as a starting point [61333096],Ziegler-Nichols starting-point tuning rules (classic PID) | Gain | Formula | Kp | 0.6 × K_u | Ki | 2 × Kp / T_u | Kd | Kp × T_u / 8,robotics;pid-control-implementation-in-cpp;lesson_passage,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=robotics&lesson=pid-control-implementation-in-cpp,lesson:robotics/pid-control-implementation-in-cpp,lesson_passage
603,Robotics,Robotics,Implementing PID Control in C++ — Tuning: Ziegler-Nichols as a starting point [b07dbe5e],"These values are only a starting point, not a final answer -- real tuning almost always needs further manual adjustment against the trade-offs described in the Control Loops lesson (faster response vs. overshoot vs. sensor noise sensitivity).",robotics;pid-control-implementation-in-cpp;lesson_passage,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=robotics&lesson=pid-control-implementation-in-cpp,lesson:robotics/pid-control-implementation-in-cpp,lesson_passage
604,Robotics,Robotics,Path Planning Basics — Waypoint following [4d1387d5],"The simplest form of path planning is a pre-defined list of (x, y) waypoints the robot should visit in order, using the odometry position estimate from the Kinematics & Odometry lesson to know how it is progressing:",robotics;path-planning-basics;lesson_passage,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=robotics&lesson=path-planning-basics,lesson:robotics/path-planning-basics,lesson_passage
605,Robotics,Robotics,Path Planning Basics — Waypoint following [3e482fee],"struct Waypoint { float x, y; };
Waypoint path[] = {{0.5, 0.0}, {0.5, 0.5}, {0.0, 0.5}, {0.0, 0.0}};
const int NUM_WAYPOINTS = 4;
int currentWaypoint = 0;

void driveToWaypoint(float robotX, float robotY, float robotHeading) {
    Waypoint target = path[currentWaypoint];
    float dx = target.x - robotX;
    float dy = target.y - robotY;
    float distance = sqrt(dx * dx + dy * dy);

    if (distance < 0.05) {   // close enough -- 5 cm tolerance
        currentWaypoint = (currentWaypoint + 1) % NUM_WAYPOINTS;
        return;
    }

    float targetHeading = atan2(dy, dx);
    float headingError = targetHeading - robotHeading;

    // drive forward while steering to reduce headingError, e.g. with the
    // same proportional-steering idea from the Line-Following lesson
}",robotics;path-planning-basics;code,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=robotics&lesson=path-planning-basics,lesson:robotics/path-planning-basics,code
606,Robotics,Robotics,Path Planning Basics — Grid-based planning [9ed0307b],"For an environment represented as a grid of free and occupied cells (e.g. built from a map, or from ultrasonic scans taken at many points), classic search algorithms like Breadth-First Search or A* can find a path from a start cell to a goal cell. A* is the most widely used in practice because it combines actual distance traveled so far with an estimated remaining distance (a heuristic, often straight-line distance to the goal) to search efficiently toward the goal instead of exploring in every direction equally.",robotics;path-planning-basics;lesson_passage,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=robotics&lesson=path-planning-basics,lesson:robotics/path-planning-basics,lesson_passage
607,Robotics,Robotics,Path Planning Basics — Why full path planning rarely runs directly on a small microcontroller [ccebe1ac],"A* and similar graph-search algorithms need memory proportional to the size of the map being searched -- workable on a computer, but often impractical on an 8-bit microcontroller with only a few kilobytes of SRAM. A common practical split is to run path planning on a more capable companion computer (or precompute the path offline) and send only the resulting waypoint list to the Arduino, which then just needs to execute the much simpler waypoint-following code above.",robotics;path-planning-basics;lesson_passage,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=robotics&lesson=path-planning-basics,lesson:robotics/path-planning-basics,lesson_passage
608,Robotics,Robotics,Localization & Dead Reckoning — Dead reckoning [2aa5f9e0],"Dead reckoning means estimating current position purely from known past position plus measured motion since then -- exactly what the odometry code in the Kinematics & Odometry lesson does. It requires no external reference at all, but every small error in wheel speed measurement, wheel slip, or numerical integration compounds over time, so the position estimate drifts further from the truth the longer the robot runs.",robotics;localization-and-dead-reckoning;lesson_passage,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=robotics&lesson=localization-and-dead-reckoning,lesson:robotics/localization-and-dead-reckoning,lesson_passage
609,Robotics,Robotics,Localization & Dead Reckoning — Absolute references reduce drift [3be1916f],"Common ways to correct dead-reckoning drift | Reference | How it helps | Known landmarks (walls, markers, beacons) | Detecting a known feature at a known map location lets the robot correct its estimated position to match | GPS (outdoors only) | Provides an absolute position fix, though with meters of error on cheap modules -- usually fused with, not used instead of, odometry | External cameras or beacons | Common in research and competition settings where infrastructure can be added to the environment",robotics;localization-and-dead-reckoning;lesson_passage,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=robotics&lesson=localization-and-dead-reckoning,lesson:robotics/localization-and-dead-reckoning,lesson_passage
610,Robotics,Robotics,Localization & Dead Reckoning — Sensor fusion [92a8dd4b],"Rather than trusting any single sensor completely, sensor fusion combines several imperfect sources -- odometry, IMU heading, and any absolute reference available -- weighting each by how much it is trusted, similar in spirit to the complementary filter from the IMU lesson but applied to full position estimation rather than just tilt angle. A Kalman filter (or its extensions for nonlinear systems) is the standard tool for this in more advanced robots, since it formally accounts for each sensor′s known noise characteristics rather than using a fixed blending weight.",robotics;localization-and-dead-reckoning;lesson_passage,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=robotics&lesson=localization-and-dead-reckoning,lesson:robotics/localization-and-dead-reckoning,lesson_passage
611,Robotics,Robotics,"Localization & Dead Reckoning — SLAM, briefly [b71ac87d]","Simultaneous Localization and Mapping (SLAM) tackles an even harder version of the problem: building a map of an unknown environment and localizing within it at the same time, using neither a pre-existing map nor external position references. SLAM is well beyond what a basic microcontroller-based robot typically attempts, but it is built from the same underlying ideas covered in this lesson -- sensor fusion and probabilistic position estimation -- run at much larger scale, usually on a full computer (or a Raspberry Pi-class companion board) rather than an 8-bit microcontroller.",robotics;localization-and-dead-reckoning;lesson_passage,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=robotics&lesson=localization-and-dead-reckoning,lesson:robotics/localization-and-dead-reckoning,lesson_passage
612,Robotics,Robotics,Robot Power Systems — Battery chemistries [21c4d447],"Common robot battery types | Chemistry | Nominal cell voltage | Notes | Alkaline (AA/AAA) | 1.5 V | Cheap and simple, but not rechargeable and voltage sags noticeably as they drain | NiMH | 1.2 V | Rechargeable, moderate energy density, tolerant of abuse | Li-ion / LiPo | 3.7 V | High energy density and steady voltage, but needs a proper charger and a protection circuit -- damaged or improperly charged cells present a real fire risk",robotics;robot-power-systems;lesson_passage,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=robotics&lesson=robot-power-systems,lesson:robotics/robot-power-systems,lesson_passage
613,Robotics,Robotics,Robot Power Systems — Powering motors and logic separately [de6f97a4],"Motors draw large, noisy, and variable current, especially at startup and under load -- directly powering an Arduino from the same supply rail as motors can cause the board to brown out and reset when a motor draws a sudden surge. Most robot designs use two separate supplies (or at least separate regulators): one for the motors and drivers, and one clean, regulated supply just for the microcontroller and low-power sensors, sharing only a common ground connection between them.",robotics;robot-power-systems;lesson_passage,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=robotics&lesson=robot-power-systems,lesson:robotics/robot-power-systems,lesson_passage
614,Robotics,Robotics,Robot Power Systems — Estimating current draw and run time [23599648],"float estimatedRuntimeHours(float batteryCapacityMah, float averageCurrentMa) {
    return batteryCapacityMah / averageCurrentMa;
}",robotics;robot-power-systems;code,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=robotics&lesson=robot-power-systems,lesson:robotics/robot-power-systems,code
615,Robotics,Robotics,Robot Power Systems — Estimating current draw and run time [15a032e4],"Battery capacity is rated in milliamp-hours (mAh) -- a 2000 mAh battery can theoretically supply 2000 mA for one hour, or 1000 mA for two hours, and so on, though real usable capacity is somewhat lower once battery efficiency and a sensible minimum-voltage cutoff are accounted for.",robotics;robot-power-systems;lesson_passage,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=robotics&lesson=robot-power-systems,lesson:robotics/robot-power-systems,lesson_passage
616,Robotics,Robotics,Robot Power Systems — Voltage regulation for robotics [1c784253],"As covered in the Electricity & Electronics course′s Power Supplies lesson, a linear regulator wastes the voltage difference as heat, while a switching regulator is far more efficient -- on a battery-powered robot, where every wasted milliamp shortens run time, a switching (""buck"") regulator is almost always the better choice for stepping a battery pack down to a logic-level supply voltage.",robotics;robot-power-systems;lesson_passage,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=robotics&lesson=robot-power-systems,lesson:robotics/robot-power-systems,lesson_passage
617,Robotics,Robotics,Wireless Control & Telemetry — Bluetooth serial control [8ada5a71],"A Bluetooth serial module (e.g. an HC-05 or HC-06) connects to the Arduino′s hardware or software serial port and appears, from the code′s point of view, exactly like a wired serial connection -- any code written against Serial works with it essentially unchanged.",robotics;wireless-control-and-telemetry;lesson_passage,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=robotics&lesson=wireless-control-and-telemetry,lesson:robotics/wireless-control-and-telemetry,lesson_passage
618,Robotics,Robotics,Wireless Control & Telemetry — Bluetooth serial control [0c32f3e5],"#include <SoftwareSerial.h>

SoftwareSerial bluetooth(10, 11);   // RX, TX

void setup() {
    Serial.begin(9600);
    bluetooth.begin(9600);
}

void loop() {
    if (bluetooth.available()) {
        char command = bluetooth.read();

        switch (command) {
            case 'F': setMotor(LEFT_IN1, LEFT_IN2, LEFT_EN, 200);
                        setMotor(RIGHT_IN1, RIGHT_IN2, RIGHT_EN, 200);
                        break;
            case 'S': setMotor(LEFT_IN1, LEFT_IN2, LEFT_EN, 0);
                        setMotor(RIGHT_IN1, RIGHT_IN2, RIGHT_EN, 0);
                        break;
        }
    }
}",robotics;wireless-control-and-telemetry;code,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=robotics&lesson=wireless-control-and-telemetry,lesson:robotics/wireless-control-and-telemetry,code
619,Robotics,Robotics,Wireless Control & Telemetry — RF modules (nRF24L01) [44da742c],"For longer range or more reliable two-way communication than Bluetooth serial, many robots use nRF24L01 RF modules with the popular RF24 library:",robotics;wireless-control-and-telemetry;lesson_passage,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=robotics&lesson=wireless-control-and-telemetry,lesson:robotics/wireless-control-and-telemetry,lesson_passage
