621,Robotics,Robotics,Wireless Control & Telemetry — Sending telemetry back [7ad76ee5],"Communication does not need to be one-way -- sending sensor readings, battery voltage, or the current PID error back to a receiving computer or phone app over the same link makes debugging and tuning dramatically easier than relying only on onboard LEDs or guessing from watching the robot′s behavior.",robotics;wireless-control-and-telemetry;lesson_passage,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=robotics&lesson=wireless-control-and-telemetry,lesson:robotics/wireless-control-and-telemetry,lesson_passage
622,Robotics,Robotics,Wireless Control & Telemetry — A safety consideration: loss of signal [18b368cd],"Any wirelessly controlled robot needs a defined behavior for when the connection drops -- continuing to execute the last received command (e.g. ""drive forward"") is a common and serious safety hazard. A simple watchdog pattern (stop the motors if no valid command has been received recently) is standard practice:",robotics;wireless-control-and-telemetry;lesson_passage,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=robotics&lesson=wireless-control-and-telemetry,lesson:robotics/wireless-control-and-telemetry,lesson_passage
623,Robotics,Robotics,Wireless Control & Telemetry — A safety consideration: loss of signal [7a375086],"unsigned long lastCommandTime = 0;
