624,Robotics,Robotics,Mechanical Design Considerations — Center of mass and stability [08751af7],"A robot′s center of mass needs to stay within its support base (the area enclosed by its wheels or feet touching the ground) or it will tip over -- a taller robot, or one with heavy components mounted high up (like a raised arm carrying a payload), is more prone to tipping during acceleration, braking, or turning than a low, wide one.",robotics;mechanical-design-considerations;lesson_passage,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=robotics&lesson=mechanical-design-considerations,lesson:robotics/mechanical-design-considerations,lesson_passage
625,Robotics,Robotics,Mechanical Design Considerations — Gear ratios and torque [36d3581c],"A gear reduction trades output speed for output torque, in direct proportion: an ideal gearbox conserves power, so if a gear ratio reduces speed by a factor of N, it increases torque by that same factor of N.",robotics;mechanical-design-considerations;lesson_passage,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=robotics&lesson=mechanical-design-considerations,lesson:robotics/mechanical-design-considerations,lesson_passage
626,Robotics,Robotics,Mechanical Design Considerations — Gear ratios and torque [142e2a4d],"Output torque = Motor torque × Gear ratio
Output speed  = Motor speed / Gear ratio",robotics;mechanical-design-considerations;code,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=robotics&lesson=mechanical-design-considerations,lesson:robotics/mechanical-design-considerations,code
627,Robotics,Robotics,Mechanical Design Considerations — Gear ratios and torque [37341ab4],"Most small DC motors spin very fast (thousands of RPM) but produce very little torque on their own -- which is why almost every geared hobby motor used in robotics already has a built-in gearbox (often labeled, e.g., ""1:48"") reducing that raw motor speed down to a useful output speed with usable torque.",robotics;mechanical-design-considerations;lesson_passage,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=robotics&lesson=mechanical-design-considerations,lesson:robotics/mechanical-design-considerations,lesson_passage
628,Robotics,Robotics,Mechanical Design Considerations — Estimating whether a motor is strong enough [d89835c0],"float requiredTorqueNm(float robotMassKg, float wheelRadiusM, float safetyFactor) {
    const float g = 9.81;
    // Simplified: torque needed to overcome the weight-related force at the wheel,
    // ignoring friction/incline for a first estimate, with a safety margin applied.
    return robotMassKg * g * wheelRadiusM * safetyFactor;
}",robotics;mechanical-design-considerations;code,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=robotics&lesson=mechanical-design-considerations,lesson:robotics/mechanical-design-considerations,code
629,Robotics,Robotics,Mechanical Design Considerations — Estimating whether a motor is strong enough [25993f1a],"This is a deliberately simplified starting estimate, not a substitute for a full analysis including friction, acceleration, and any incline the robot needs to climb -- but it is a reasonable first sanity check before committing to a specific motor and gear ratio.",robotics;mechanical-design-considerations;lesson_passage,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=robotics&lesson=mechanical-design-considerations,lesson:robotics/mechanical-design-considerations,lesson_passage
630,Robotics,Robotics,Mechanical Design Considerations — Chassis material and wiring practicalities [a5265d2c],"Common hobby chassis materials | Material | Trade-offs | Acrylic | Easy to laser-cut, rigid, but brittle -- cracks under sharp impact | 3D-printed plastic (PLA/PETG) | Very flexible design freedom, moderate strength, easy to iterate | Aluminum | Strong and lightweight, but needs more specialized tools to fabricate or modify",robotics;mechanical-design-considerations;lesson_passage,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=robotics&lesson=mechanical-design-considerations,lesson:robotics/mechanical-design-considerations,lesson_passage
631,Robotics,Robotics,Mechanical Design Considerations — Chassis material and wiring practicalities [df0b6d9c],"Beyond the structure itself, leaving slack in wiring near any moving joint, securing the battery so it cannot shift the center of mass while driving, and keeping motor wiring away from sensitive analog sensor wiring (motor noise can corrupt nearby analog readings) are all practical details that matter as much as the headline material choice.",robotics;mechanical-design-considerations;lesson_passage,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=robotics&lesson=mechanical-design-considerations,lesson:robotics/mechanical-design-considerations,lesson_passage
632,Robotics,Robotics,Finite State Machines for Robot Behavior — Why a state machine [074828ba],"A robot with more than one or two behaviors (patrol, avoid, return-to-base, low-battery-return) quickly becomes a tangle of interacting if statements if written without structure. A finite state machine -- the same concept covered formally in the Digital Logic Design course′s Finite State Machines lesson -- keeps exactly one behavior active at a time and makes the transitions between behaviors explicit and easy to reason about.",robotics;finite-state-machines-for-robot-behavior;lesson_passage,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=robotics&lesson=finite-state-machines-for-robot-behavior,lesson:robotics/finite-state-machines-for-robot-behavior,lesson_passage
633,Robotics,Robotics,Finite State Machines for Robot Behavior — Implementing a behavior FSM in C++ [8f5d9632],"enum RobotState { PATROL, AVOID, RETURN_HOME, STOPPED };
RobotState currentState = PATROL;

void updateStateMachine() {
    float distance = readDistanceCm();
    float batteryVoltage = readBatteryVoltage();

    switch (currentState) {
        case PATROL:
            if (distance > 0 && distance < 20) {
                currentState = AVOID;
            } else if (batteryVoltage < 6.5) {
                currentState = RETURN_HOME;
            }
            break;

        case AVOID:
            performAvoidanceManeuver();
            if (distance > 30 || distance < 0) {
                currentState = PATROL;
            }
            break;

        case RETURN_HOME:
            driveTowardHome();
            if (isAtHome()) {
                currentState = STOPPED;
            }
            break;

        case STOPPED:
            setMotor(LEFT_IN1, LEFT_IN2, LEFT_EN, 0);
            setMotor(RIGHT_IN1, RIGHT_IN2, RIGHT_EN, 0);
            break;
    }
}

void loop() {
    updateStateMachine();
}",robotics;finite-state-machines-for-robot-behavior;code,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=robotics&lesson=finite-state-machines-for-robot-behavior,lesson:robotics/finite-state-machines-for-robot-behavior,code
634,Robotics,Robotics,Finite State Machines for Robot Behavior — Implementing a behavior FSM in C++ [ccfb2196],"Notice this is exactly the Moore/Mealy structure from the Digital Logic Design course, translated directly into software: a variable holding the current state, a block of logic deciding the next state from the current state and current inputs, and per-state output behavior -- just running once per loop() iteration instead of once per hardware clock edge.",robotics;finite-state-machines-for-robot-behavior;lesson_passage,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=robotics&lesson=finite-state-machines-for-robot-behavior,lesson:robotics/finite-state-machines-for-robot-behavior,lesson_passage
635,Robotics,Robotics,"Finite State Machines for Robot Behavior — Adding hysteresis to avoid state ""chattering"" [4ef5eee8]","Using slightly different thresholds for entering versus leaving a state (here, entering AVOID under 20 cm but only leaving it above 30 cm) prevents the robot from rapidly flickering back and forth between two states when a sensor reading sits right at a single borderline threshold.",robotics;finite-state-machines-for-robot-behavior;lesson_passage,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=robotics&lesson=finite-state-machines-for-robot-behavior,lesson:robotics/finite-state-machines-for-robot-behavior,lesson_passage
636,Robotics,Robotics,"Safety, Testing & Debugging — Hardware emergency stop [e6cb85ef]","A physical emergency-stop (E-stop) switch that cuts power to the motors directly -- independent of whatever the microcontroller′s code is currently doing -- is essential on anything larger or faster than a small desktop robot. Relying on software alone to stop a runaway robot is not a safe design, since it assumes the very code that may be misbehaving will still correctly detect the problem and respond.",robotics;safety-testing-and-debugging-robots;lesson_passage,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=robotics&lesson=safety-testing-and-debugging-robots,lesson:robotics/safety-testing-and-debugging-robots,lesson_passage
637,Robotics,Robotics,"Safety, Testing & Debugging — Testing incrementally [45c1b3c1]","Test each subsystem in isolation before combining them -- confirm a motor spins the right direction and speed before adding a sensor loop on top of it, confirm a sensor reads sensible values before wiring it into an avoidance behavior.",robotics;safety-testing-and-debugging-robots;lesson_passage,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=robotics&lesson=safety-testing-and-debugging-robots,lesson:robotics/safety-testing-and-debugging-robots,lesson_passage
638,Robotics,Robotics,"Safety, Testing & Debugging — Testing incrementally [3cff40f3]","Run a robot on blocks (wheels off the ground) the first time any new motor code is tried, so a bug cannot send it driving into something before it is caught.",robotics;safety-testing-and-debugging-robots;lesson_passage,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=robotics&lesson=safety-testing-and-debugging-robots,lesson:robotics/safety-testing-and-debugging-robots,lesson_passage
639,Robotics,Robotics,"Safety, Testing & Debugging — Testing incrementally [0cb471e9]","Add a startup delay or explicit ""arm"" signal before motors can move, giving time to react if something is about to go wrong the moment power is applied.",robotics;safety-testing-and-debugging-robots;lesson_passage,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=robotics&lesson=safety-testing-and-debugging-robots,lesson:robotics/safety-testing-and-debugging-robots,lesson_passage
