endmodule",verilog;verilog-syntax-basics;code,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=verilog&lesson=verilog-syntax-basics,lesson:verilog/verilog-syntax-basics,code
645,Digital Logic Design,Verilog,Verilog Syntax Basics — wire vs. reg [4b41c65e],wire vs. reg | Type | Used for | Assigned with | wire | Combinational connections between components | assign statements | reg | Values that hold state inside procedural blocks | always blocks,verilog;verilog-syntax-basics;lesson_passage,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=verilog&lesson=verilog-syntax-basics,lesson:verilog/verilog-syntax-basics,lesson_passage
646,Digital Logic Design,Verilog,Verilog Syntax Basics — wire vs. reg [4f2c8d10],"Note that reg does not necessarily mean a physical register -- it just means ""assigned inside an always block"". Whether real flip-flops are created depends on how the always block is written.",verilog;verilog-syntax-basics;lesson_passage,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=verilog&lesson=verilog-syntax-basics,lesson:verilog/verilog-syntax-basics,lesson_passage
647,Digital Logic Design,Verilog,Combinational Logic in Verilog — Continuous assignment [eb988f2b],Combinational logic (logic with no memory) is usually written with assign statements describing what a wire is always equal to.,verilog;combinational-logic-in-verilog;lesson_passage,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=verilog&lesson=combinational-logic-in-verilog,lesson:verilog/combinational-logic-in-verilog,lesson_passage
648,Digital Logic Design,Verilog,Combinational Logic in Verilog — Continuous assignment [d4041170],"module mux2to1 (
