endmodule",verilog;combinational-logic-in-verilog;code,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=verilog&lesson=combinational-logic-in-verilog,lesson:verilog/combinational-logic-in-verilog,code
649,Digital Logic Design,Verilog,Combinational Logic in Verilog — Continuous assignment [65089a95],"This describes a 2-to-1 multiplexer: when sel is 0, output y follows input a; when sel is 1, it follows b.",verilog;combinational-logic-in-verilog;lesson_passage,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=verilog&lesson=combinational-logic-in-verilog,lesson:verilog/combinational-logic-in-verilog,lesson_passage
650,Digital Logic Design,Verilog,Combinational Logic in Verilog — Continuous assignment [111934af],2-to-1 multiplexer truth table | sel | a | b | y | 0 | 0 | X | 0 | 0 | 1 | X | 1 | 1 | X | 0 | 0 | 1 | X | 1 | 1,verilog;combinational-logic-in-verilog;lesson_passage,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=verilog&lesson=combinational-logic-in-verilog,lesson:verilog/combinational-logic-in-verilog,lesson_passage
651,Digital Logic Design,Verilog,Combinational Logic in Verilog — Continuous assignment [b370e1ac],"(X means ""does not matter"" -- the output does not depend on that input for that row.)",verilog;combinational-logic-in-verilog;lesson_passage,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=verilog&lesson=combinational-logic-in-verilog,lesson:verilog/combinational-logic-in-verilog,lesson_passage
652,Digital Logic Design,Verilog,Always Blocks & Sequential Logic — Describing a clocked flip-flop [eed0ee51],"Sequential logic depends on a clock edge, described in Verilog with an always @(posedge clk) block.",verilog;always-blocks-sequential-logic;lesson_passage,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=verilog&lesson=always-blocks-sequential-logic,lesson:verilog/always-blocks-sequential-logic,lesson_passage
653,Digital Logic Design,Verilog,Always Blocks & Sequential Logic — Describing a clocked flip-flop [274227f1],"module d_flip_flop (
    input  clk,
    input  reset,
    input  d,
    output reg q
);
    always @(posedge clk or posedge reset) begin
        if (reset)
            q <= 1'b0;
        else
            q <= d;
    end
endmodule",verilog;always-blocks-sequential-logic;code,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=verilog&lesson=always-blocks-sequential-logic,lesson:verilog/always-blocks-sequential-logic,code
654,Digital Logic Design,Verilog,Always Blocks & Sequential Logic — Describing a clocked flip-flop [d72ae5c7],"This matches the D flip-flop truth table from the Digital Logic Design course: on every rising clock edge, q takes on the value of d (unless reset is asserted, which forces q to 0).",verilog;always-blocks-sequential-logic;lesson_passage,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=verilog&lesson=always-blocks-sequential-logic,lesson:verilog/always-blocks-sequential-logic,lesson_passage
655,Digital Logic Design,Verilog,Always Blocks & Sequential Logic — Blocking (=) vs. non-blocking (<=) assignment [f440aa74],"Blocking vs. non-blocking assignment | Operator | Executes | Use inside | = (blocking) | Immediately, in order, like normal software statements | always @(*) blocks for combinational logic | <= (non-blocking) | All right-hand sides evaluated first, then all assignments happen together | always @(posedge clk) blocks for sequential logic",verilog;always-blocks-sequential-logic;lesson_passage,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=verilog&lesson=always-blocks-sequential-logic,lesson:verilog/always-blocks-sequential-logic,lesson_passage
656,Digital Logic Design,Verilog,Always Blocks & Sequential Logic — Blocking (=) vs. non-blocking (<=) assignment [d58007f8],"Mixing them up is one of the most common Verilog bugs -- as a rule of thumb, use non-blocking (<=) for clocked logic and blocking (=) for combinational logic.",verilog;always-blocks-sequential-logic;lesson_passage,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=verilog&lesson=always-blocks-sequential-logic,lesson:verilog/always-blocks-sequential-logic,lesson_passage
657,Digital Logic Design,Verilog,Half & Full Adders in Verilog — Half adder: dataflow style [958b76bf],"The half adder from the Digital Logic Design course (Sum = A XOR B, Cout = A AND B) translates directly into one assign statement per output.",verilog;verilog-half-full-adders;lesson_passage,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=verilog&lesson=verilog-half-full-adders,lesson:verilog/verilog-half-full-adders,lesson_passage
658,Digital Logic Design,Verilog,Half & Full Adders in Verilog — Half adder: dataflow style [0cb5674f],"module half_adder (
    input  a,
    input  b,
    output sum,
    output cout
);
    assign sum  = a ^ b;
    assign cout = a & b;
endmodule",verilog;verilog-half-full-adders;code,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=verilog&lesson=verilog-half-full-adders,lesson:verilog/verilog-half-full-adders,code
659,Digital Logic Design,Verilog,Half & Full Adders in Verilog — Full adder: dataflow style [a0898944],"The full adder equations (Sum = A ⊕ B ⊕ Cin, Cout = AB + ACin + BCin) map just as directly:",verilog;verilog-half-full-adders;lesson_passage,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=verilog&lesson=verilog-half-full-adders,lesson:verilog/verilog-half-full-adders,lesson_passage
660,Digital Logic Design,Verilog,Half & Full Adders in Verilog — Full adder: dataflow style [885aab48],"module full_adder (
    input  a,
    input  b,
    input  cin,
    output sum,
    output cout
);
    assign sum  = a ^ b ^ cin;
    assign cout = (a & b) | (a & cin) | (b & cin);
endmodule",verilog;verilog-half-full-adders;code,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=verilog&lesson=verilog-half-full-adders,lesson:verilog/verilog-half-full-adders,code
661,Digital Logic Design,Verilog,"Half & Full Adders in Verilog — Full adder: structural style, built from two half adders [9af42896]",Verilog also lets a module be built purely by wiring up instances of other modules -- exactly like the DLD lesson describes constructing a full adder from two half adders.,verilog;verilog-half-full-adders;lesson_passage,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=verilog&lesson=verilog-half-full-adders,lesson:verilog/verilog-half-full-adders,lesson_passage
662,Digital Logic Design,Verilog,"Half & Full Adders in Verilog — Full adder: structural style, built from two half adders [c7595c02]","module full_adder_structural (
    input  a,
    input  b,
    input  cin,
    output sum,
    output cout
);
    wire s1, c1, c2;

    half_adder ha1 (.a(a),  .b(b),   .sum(s1),  .cout(c1));
    half_adder ha2 (.a(s1), .b(cin), .sum(sum), .cout(c2));

    assign cout = c1 | c2;
endmodule",verilog;verilog-half-full-adders;code,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=verilog&lesson=verilog-half-full-adders,lesson:verilog/verilog-half-full-adders,code
663,Digital Logic Design,Verilog,"Half & Full Adders in Verilog — Full adder: structural style, built from two half adders [622bec2f]","The .port_name(signal) syntax is called named port connection -- it wires a signal to a specific port of the instantiated module regardless of the order the ports were declared in, which makes the connections self-documenting and less error-prone than positional connection.",verilog;verilog-half-full-adders;lesson_passage,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=verilog&lesson=verilog-half-full-adders,lesson:verilog/verilog-half-full-adders,lesson_passage
664,Digital Logic Design,Verilog,Decoders & Encoders in Verilog — 2-to-4 decoder [ccafac8b],"A decoder is naturally described with one assign per output line, each one a single minterm, exactly matching the DLD truth table.",verilog;verilog-decoders-encoders;lesson_passage,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=verilog&lesson=verilog-decoders-encoders,lesson:verilog/verilog-decoders-encoders,lesson_passage
665,Digital Logic Design,Verilog,Decoders & Encoders in Verilog — 2-to-4 decoder [b9f9f1b8],"module decoder_2to4 (
    input  [1:0] a,
    output       d0,
    output       d1,
    output       d2,
    output       d3
);
    assign d0 = (~a[1]) & (~a[0]);
    assign d1 = (~a[1]) &   a[0];
    assign d2 =   a[1]  & (~a[0]);
    assign d3 =   a[1]  &   a[0];
endmodule",verilog;verilog-decoders-encoders;code,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=verilog&lesson=verilog-decoders-encoders,lesson:verilog/verilog-decoders-encoders,code
666,Digital Logic Design,Verilog,Decoders & Encoders in Verilog — A cleaner decoder using a bus and a case statement [24038d89],"Rather than one equation per output, real designs usually pack the outputs into a single bus and let a case statement inside an always block pick the pattern -- easier to read and easier to extend to more outputs.",verilog;verilog-decoders-encoders;lesson_passage,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=verilog&lesson=verilog-decoders-encoders,lesson:verilog/verilog-decoders-encoders,lesson_passage
667,Digital Logic Design,Verilog,Decoders & Encoders in Verilog — A cleaner decoder using a bus and a case statement [9f887be4],"module decoder_2to4_case (
    input  [1:0]      a,
    output reg [3:0]  d
);
    always @(*) begin
        case (a)
            2'b00: d = 4'b0001;
            2'b01: d = 4'b0010;
            2'b10: d = 4'b0100;
            2'b11: d = 4'b1000;
        endcase
    end
endmodule",verilog;verilog-decoders-encoders;code,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=verilog&lesson=verilog-decoders-encoders,lesson:verilog/verilog-decoders-encoders,code
668,Digital Logic Design,Verilog,Decoders & Encoders in Verilog — A cleaner decoder using a bus and a case statement [610f4cc5],"always @(*) means ""re-evaluate this block whenever any signal it reads changes"" -- the standard way to describe combinational logic with a case or if statement instead of a plain assign.",verilog;verilog-decoders-encoders;lesson_passage,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=verilog&lesson=verilog-decoders-encoders,lesson:verilog/verilog-decoders-encoders,lesson_passage
669,Digital Logic Design,Verilog,Decoders & Encoders in Verilog — 4-to-2 encoder [419443e8],"module encoder_4to2 (
    input  [3:0]      d,
    output reg [1:0]  a
);
    always @(*) begin
        case (d)
            4'b0001: a = 2'b00;
            4'b0010: a = 2'b01;
            4'b0100: a = 2'b10;
            4'b1000: a = 2'b11;
            default:  a = 2'b00;
        endcase
    end
endmodule",verilog;verilog-decoders-encoders;code,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=verilog&lesson=verilog-decoders-encoders,lesson:verilog/verilog-decoders-encoders,code
670,Digital Logic Design,Verilog,Decoders & Encoders in Verilog — 4-to-2 encoder [c520a46a],"The default case matters here: without it, an unexpected input pattern (e.g. two bits set at once) would leave a unassigned in simulation, which can create an accidental latch instead of the combinational logic that was intended.",verilog;verilog-decoders-encoders;lesson_passage,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=verilog&lesson=verilog-decoders-encoders,lesson:verilog/verilog-decoders-encoders,lesson_passage
671,Digital Logic Design,Verilog,Comparators & Parity Circuits in Verilog — Multi-bit comparators [c7260c83],"Verilog′s built-in comparison operators (==, >, <) do exactly what the DLD comparator lesson built from gates, but in one line, for any bus width.",verilog;verilog-comparators-parity;lesson_passage,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=verilog&lesson=verilog-comparators-parity,lesson:verilog/verilog-comparators-parity,lesson_passage
672,Digital Logic Design,Verilog,Comparators & Parity Circuits in Verilog — Multi-bit comparators [00bb2b61],"module comparator #(parameter WIDTH = 4) (
    input  [WIDTH-1:0] a,
    input  [WIDTH-1:0] b,
    output gt,
    output eq,
    output lt
);
    assign gt = (a >  b);
    assign eq = (a == b);
    assign lt = (a <  b);
endmodule",verilog;verilog-comparators-parity;code,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=verilog&lesson=verilog-comparators-parity,lesson:verilog/verilog-comparators-parity,code
673,Digital Logic Design,Verilog,Comparators & Parity Circuits in Verilog — Multi-bit comparators [60ebe73f],"The #(parameter WIDTH = 4) syntax makes this comparator reusable at any width -- instantiate it with comparator #(.WIDTH(8)) cmp8 (...) for an 8-bit version, with no changes to the module body.",verilog;verilog-comparators-parity;lesson_passage,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=verilog&lesson=verilog-comparators-parity,lesson:verilog/verilog-comparators-parity,lesson_passage
674,Digital Logic Design,Verilog,Comparators & Parity Circuits in Verilog — Parity generator and checker [3e69d18d],"The reduction XOR operator (a single ^ applied to a whole bus) XORs every bit of the bus together, which is exactly the XOR tree the DLD parity lesson describes.",verilog;verilog-comparators-parity;lesson_passage,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=verilog&lesson=verilog-comparators-parity,lesson:verilog/verilog-comparators-parity,lesson_passage
675,Digital Logic Design,Verilog,Comparators & Parity Circuits in Verilog — Parity generator and checker [835d02d9],"module parity_gen #(parameter WIDTH = 8) (
    input  [WIDTH-1:0] data,
    output             even_parity_bit
);
    assign even_parity_bit = ^data;   // XOR of every bit in data
endmodule

module parity_check #(parameter WIDTH = 9) (
    input  [WIDTH-1:0] received,      // data bits + parity bit together
    output             error
);
    assign error = ^received;         // 0 = no error, 1 = error detected
endmodule",verilog;verilog-comparators-parity;code,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=verilog&lesson=verilog-comparators-parity,lesson:verilog/verilog-comparators-parity,code
676,Digital Logic Design,Verilog,Multiplexers & Demultiplexers in Verilog — 2-to-1 and 4-to-1 multiplexers [6ace4e82],A small mux is often clearest as a ternary expression; the DLD 4-to-1 mux truth table maps directly onto a case statement once there are more than two choices.,verilog;verilog-multiplexers-demultiplexers;lesson_passage,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=verilog&lesson=verilog-multiplexers-demultiplexers,lesson:verilog/verilog-multiplexers-demultiplexers,lesson_passage
677,Digital Logic Design,Verilog,Multiplexers & Demultiplexers in Verilog — 2-to-1 and 4-to-1 multiplexers [d5ce1e69],"module mux2to1 (
    input  sel,
    input  a,
    input  b,
    output y
);
    assign y = sel ? b : a;
endmodule

module mux4to1 (
    input  [1:0]  sel,
    input  [3:0]  d,
    output reg    y
);
    always @(*) begin
        case (sel)
            2'b00: y = d[0];
            2'b01: y = d[1];
            2'b10: y = d[2];
            2'b11: y = d[3];
        endcase
    end
endmodule",verilog;verilog-multiplexers-demultiplexers;code,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=verilog&lesson=verilog-multiplexers-demultiplexers,lesson:verilog/verilog-multiplexers-demultiplexers,code
678,Digital Logic Design,Verilog,Multiplexers & Demultiplexers in Verilog — Parameterized N-to-1 multiplexer [afeed36f],A common plain-Verilog pattern packs all the data inputs into one wide bus and uses the select value to slice out the right piece:,verilog;verilog-multiplexers-demultiplexers;lesson_passage,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=verilog&lesson=verilog-multiplexers-demultiplexers,lesson:verilog/verilog-multiplexers-demultiplexers,lesson_passage
679,Digital Logic Design,Verilog,Multiplexers & Demultiplexers in Verilog — Parameterized N-to-1 multiplexer [f6def212],"module mux_n #(parameter WIDTH = 4, parameter N = 8) (
    input  [$clog2(N)-1:0] sel,
    input  [N*WIDTH-1:0]   data_in,   // N inputs of WIDTH bits, concatenated
    output [WIDTH-1:0]     y
);
    assign y = data_in[sel*WIDTH +: WIDTH];
endmodule",verilog;verilog-multiplexers-demultiplexers;code,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=verilog&lesson=verilog-multiplexers-demultiplexers,lesson:verilog/verilog-multiplexers-demultiplexers,code
680,Digital Logic Design,Verilog,Multiplexers & Demultiplexers in Verilog — Parameterized N-to-1 multiplexer [1b68d2aa],"+: is an indexed part-select: data_in[sel*WIDTH +: WIDTH] means ""WIDTH bits, starting at bit position sel*WIDTH"" -- a variable-position slice that an ordinary [hi:lo] range cannot express, since sel is not a constant.",verilog;verilog-multiplexers-demultiplexers;lesson_passage,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=verilog&lesson=verilog-multiplexers-demultiplexers,lesson:verilog/verilog-multiplexers-demultiplexers,lesson_passage
681,Digital Logic Design,Verilog,Multiplexers & Demultiplexers in Verilog — 1-to-4 demultiplexer [150b9c8b],"module demux1to4 (
    input  [1:0]      sel,
    input             d,
    output reg [3:0]  y
);
    always @(*) begin
        y = 4'b0000;
        y[sel] = d;
    end
endmodule",verilog;verilog-multiplexers-demultiplexers;code,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=verilog&lesson=verilog-multiplexers-demultiplexers,lesson:verilog/verilog-multiplexers-demultiplexers,code
682,Digital Logic Design,Verilog,Multiplexers & Demultiplexers in Verilog — 1-to-4 demultiplexer [fd6a25b1],"Initializing y to all zeros before the conditional assignment is a common Verilog idiom: it guarantees every output bit has a defined value in every branch, which avoids accidentally inferring a latch for the bits that are not selected.",verilog;verilog-multiplexers-demultiplexers;lesson_passage,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=verilog&lesson=verilog-multiplexers-demultiplexers,lesson:verilog/verilog-multiplexers-demultiplexers,lesson_passage
683,Digital Logic Design,Verilog,Ripple-Carry Adders & a Simple ALU — Ripple-carry adder from a generate loop [3bea3452],Instantiating N full adders by hand for a wide adder is tedious and error-prone. A generate for loop with a genvar lets Verilog create the instances for you at compile time.,verilog;verilog-ripple-carry-adder-alu;lesson_passage,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=verilog&lesson=verilog-ripple-carry-adder-alu,lesson:verilog/verilog-ripple-carry-adder-alu,lesson_passage
684,Digital Logic Design,Verilog,Ripple-Carry Adders & a Simple ALU — Ripple-carry adder from a generate loop [1eb83c4d],"module ripple_adder #(parameter WIDTH = 4) (
    input  [WIDTH-1:0] a,
    input  [WIDTH-1:0] b,
    input              cin,
    output [WIDTH-1:0] sum,
    output             cout
);
    wire [WIDTH:0] carry;
    assign carry[0] = cin;
    assign cout = carry[WIDTH];

    genvar i;
    generate
        for (i = 0; i < WIDTH; i = i + 1) begin : fa_stage
            full_adder fa (
                .a    (a[i]),
                .b    (b[i]),
                .cin  (carry[i]),
                .sum  (sum[i]),
                .cout (carry[i+1])
            );
        end
    endgenerate
endmodule",verilog;verilog-ripple-carry-adder-alu;code,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=verilog&lesson=verilog-ripple-carry-adder-alu,lesson:verilog/verilog-ripple-carry-adder-alu,code
685,Digital Logic Design,Verilog,Ripple-Carry Adders & a Simple ALU — Ripple-carry adder from a generate loop [c87676e5],"This is structurally identical to the ripple-carry adder from the Digital Logic Design course: each stage′s carry-out wire feeds the next stage′s carry-in, and the delay still ripples from the least significant bit to the most significant bit.",verilog;verilog-ripple-carry-adder-alu;lesson_passage,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=verilog&lesson=verilog-ripple-carry-adder-alu,lesson:verilog/verilog-ripple-carry-adder-alu,lesson_passage
686,Digital Logic Design,Verilog,Ripple-Carry Adders & a Simple ALU — A simple mode-controlled adder/subtractor [db09a9cf],"Following the same adder/subtractor trick from the DLD course -- XOR every B bit with a mode line M, and feed M into the carry-in -- turns the same adder into a subtractor:",verilog;verilog-ripple-carry-adder-alu;lesson_passage,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=verilog&lesson=verilog-ripple-carry-adder-alu,lesson:verilog/verilog-ripple-carry-adder-alu,lesson_passage
687,Digital Logic Design,Verilog,Ripple-Carry Adders & a Simple ALU — A simple mode-controlled adder/subtractor [f15416f0],"module addsub #(parameter WIDTH = 4) (
    input  [WIDTH-1:0] a,
    input  [WIDTH-1:0] b,
    input              mode,     // 0 = add, 1 = subtract (a - b)
    output [WIDTH-1:0] result,
    output             cout
);
    wire [WIDTH-1:0] b_xored = b ^ {WIDTH{mode}};

    ripple_adder #(.WIDTH(WIDTH)) core (
        .a    (a),
        .b    (b_xored),
        .cin  (mode),
        .sum  (result),
        .cout (cout)
    );
endmodule",verilog;verilog-ripple-carry-adder-alu;code,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=verilog&lesson=verilog-ripple-carry-adder-alu,lesson:verilog/verilog-ripple-carry-adder-alu,code
688,Digital Logic Design,Verilog,Ripple-Carry Adders & a Simple ALU — A simple mode-controlled adder/subtractor [0889a71c],"{WIDTH{mode}} is a replication operator: it repeats the single bit mode WIDTH times, producing a bus of all 0s (mode = 0, B passes through unchanged) or all 1s (mode = 1, B is bitwise complemented before adding, matching two′s complement negation).",verilog;verilog-ripple-carry-adder-alu;lesson_passage,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=verilog&lesson=verilog-ripple-carry-adder-alu,lesson:verilog/verilog-ripple-carry-adder-alu,lesson_passage
689,Digital Logic Design,Verilog,Latches & Flip-Flop Types in Verilog — Why you rarely write a latch on purpose [5c7b6eb7],"An SR or D latch can be described behaviorally, but most synthesis tools flag inferred latches as a likely bug, since an incompletely specified combinational always block infers one by accident. Real designs almost always use the clocked, edge-triggered styles below instead.",verilog;verilog-latches-flip-flop-types;lesson_passage,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=verilog&lesson=verilog-latches-flip-flop-types,lesson:verilog/verilog-latches-flip-flop-types,lesson_passage
690,Digital Logic Design,Verilog,Latches & Flip-Flop Types in Verilog — Why you rarely write a latch on purpose [7d2d8507],"// A gated D latch -- shown for completeness, rarely used directly
module d_latch (
    input  en,
    input  d,
    output reg q
);
    always @(en or d)
        if (en)
            q = d;
        // no else: when en = 0, q intentionally holds its value
        // (this is what makes it a latch, and what a linter will flag)
endmodule",verilog;verilog-latches-flip-flop-types;code,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=verilog&lesson=verilog-latches-flip-flop-types,lesson:verilog/verilog-latches-flip-flop-types,code
691,Digital Logic Design,Verilog,Latches & Flip-Flop Types in Verilog — JK flip-flop [6b6ad480],The DLD course gives the JK characteristic equation Q(next) = JQ′ + K′Q. Implemented directly:,verilog;verilog-latches-flip-flop-types;lesson_passage,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=verilog&lesson=verilog-latches-flip-flop-types,lesson:verilog/verilog-latches-flip-flop-types,lesson_passage
692,Digital Logic Design,Verilog,Latches & Flip-Flop Types in Verilog — JK flip-flop [b0f406e9],"module jk_flip_flop (
    input  clk,
    input  reset,
    input  j,
    input  k,
    output reg q
);
    always @(posedge clk or posedge reset) begin
        if (reset)
            q <= 1'b0;
        else
            case ({j, k})
                2'b00: q <= q;       // hold
                2'b01: q <= 1'b0;   // reset
                2'b10: q <= 1'b1;   // set
                2'b11: q <= ~q;      // toggle
            endcase
    end
endmodule",verilog;verilog-latches-flip-flop-types;code,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=verilog&lesson=verilog-latches-flip-flop-types,lesson:verilog/verilog-latches-flip-flop-types,code
693,Digital Logic Design,Verilog,Latches & Flip-Flop Types in Verilog — T flip-flop [f531d77f],"module t_flip_flop (
    input  clk,
    input  reset,
    input  t,
    output reg q
);
    always @(posedge clk or posedge reset) begin
        if (reset)
            q <= 1'b0;
        else if (t)
            q <= ~q;
    end
endmodule",verilog;verilog-latches-flip-flop-types;code,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=verilog&lesson=verilog-latches-flip-flop-types,lesson:verilog/verilog-latches-flip-flop-types,code
694,Digital Logic Design,Verilog,Latches & Flip-Flop Types in Verilog — T flip-flop [b3771c0c],Both flip-flops use the same skeleton as the D flip-flop from the beginner lessons: a clocked always block with non-blocking assignment. Only the equation deciding the next value of q changes -- exactly matching the characteristic-equation table from the Digital Logic Design course.,verilog;verilog-latches-flip-flop-types;lesson_passage,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=verilog&lesson=verilog-latches-flip-flop-types,lesson:verilog/verilog-latches-flip-flop-types,lesson_passage
695,Digital Logic Design,Verilog,Registers & Shift Registers in Verilog — A parallel-load register [b5e7eab6],"module register #(parameter WIDTH = 8) (
    input                   clk,
