696,Digital Logic Design,Verilog,"Registers & Shift Registers in Verilog — SIPO shift register (serial-in, parallel-out) [a6caa89f]","module sipo #(parameter WIDTH = 8) (
    input                   clk,
    input                   serial_in,
    output reg [WIDTH-1:0]  q
);
    always @(posedge clk)
        q <= {q[WIDTH-2:0], serial_in};
endmodule",verilog;verilog-registers-shift-registers;code,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=verilog&lesson=verilog-registers-shift-registers,lesson:verilog/verilog-registers-shift-registers,code
697,Digital Logic Design,Verilog,"Registers & Shift Registers in Verilog — SIPO shift register (serial-in, parallel-out) [0e5f9eeb]","Each clock edge shifts every bit one position left and drops the new serial bit into the bottom -- the concatenation {q[WIDTH-2:0], serial_in} builds exactly that new value in one expression.",verilog;verilog-registers-shift-registers;lesson_passage,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=verilog&lesson=verilog-registers-shift-registers,lesson:verilog/verilog-registers-shift-registers,lesson_passage
698,Digital Logic Design,Verilog,Registers & Shift Registers in Verilog — Universal shift register [1ef5d91b],"Combining hold, shift-left, shift-right, and parallel load into one module, selected by a 2-bit mode input, matches the universal shift register from the DLD course:",verilog;verilog-registers-shift-registers;lesson_passage,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=verilog&lesson=verilog-registers-shift-registers,lesson:verilog/verilog-registers-shift-registers,lesson_passage
699,Digital Logic Design,Verilog,Registers & Shift Registers in Verilog — Universal shift register [c66f8862],"module universal_shift_reg #(parameter WIDTH = 8) (
    input                   clk,
    input      [1:0]        mode,   // 00=hold, 01=shift right, 10=shift left, 11=load
    input                   sin_r,  // serial in for shift-right
    input                   sin_l,  // serial in for shift-left
    input      [WIDTH-1:0]  d,      // parallel load data
    output reg [WIDTH-1:0]  q
);
    always @(posedge clk) begin
        case (mode)
            2'b00: q <= q;
            2'b01: q <= {sin_r, q[WIDTH-1:1]};
            2'b10: q <= {q[WIDTH-2:0], sin_l};
            2'b11: q <= d;
        endcase
    end
endmodule",verilog;verilog-registers-shift-registers;code,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=verilog&lesson=verilog-registers-shift-registers,lesson:verilog/verilog-registers-shift-registers,code
700,Digital Logic Design,Verilog,Counters in Verilog — Free-running binary up counter [7d65c2aa],"module up_counter #(parameter WIDTH = 4) (
    input                   clk,
    input                   reset,
    output reg [WIDTH-1:0]  count
);
    always @(posedge clk or posedge reset)
        if (reset)
            count <= 0;
        else
            count <= count + 1'b1;
endmodule",verilog;verilog-counters;code,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=verilog&lesson=verilog-counters,lesson:verilog/verilog-counters,code
701,Digital Logic Design,Verilog,Counters in Verilog — Free-running binary up counter [5de105ea],"This single line, count <= count + 1, is what a synchronous counter′s per-bit toggle logic (from the DLD counters lesson) compiles down to -- the synthesis tool works out the individual AND-gate toggle equations for you.",verilog;verilog-counters;lesson_passage,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=verilog&lesson=verilog-counters,lesson:verilog/verilog-counters,lesson_passage
702,Digital Logic Design,Verilog,Counters in Verilog — Up/down counter [d0f4c484],"module updown_counter #(parameter WIDTH = 4) (
    input                   clk,
    input                   reset,
    input                   up_down,   // 1 = count up, 0 = count down
    output reg [WIDTH-1:0]  count
);
    always @(posedge clk or posedge reset)
        if (reset)
            count <= 0;
        else if (up_down)
            count <= count + 1'b1;
        else
            count <= count - 1'b1;
endmodule",verilog;verilog-counters;code,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=verilog&lesson=verilog-counters,lesson:verilog/verilog-counters,code
703,Digital Logic Design,Verilog,Counters in Verilog — Mod-N (e.g. BCD/decade) counter [48c96f06],"module mod_n_counter #(parameter WIDTH = 4, parameter N = 10) (
    input                   clk,
    input                   reset,
    output reg [WIDTH-1:0]  count
);
    always @(posedge clk or posedge reset)
        if (reset)
            count <= 0;
        else if (count == N - 1)
            count <= 0;
        else
            count <= count + 1'b1;
endmodule",verilog;verilog-counters;code,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=verilog&lesson=verilog-counters,lesson:verilog/verilog-counters,code
704,Digital Logic Design,Verilog,Counters in Verilog — Mod-N (e.g. BCD/decade) counter [65d6a5a0],"Setting N = 10 gives exactly the BCD decade counter from the Digital Logic Design course: it counts 0 through 9 and then wraps back to 0, instead of continuing into the unused 1010-1111 codes.",verilog;verilog-counters;lesson_passage,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=verilog&lesson=verilog-counters,lesson:verilog/verilog-counters,lesson_passage
705,Digital Logic Design,Verilog,Carry-Lookahead Adders & Generate Blocks — Computing carries in parallel [96a2e18c],"The DLD course defines, for each bit position i: Pi = Ai ⊕ Bi (propagate) and Gi = Ai · Bi (generate), with C(i+1) = Gi + Pi · Ci. Verilog can compute every carry from these equations directly, without waiting for a chain of full adders:",verilog;verilog-carry-lookahead-generate;lesson_passage,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=verilog&lesson=verilog-carry-lookahead-generate,lesson:verilog/verilog-carry-lookahead-generate,lesson_passage
706,Digital Logic Design,Verilog,Carry-Lookahead Adders & Generate Blocks — Computing carries in parallel [c96a233b],"module cla_adder #(parameter WIDTH = 4) (
    input  [WIDTH-1:0] a,
    input  [WIDTH-1:0] b,
    input              cin,
    output [WIDTH-1:0] sum,
    output             cout
);
    wire [WIDTH-1:0] p = a ^ b;         // propagate
    wire [WIDTH-1:0] g = a & b;         // generate
    wire [WIDTH:0]   c;

    assign c[0] = cin;

    genvar i;
    generate
        for (i = 0; i < WIDTH; i = i + 1) begin : carry_stage
            assign c[i+1] = g[i] | (p[i] & c[i]);
        end
    endgenerate

    assign sum  = p ^ c[WIDTH-1:0];
    assign cout = c[WIDTH];
endmodule",verilog;verilog-carry-lookahead-generate;code,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=verilog&lesson=verilog-carry-lookahead-generate,lesson:verilog/verilog-carry-lookahead-generate,code
707,Digital Logic Design,Verilog,Carry-Lookahead Adders & Generate Blocks — Computing carries in parallel [3f972b52],"Because every c[i+1] is an independent assign statement rather than a signal threaded through instantiated full-adder modules, a synthesis tool is free to expand and optimize the whole carry chain into a shallow tree of gates -- which is exactly the ""compute carries ahead of time"" idea the name describes.",verilog;verilog-carry-lookahead-generate;lesson_passage,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=verilog&lesson=verilog-carry-lookahead-generate,lesson:verilog/verilog-carry-lookahead-generate,lesson_passage
708,Digital Logic Design,Verilog,Carry-Lookahead Adders & Generate Blocks — generate blocks beyond adders [473159d4],"The same generate/genvar pattern used here and in the ripple-carry adder lesson scales to building any regular, repeated structure parametrically -- an N-bit ring counter, an array of registers, or a tree of comparators -- without writing out each stage by hand.",verilog;verilog-carry-lookahead-generate;lesson_passage,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=verilog&lesson=verilog-carry-lookahead-generate,lesson:verilog/verilog-carry-lookahead-generate,lesson_passage
709,Digital Logic Design,Verilog,Finite State Machines in Verilog — Encoding states [0f2c35b6],"Following the DLD design steps, an FSM in Verilog starts by naming and encoding its states, usually with localparam constants instead of hard-coded numbers scattered through the code:",verilog;verilog-fsm-design;lesson_passage,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=verilog&lesson=verilog-fsm-design,lesson:verilog/verilog-fsm-design,lesson_passage
