711,Digital Logic Design,Verilog,"Finite State Machines in Verilog — Moore sequence detector for ""101"" [81ed0392]","This is the Moore version outlined in the DLD FSM lesson: it needs a dedicated state (S_MATCH) whose output is 1, since a Moore machine′s output can only depend on the current state.",verilog;verilog-fsm-design;lesson_passage,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=verilog&lesson=verilog-fsm-design,lesson:verilog/verilog-fsm-design,lesson_passage
712,Digital Logic Design,Verilog,"Finite State Machines in Verilog — Moore sequence detector for ""101"" [c500305b]","module seq_detector_101_moore (
    input  clk,
    input  reset,
    input  bit_in,
    output y
);
    localparam S_IDLE  = 2'b00;
    localparam S_GOT1  = 2'b01;
    localparam S_GOT10 = 2'b10;
    localparam S_MATCH = 2'b11;

    reg [1:0] state, next_state;

    // next-state logic (combinational)
    always @(*) begin
        case (state)
            S_IDLE:  next_state = bit_in ? S_GOT1  : S_IDLE;
            S_GOT1:  next_state = bit_in ? S_GOT1  : S_GOT10;
            S_GOT10: next_state = bit_in ? S_MATCH : S_IDLE;
            S_MATCH: next_state = bit_in ? S_GOT1  : S_GOT10;
            default: next_state = S_IDLE;
        endcase
    end

    // state register (sequential)
    always @(posedge clk or posedge reset)
        if (reset)
            state <= S_IDLE;
        else
            state <= next_state;

    // Moore output: depends only on state
    assign y = (state == S_MATCH);
endmodule",verilog;verilog-fsm-design;code,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=verilog&lesson=verilog-fsm-design,lesson:verilog/verilog-fsm-design,code
713,Digital Logic Design,Verilog,"Finite State Machines in Verilog — Moore sequence detector for ""101"" [ea5c5246]","This three-block structure -- combinational next-state logic, a clocked state register, and a combinational output equation -- is the standard template for describing any FSM in Verilog, and it maps directly onto the DLD design steps: the case statement in the first block is the state table, and keeping it in its own always @(*) block, separate from the clocked block, keeps the combinational and sequential parts of the design cleanly separated.",verilog;verilog-fsm-design;lesson_passage,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=verilog&lesson=verilog-fsm-design,lesson:verilog/verilog-fsm-design,lesson_passage
714,Digital Logic Design,Verilog,Finite State Machines in Verilog — Mealy version [7aa976be],"A Mealy machine produces its output on the transition, which in Verilog means the output equation reads the current state and the current input, instead of state alone:",verilog;verilog-fsm-design;lesson_passage,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=verilog&lesson=verilog-fsm-design,lesson:verilog/verilog-fsm-design,lesson_passage
715,Digital Logic Design,Verilog,Finite State Machines in Verilog — Mealy version [e794c7ff],"With this change, S_MATCH is no longer needed as a separate state at all -- exactly the ""typically needing only 3 states"" difference the DLD FSM lesson describes between the Moore and Mealy versions of the same detector.",verilog;verilog-fsm-design;lesson_passage,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=verilog&lesson=verilog-fsm-design,lesson:verilog/verilog-fsm-design,lesson_passage
716,Digital Logic Design,Verilog,Modeling Memory in Verilog — Modeling a ROM [1b625d5c],"The DLD memory lesson describes a ROM as a fixed decoder feeding a programmable OR array. In Verilog, the simplest behavioral model of that same idea is just an array of registers, initialized once, and read combinationally by address:",verilog;verilog-memory-modeling;lesson_passage,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=verilog&lesson=verilog-memory-modeling,lesson:verilog/verilog-memory-modeling,lesson_passage
717,Digital Logic Design,Verilog,Modeling Memory in Verilog — Modeling a ROM [66abe104],"module rom_16x8 (
    input  [3:0] addr,
    output [7:0] data
);
    reg [7:0] mem [0:15];

    initial begin
        mem[0] = 8'h00;
        mem[1] = 8'h01;
        mem[2] = 8'h04;
        // ... remaining entries would be filled in the same way,
        // or loaded from a file with $readmemh in simulation
    end

    assign data = mem[addr];
endmodule",verilog;verilog-memory-modeling;code,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=verilog&lesson=verilog-memory-modeling,lesson:verilog/verilog-memory-modeling,code
718,Digital Logic Design,Verilog,Modeling Memory in Verilog — Modeling a RAM [1a418219],"A RAM adds a write port: on a write-enabled clock edge the addressed word is updated, exactly like the SRAM described in the DLD lesson (a latch per storage cell, holding its value until explicitly rewritten).",verilog;verilog-memory-modeling;lesson_passage,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=verilog&lesson=verilog-memory-modeling,lesson:verilog/verilog-memory-modeling,lesson_passage
719,Digital Logic Design,Verilog,Modeling Memory in Verilog — Modeling a RAM [b64eecf6],"module ram_16x8 (
    input        clk,
    input        write_enable,
    input  [3:0] addr,
    input  [7:0] data_in,
    output [7:0] data_out
);
    reg [7:0] mem [0:15];

    always @(posedge clk)
        if (write_enable)
            mem[addr] <= data_in;

    assign data_out = mem[addr];   // combinational read
endmodule",verilog;verilog-memory-modeling;code,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=verilog&lesson=verilog-memory-modeling,lesson:verilog/verilog-memory-modeling,code
720,Digital Logic Design,Verilog,Modeling Memory in Verilog — Modeling a RAM [760d690e],"This combinational-read, synchronous-write style is the most common simple RAM model. Real on-chip RAM blocks are usually synchronous on both read and write (registering data_out as well) to match how physical memory macros actually behave, and to keep timing predictable.",verilog;verilog-memory-modeling;lesson_passage,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=verilog&lesson=verilog-memory-modeling,lesson:verilog/verilog-memory-modeling,lesson_passage
721,Digital Logic Design,Verilog,Testbenches & Verification — Why testbenches [7b1aeb40],"Every circuit in this course, from a half adder to an FSM, needs to be checked against its truth table or state diagram before anyone trusts it in real hardware. A testbench is a separate Verilog module, never synthesized, that instantiates the design and drives it with test inputs in simulation.",verilog;verilog-testbenches-verification;lesson_passage,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=verilog&lesson=verilog-testbenches-verification,lesson:verilog/verilog-testbenches-verification,lesson_passage
722,Digital Logic Design,Verilog,Testbenches & Verification — A simple testbench for the full adder [58a94eaa],"module full_adder_tb;
    reg  a, b, cin;
    wire sum, cout;

    full_adder dut (.a(a), .b(b), .cin(cin), .sum(sum), .cout(cout));

    initial begin
        $monitor(""t=%0t a=%b b=%b cin=%b -> sum=%b cout=%b"", $time, a, b, cin, sum, cout);

        a = 0; b = 0; cin = 0; #10;
        a = 0; b = 1; cin = 0; #10;
        a = 1; b = 1; cin = 0; #10;
        a = 1; b = 1; cin = 1; #10;

        $finish;
    end
endmodule",verilog;verilog-testbenches-verification;code,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=verilog&lesson=verilog-testbenches-verification,lesson:verilog/verilog-testbenches-verification,code
723,Digital Logic Design,Verilog,Testbenches & Verification — A simple testbench for the full adder [135ed0e9],"dut (""device under test"") is just a conventional instance name. $monitor prints a new line automatically whenever any of its listed signals changes, so the whole simulation timeline shows up without needing a $display after every input change. #10 advances simulation time by 10 time units, letting the combinational logic settle before the next input change.",verilog;verilog-testbenches-verification;lesson_passage,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=verilog&lesson=verilog-testbenches-verification,lesson:verilog/verilog-testbenches-verification,lesson_passage
724,Digital Logic Design,Verilog,Testbenches & Verification — Clock generation and a self-checking testbench [e0199922],"Sequential designs additionally need a clock generator, and a well-written testbench compares the design′s output against an expected value automatically instead of requiring a human to read through a log. Rather than hard-coding the exact cycle reset releases on, this testbench captures whatever count value appears right after reset is lifted as its baseline, then checks that every following edge increments from there by exactly one -- a pattern that does not depend on precisely hand-tracking simulation timing:",verilog;verilog-testbenches-verification;lesson_passage,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=verilog&lesson=verilog-testbenches-verification,lesson:verilog/verilog-testbenches-verification,lesson_passage
725,Digital Logic Design,Verilog,Testbenches & Verification — Clock generation and a self-checking testbench [eba3f1c6],"module up_counter_tb;
    reg clk = 0;
    reg reset = 1;
    wire [3:0] count;

    up_counter #(.WIDTH(4)) dut (.clk(clk), .reset(reset), .count(count));

    always #5 clk = ~clk;   // 10-time-unit clock period

    reg [3:0] expected;
    integer i;

    initial begin
        #12 reset = 0;
        @(posedge clk);
        expected = count;   // capture the count right after reset releases

        for (i = 0; i < 20; i = i + 1) begin
            @(posedge clk);
            expected = expected + 1'b1;
            if (count !== expected)
                $display(""MISMATCH at i=%0d: expected %0d, got %0d"", i, expected, count);
        end

        $display(""Test complete."");
        $finish;
    end
endmodule",verilog;verilog-testbenches-verification;code,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=verilog&lesson=verilog-testbenches-verification,lesson:verilog/verilog-testbenches-verification,code
726,Digital Logic Design,Verilog,Testbenches & Verification — Clock generation and a self-checking testbench [9495bc78],"Writing testbenches like this for every module in this course -- checking each one against the exact truth table, characteristic equation, or state diagram it was designed from in the Digital Logic Design course -- is one of the most valuable habits for catching bugs before a design ever reaches real hardware.",verilog;verilog-testbenches-verification;lesson_passage,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=verilog&lesson=verilog-testbenches-verification,lesson:verilog/verilog-testbenches-verification,lesson_passage
727,Digital Logic Design,VHDL,Entities & Architectures — Entity: the interface [fd21e2e2],"An entity declares a component's name and its ports (inputs and outputs), similar to a Verilog module's port list.",vhdl;vhdl-entity-architecture;lesson_passage,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=vhdl&lesson=vhdl-entity-architecture,lesson:vhdl/vhdl-entity-architecture,lesson_passage
