730,Digital Logic Design,VHDL,Entities & Architectures — Architecture: the behavior [8527ee31],"A single entity can have multiple architectures (for example, one for simulation behavior and one for a gate-level implementation), and you choose which one to use at compile/simulation time.",vhdl;vhdl-entity-architecture;lesson_passage,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=vhdl&lesson=vhdl-entity-architecture,lesson:vhdl/vhdl-entity-architecture,lesson_passage
731,Digital Logic Design,VHDL,VHDL Data Types — Why VHDL types matter more than in Verilog [7f1a8128],"VHDL is strongly typed: unlike Verilog, values of different types cannot be mixed in an expression without an explicit conversion, and the compiler enforces this strictly. This catches more mistakes at compile time, at the cost of more boilerplate than Verilog needs for the same circuit.",vhdl;vhdl-data-types;lesson_passage,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=vhdl&lesson=vhdl-data-types,lesson:vhdl/vhdl-data-types,lesson_passage
732,Digital Logic Design,VHDL,VHDL Data Types — std_logic and std_logic_vector [2dc078de],"The IEEE std_logic type represents a single bit, but with more possible values than just 0 and 1 -- useful for simulating real hardware behavior such as an undriven or conflicting signal.",vhdl;vhdl-data-types;lesson_passage,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=vhdl&lesson=vhdl-data-types,lesson:vhdl/vhdl-data-types,lesson_passage
733,Digital Logic Design,VHDL,VHDL Data Types — std_logic and std_logic_vector [ded5667e],"Common std_logic values | Value | Meaning | '0', '1' | Logic low / logic high | 'Z' | High impedance (e.g. an undriven tri-state output) | 'X' | Unknown (e.g. two drivers fighting over the same signal) | 'U' | Uninitialized (a signal′s value before simulation gives it one) | '-' | Don′t care",vhdl;vhdl-data-types;lesson_passage,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=vhdl&lesson=vhdl-data-types,lesson:vhdl/vhdl-data-types,lesson_passage
734,Digital Logic Design,VHDL,VHDL Data Types — std_logic and std_logic_vector [6c751525],"library ieee;
use ieee.std_logic_1164.all;

entity example is
    port (
        a : in  std_logic;
        b : in  std_logic_vector(3 downto 0);
        y : out std_logic
    );
end entity example;",vhdl;vhdl-data-types;code,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=vhdl&lesson=vhdl-data-types,lesson:vhdl/vhdl-data-types,code
735,Digital Logic Design,VHDL,VHDL Data Types — std_logic and std_logic_vector [4c6af65d],"std_logic_vector is a bus of std_logic bits -- std_logic_vector(3 downto 0) is a 4-bit bus, with bit 3 as the most significant bit and bit 0 as the least significant, matching the order most engineers read a binary number in.",vhdl;vhdl-data-types;lesson_passage,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=vhdl&lesson=vhdl-data-types,lesson:vhdl/vhdl-data-types,lesson_passage
736,Digital Logic Design,VHDL,VHDL Data Types — The two library packages nearly every VHDL file needs [4aecf8d2],"library ieee;
use ieee.std_logic_1164.all;   -- defines std_logic and std_logic_vector
use ieee.numeric_std.all;      -- defines signed/unsigned and arithmetic on them",vhdl;vhdl-data-types;code,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=vhdl&lesson=vhdl-data-types,lesson:vhdl/vhdl-data-types,code
737,Digital Logic Design,VHDL,VHDL Data Types — signed and unsigned: enabling arithmetic [c1493d70],"std_logic_vector alone has no defined arithmetic -- VHDL will not let you add two std_logic_vector signals directly. The unsigned and signed types (from numeric_std) are built on the same bits but carry arithmetic meaning, so + and - work on them directly.",vhdl;vhdl-data-types;lesson_passage,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=vhdl&lesson=vhdl-data-types,lesson:vhdl/vhdl-data-types,lesson_passage
738,Digital Logic Design,VHDL,VHDL Data Types — signed and unsigned: enabling arithmetic [5c8f09d9],"signal a, b, sum : unsigned(3 downto 0);
sum <= a + b;   -- valid: unsigned addition",vhdl;vhdl-data-types;code,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=vhdl&lesson=vhdl-data-types,lesson:vhdl/vhdl-data-types,code
739,Digital Logic Design,VHDL,VHDL Data Types — signed and unsigned: enabling arithmetic [ffeec7de],"signal data  : std_logic_vector(7 downto 0);
