742,Digital Logic Design,VHDL,VHDL Data Types — Constants and generics [94c857f4],A generic is VHDL′s equivalent of a Verilog module parameter -- a compile-time value that lets the same entity be instantiated at different widths without rewriting it.,vhdl;vhdl-data-types;lesson_passage,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=vhdl&lesson=vhdl-data-types,lesson:vhdl/vhdl-data-types,lesson_passage
743,Digital Logic Design,VHDL,Signals & Processes — Signals [9279c50b],"A signal represents a wire or piece of state inside an architecture, declared with a type such as std_logic (a single bit) or std_logic_vector (a bus of bits).",vhdl;vhdl-signals-processes;lesson_passage,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=vhdl&lesson=vhdl-signals-processes,lesson:vhdl/vhdl-signals-processes,lesson_passage
744,Digital Logic Design,VHDL,Signals & Processes — Processes [f1eca340],A process block groups sequential statements that execute whenever something in its sensitivity list changes -- most commonly a clock.,vhdl;vhdl-signals-processes;lesson_passage,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=vhdl&lesson=vhdl-signals-processes,lesson:vhdl/vhdl-signals-processes,lesson_passage
745,Digital Logic Design,VHDL,Signals & Processes — Processes [b28bc6a8],"architecture behavior of d_flip_flop is
begin
    process (clk, reset)
    begin
        if reset = '1' then
            q <= '0';
        elsif rising_edge(clk) then
            q <= d;
        end if;
    end process;
end architecture behavior;",vhdl;vhdl-signals-processes;code,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=vhdl&lesson=vhdl-signals-processes,lesson:vhdl/vhdl-signals-processes,code
746,Digital Logic Design,VHDL,Signals & Processes — Signal vs. variable [9760ac2b],"signal vs. variable | Kind | Update timing | Scope | signal | Updated at the end of the current simulation cycle | Visible across the whole architecture | variable | Updated immediately, in program order | Local to the process it is declared in",vhdl;vhdl-signals-processes;lesson_passage,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=vhdl&lesson=vhdl-signals-processes,lesson:vhdl/vhdl-signals-processes,lesson_passage
747,Digital Logic Design,VHDL,Half & Full Adders in VHDL — Half adder: concurrent (dataflow) style [4b58354b],"library ieee;
use ieee.std_logic_1164.all;

entity half_adder is
    port (
        a, b      : in  std_logic;
        sum, cout : out std_logic
    );
end entity half_adder;

architecture dataflow of half_adder is
begin
    sum  <= a xor b;
    cout <= a and b;
end architecture dataflow;",vhdl;vhdl-half-full-adders;code,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=vhdl&lesson=vhdl-half-full-adders,lesson:vhdl/vhdl-half-full-adders,code
748,Digital Logic Design,VHDL,Half & Full Adders in VHDL — Full adder: concurrent style [f968a6cf],"entity full_adder is
    port (
        a, b, cin : in  std_logic;
        sum, cout : out std_logic
    );
end entity full_adder;

architecture dataflow of full_adder is
begin
    sum  <= a xor b xor cin;
    cout <= (a and b) or (a and cin) or (b and cin);
end architecture dataflow;",vhdl;vhdl-half-full-adders;code,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=vhdl&lesson=vhdl-half-full-adders,lesson:vhdl/vhdl-half-full-adders,code
749,Digital Logic Design,VHDL,"Half & Full Adders in VHDL — Full adder: structural style, built from two half adders [5cdbbdce]","Just like Verilog, VHDL lets an architecture be built purely by wiring up instances of other entities -- exactly matching the DLD lesson describing a full adder built from two half adders.",vhdl;vhdl-half-full-adders;lesson_passage,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=vhdl&lesson=vhdl-half-full-adders,lesson:vhdl/vhdl-half-full-adders,lesson_passage
750,Digital Logic Design,VHDL,"Half & Full Adders in VHDL — Full adder: structural style, built from two half adders [30057ff3]","architecture structural of full_adder is
    signal s1, c1, c2 : std_logic;
begin
    ha1 : entity work.half_adder
        port map (a => a, b => b, sum => s1, cout => c1);

    ha2 : entity work.half_adder
        port map (a => s1, b => cin, sum => sum, cout => c2);

    cout <= c1 or c2;
end architecture structural;",vhdl;vhdl-half-full-adders;code,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=vhdl&lesson=vhdl-half-full-adders,lesson:vhdl/vhdl-half-full-adders,code
751,Digital Logic Design,VHDL,"Half & Full Adders in VHDL — Full adder: structural style, built from two half adders [b3d1915b]","port map (port_name => signal, ...) is VHDL′s named port connection -- the VHDL equivalent of the .port_name(signal) syntax from the Verilog course, connecting each entity port to a specific signal regardless of declaration order.",vhdl;vhdl-half-full-adders;lesson_passage,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=vhdl&lesson=vhdl-half-full-adders,lesson:vhdl/vhdl-half-full-adders,lesson_passage
752,Digital Logic Design,VHDL,Decoders & Encoders in VHDL — 2-to-4 decoder: concurrent style [d2b55405],"entity decoder_2to4 is
    port (
        a : in  std_logic_vector(1 downto 0);
        d : out std_logic_vector(3 downto 0)
    );
end entity decoder_2to4;

architecture dataflow of decoder_2to4 is
begin
    d(0) <= (not a(1)) and (not a(0));
    d(1) <= (not a(1)) and a(0);
    d(2) <= a(1) and (not a(0));
    d(3) <= a(1) and a(0);
end architecture dataflow;",vhdl;vhdl-decoders-encoders;code,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=vhdl&lesson=vhdl-decoders-encoders,lesson:vhdl/vhdl-decoders-encoders,code
753,Digital Logic Design,VHDL,Decoders & Encoders in VHDL — A cleaner decoder using a process and a case statement [da8464db],"architecture behavioral of decoder_2to4 is
begin
    process(a)
    begin
        case a is
            when ""00""   => d <= ""0001"";
            when ""01""   => d <= ""0010"";
            when ""10""   => d <= ""0100"";
            when ""11""   => d <= ""1000"";
            when others => d <= ""0000"";
        end case;
    end process;
end architecture behavioral;",vhdl;vhdl-decoders-encoders;code,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=vhdl&lesson=vhdl-decoders-encoders,lesson:vhdl/vhdl-decoders-encoders,code
754,Digital Logic Design,VHDL,Decoders & Encoders in VHDL — A cleaner decoder using a process and a case statement [3c974fc0],"A process with every signal it reads listed in its sensitivity list (here, just a) is VHDL′s way of describing combinational logic with a case or if statement, matching the role of Verilog′s always @(*) block. The when others branch is required by VHDL whenever a case does not explicitly cover every possible value of its type -- for a 2-bit std_logic_vector, that technically includes values like ""ZZ"" or ""XX"", not just the four binary combinations.",vhdl;vhdl-decoders-encoders;lesson_passage,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=vhdl&lesson=vhdl-decoders-encoders,lesson:vhdl/vhdl-decoders-encoders,lesson_passage
755,Digital Logic Design,VHDL,Decoders & Encoders in VHDL — 4-to-2 encoder [5440dc10],"entity encoder_4to2 is
    port (
        d : in  std_logic_vector(3 downto 0);
        a : out std_logic_vector(1 downto 0)
    );
end entity encoder_4to2;

architecture behavioral of encoder_4to2 is
begin
    process(d)
    begin
        case d is
            when ""0001"" => a <= ""00"";
            when ""0010"" => a <= ""01"";
            when ""0100"" => a <= ""10"";
            when ""1000"" => a <= ""11"";
            when others => a <= ""00"";
        end case;
    end process;
end architecture behavioral;",vhdl;vhdl-decoders-encoders;code,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=vhdl&lesson=vhdl-decoders-encoders,lesson:vhdl/vhdl-decoders-encoders,code
756,Digital Logic Design,VHDL,Decoders & Encoders in VHDL — 4-to-2 encoder [1677dcbd],"As with the Verilog version of this circuit, the when others branch matters: without it, an unexpected input pattern would leave a holding its previous value rather than a defined one, which can synthesize into an unintended latch.",vhdl;vhdl-decoders-encoders;lesson_passage,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=vhdl&lesson=vhdl-decoders-encoders,lesson:vhdl/vhdl-decoders-encoders,lesson_passage
757,Digital Logic Design,VHDL,Comparators & Parity Circuits in VHDL — Multi-bit comparators [2af0aed7],"library ieee;
use ieee.std_logic_1164.all;
use ieee.numeric_std.all;

entity comparator is
    generic (WIDTH : integer := 4);
    port (
        a, b       : in  std_logic_vector(WIDTH-1 downto 0);
        gt, eq, lt : out std_logic
    );
end entity comparator;

architecture dataflow of comparator is
begin
    gt <= '1' when unsigned(a) > unsigned(b) else '0';
    eq <= '1' when unsigned(a) = unsigned(b) else '0';
    lt <= '1' when unsigned(a) < unsigned(b) else '0';
end architecture dataflow;",vhdl;vhdl-comparators-parity;code,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=vhdl&lesson=vhdl-comparators-parity,lesson:vhdl/vhdl-comparators-parity,code
758,Digital Logic Design,VHDL,Comparators & Parity Circuits in VHDL — Multi-bit comparators [41fb3d35],The when ... else construct is a concurrent conditional assignment -- VHDL′s way of writing a one-line combinational condition without needing a full process block. Comparing as unsigned rather than directly on std_logic_vector is what gives > and < their expected numeric meaning rather than a simple bit-pattern comparison.,vhdl;vhdl-comparators-parity;lesson_passage,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=vhdl&lesson=vhdl-comparators-parity,lesson:vhdl/vhdl-comparators-parity,lesson_passage
759,Digital Logic Design,VHDL,Comparators & Parity Circuits in VHDL — Parity generator and checker [7f0c19c1],"VHDL does not have a built-in reduction-XOR operator the way Verilog does, but a small loop with a variable produces the same result.",vhdl;vhdl-comparators-parity;lesson_passage,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=vhdl&lesson=vhdl-comparators-parity,lesson:vhdl/vhdl-comparators-parity,lesson_passage
760,Digital Logic Design,VHDL,Comparators & Parity Circuits in VHDL — Parity generator and checker [ab78ecc5],"entity parity_gen is
    generic (WIDTH : integer := 8);
    port (
        data            : in  std_logic_vector(WIDTH-1 downto 0);
        even_parity_bit : out std_logic
    );
end entity parity_gen;

architecture behavioral of parity_gen is
begin
    process(data)
        variable p : std_logic;
    begin
        p := '0';
        for i in data'range loop
            p := p xor data(i);
        end loop;
        even_parity_bit <= p;
    end process;
end architecture behavioral;",vhdl;vhdl-comparators-parity;code,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=vhdl&lesson=vhdl-comparators-parity,lesson:vhdl/vhdl-comparators-parity,code
761,Digital Logic Design,VHDL,Comparators & Parity Circuits in VHDL — Parity generator and checker [89ac9027],"A variable (declared inside the process, updated immediately in program order) is the natural tool here, since each loop iteration needs to see the previous iteration′s updated value right away -- unlike a signal, whose new value would not be visible until the next time the process runs.",vhdl;vhdl-comparators-parity;lesson_passage,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=vhdl&lesson=vhdl-comparators-parity,lesson:vhdl/vhdl-comparators-parity,lesson_passage
762,Digital Logic Design,VHDL,Multiplexers & Demultiplexers in VHDL — 2-to-1 multiplexer: conditional signal assignment [4dfa0db9],"entity mux2to1 is
    port (
        sel  : in  std_logic;
        a, b : in  std_logic;
        y    : out std_logic
    );
end entity mux2to1;

architecture dataflow of mux2to1 is
begin
    y <= b when sel = '1' else a;
end architecture dataflow;",vhdl;vhdl-multiplexers-demultiplexers;code,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=vhdl&lesson=vhdl-multiplexers-demultiplexers,lesson:vhdl/vhdl-multiplexers-demultiplexers,code
763,Digital Logic Design,VHDL,Multiplexers & Demultiplexers in VHDL — 4-to-1 multiplexer: selected signal assignment [99d9485e],"entity mux4to1 is
    port (
        sel : in  std_logic_vector(1 downto 0);
        d   : in  std_logic_vector(3 downto 0);
        y   : out std_logic
    );
end entity mux4to1;

architecture dataflow of mux4to1 is
begin
    with sel select
        y <= d(0) when ""00"",
             d(1) when ""01"",
             d(2) when ""10"",
             d(3) when ""11"",
             '0' when others;
end architecture dataflow;",vhdl;vhdl-multiplexers-demultiplexers;code,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=vhdl&lesson=vhdl-multiplexers-demultiplexers,lesson:vhdl/vhdl-multiplexers-demultiplexers,code
764,Digital Logic Design,VHDL,Multiplexers & Demultiplexers in VHDL — 4-to-1 multiplexer: selected signal assignment [3cad8a42],"with ... select is VHDL′s selected signal assignment -- functionally similar to a case statement inside a process, but written as a single concurrent statement instead. As with any case-like construct over a std_logic_vector, a final when others branch is required to cover every possible value of the selector, not just the two ""real"" bit patterns.",vhdl;vhdl-multiplexers-demultiplexers;lesson_passage,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=vhdl&lesson=vhdl-multiplexers-demultiplexers,lesson:vhdl/vhdl-multiplexers-demultiplexers,lesson_passage
765,Digital Logic Design,VHDL,Multiplexers & Demultiplexers in VHDL — 1-to-4 demultiplexer [7a8a8703],"entity demux1to4 is
    port (
        sel : in  std_logic_vector(1 downto 0);
        d   : in  std_logic;
        y   : out std_logic_vector(3 downto 0)
    );
end entity demux1to4;

architecture behavioral of demux1to4 is
begin
    process(sel, d)
    begin
        y <= ""0000"";
        case sel is
            when ""00""   => y(0) <= d;
            when ""01""   => y(1) <= d;
            when ""10""   => y(2) <= d;
            when ""11""   => y(3) <= d;
            when others => null;
        end case;
    end process;
end architecture behavioral;",vhdl;vhdl-multiplexers-demultiplexers;code,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=vhdl&lesson=vhdl-multiplexers-demultiplexers,lesson:vhdl/vhdl-multiplexers-demultiplexers,code
766,Digital Logic Design,VHDL,Multiplexers & Demultiplexers in VHDL — 1-to-4 demultiplexer [743facb7],"Assigning y <= ""0000"" first, then overriding a single bit inside the case, is the same idiom used in the Verilog course′s demux example: it guarantees every output bit has a defined value on every path through the process, avoiding an inferred latch on the non-selected bits. null is VHDL′s explicit ""do nothing"" statement, needed here because the when others branch has no work left to do once the default assignment above has already run.",vhdl;vhdl-multiplexers-demultiplexers;lesson_passage,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=vhdl&lesson=vhdl-multiplexers-demultiplexers,lesson:vhdl/vhdl-multiplexers-demultiplexers,lesson_passage
767,Digital Logic Design,VHDL,Ripple-Carry Adders & a Simple ALU in VHDL — Ripple-carry adder from a generate statement [5410cc36],"library ieee;
use ieee.std_logic_1164.all;

entity ripple_adder is
    generic (WIDTH : integer := 4);
    port (
        a, b : in  std_logic_vector(WIDTH-1 downto 0);
        cin  : in  std_logic;
        sum  : out std_logic_vector(WIDTH-1 downto 0);
        cout : out std_logic
    );
end entity ripple_adder;

architecture structural of ripple_adder is
    signal carry : std_logic_vector(WIDTH downto 0);
begin
    carry(0) <= cin;
    cout <= carry(WIDTH);

    fa_stage: for i in 0 to WIDTH-1 generate
        fa : entity work.full_adder
            port map (
                a    => a(i),
                b    => b(i),
                cin  => carry(i),
                sum  => sum(i),
                cout => carry(i+1)
            );
    end generate fa_stage;
end architecture structural;",vhdl;vhdl-ripple-carry-adder-alu;code,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=vhdl&lesson=vhdl-ripple-carry-adder-alu,lesson:vhdl/vhdl-ripple-carry-adder-alu,code
768,Digital Logic Design,VHDL,Ripple-Carry Adders & a Simple ALU in VHDL — Ripple-carry adder from a generate statement [59b5d4ce],"A for ... generate statement is VHDL′s equivalent of Verilog′s generate for loop with a genvar -- it creates WIDTH copies of the full_adder instance at compile time, each wired to a different bit of a, b, and the carry chain, exactly matching the ripple-carry adder from the Digital Logic Design course.",vhdl;vhdl-ripple-carry-adder-alu;lesson_passage,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=vhdl&lesson=vhdl-ripple-carry-adder-alu,lesson:vhdl/vhdl-ripple-carry-adder-alu,lesson_passage
769,Digital Logic Design,VHDL,Ripple-Carry Adders & a Simple ALU in VHDL — A mode-controlled adder/subtractor [ede46bd0],"use ieee.numeric_std.all;

entity addsub is
    generic (WIDTH : integer := 4);
    port (
        a, b   : in  std_logic_vector(WIDTH-1 downto 0);
        mode   : in  std_logic;   -- '0' = add, '1' = subtract (a - b)
        result : out std_logic_vector(WIDTH-1 downto 0);
        cout   : out std_logic
    );
end entity addsub;

architecture structural of addsub is
    signal b_xored : std_logic_vector(WIDTH-1 downto 0);
begin
    b_xored <= b xor (WIDTH-1 downto 0 => mode);

    core : entity work.ripple_adder
        generic map (WIDTH => WIDTH)
        port map (a => a, b => b_xored, cin => mode, sum => result, cout => cout);
end architecture structural;",vhdl;vhdl-ripple-carry-adder-alu;code,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=vhdl&lesson=vhdl-ripple-carry-adder-alu,lesson:vhdl/vhdl-ripple-carry-adder-alu,code
770,Digital Logic Design,VHDL,Ripple-Carry Adders & a Simple ALU in VHDL — A mode-controlled adder/subtractor [25143946],"(WIDTH-1 downto 0 => mode) is a VHDL aggregate: it builds a WIDTH-bit value where every bit equals mode -- the VHDL equivalent of Verilog′s {WIDTH{mode}} replication operator. XORing b against all 0s leaves it unchanged (addition); XORing against all 1s complements every bit, and combined with cin = mode = '1' adding 1, this produces the two′s complement of b -- the same adder/subtractor trick from the Digital Logic Design course.",vhdl;vhdl-ripple-carry-adder-alu;lesson_passage,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=vhdl&lesson=vhdl-ripple-carry-adder-alu,lesson:vhdl/vhdl-ripple-carry-adder-alu,lesson_passage
771,Digital Logic Design,VHDL,Latches & Flip-Flop Types in VHDL — Why you rarely write a latch on purpose [255b9099],"Just as in Verilog, an incompletely specified combinational process in VHDL -- one that does not assign an output on every possible path -- infers a latch rather than combinational logic, which most synthesis tools flag as a likely bug. Real designs almost always use the clocked, edge-triggered styles below instead.",vhdl;vhdl-latches-flip-flop-types;lesson_passage,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=vhdl&lesson=vhdl-latches-flip-flop-types,lesson:vhdl/vhdl-latches-flip-flop-types,lesson_passage
772,Digital Logic Design,VHDL,Latches & Flip-Flop Types in VHDL — D flip-flop [bdd07ca1],"library ieee;
use ieee.std_logic_1164.all;

entity d_flip_flop is
    port (
        clk, reset : in  std_logic;
        d          : in  std_logic;
        q          : out std_logic
    );
end entity d_flip_flop;

architecture behavioral of d_flip_flop is
begin
    process(clk, reset)
    begin
        if reset = '1' then
            q <= '0';
        elsif rising_edge(clk) then
            q <= d;
        end if;
    end process;
end architecture behavioral;",vhdl;vhdl-latches-flip-flop-types;code,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=vhdl&lesson=vhdl-latches-flip-flop-types,lesson:vhdl/vhdl-latches-flip-flop-types,code
773,Digital Logic Design,VHDL,Latches & Flip-Flop Types in VHDL — D flip-flop [be25f9be],"rising_edge(clk) is the standard, preferred way to detect a clock edge in modern VHDL -- clearer than the older clk'event and clk = '1' idiom still seen in legacy code, though both mean the same thing.",vhdl;vhdl-latches-flip-flop-types;lesson_passage,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=vhdl&lesson=vhdl-latches-flip-flop-types,lesson:vhdl/vhdl-latches-flip-flop-types,lesson_passage
774,Digital Logic Design,VHDL,Latches & Flip-Flop Types in VHDL — JK flip-flop [7b0d24a8],"entity jk_flip_flop is
    port (
        clk, reset : in  std_logic;
        j, k       : in  std_logic;
        q          : out std_logic
    );
end entity jk_flip_flop;

architecture behavioral of jk_flip_flop is
    signal q_internal : std_logic := '0';
begin
    process(clk, reset)
    begin
        if reset = '1' then
            q_internal <= '0';
        elsif rising_edge(clk) then
            case (j & k) is
                when ""00""   => q_internal <= q_internal;       -- hold
                when ""01""   => q_internal <= '0';               -- reset
                when ""10""   => q_internal <= '1';               -- set
                when ""11""   => q_internal <= not q_internal;     -- toggle
                when others => q_internal <= 'X';
            end case;
        end if;
    end process;

    q <= q_internal;
end architecture behavioral;",vhdl;vhdl-latches-flip-flop-types;code,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=vhdl&lesson=vhdl-latches-flip-flop-types,lesson:vhdl/vhdl-latches-flip-flop-types,code
775,Digital Logic Design,VHDL,Latches & Flip-Flop Types in VHDL — JK flip-flop [54360a4d],"An internal signal (q_internal) is used here because an entity′s out port cannot always be read back inside its own architecture -- a common pattern whenever a flip-flop′s current output needs to feed back into its own next-state logic, as the toggle case does here. j & k concatenates the two single-bit inputs into a 2-bit vector so they can be matched together in one case statement.",vhdl;vhdl-latches-flip-flop-types;lesson_passage,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=vhdl&lesson=vhdl-latches-flip-flop-types,lesson:vhdl/vhdl-latches-flip-flop-types,lesson_passage
776,Digital Logic Design,VHDL,Latches & Flip-Flop Types in VHDL — T flip-flop [7d7ec61b],"entity t_flip_flop is
    port (
        clk, reset : in  std_logic;
        t          : in  std_logic;
        q          : out std_logic
    );
end entity t_flip_flop;

architecture behavioral of t_flip_flop is
    signal q_internal : std_logic := '0';
begin
    process(clk, reset)
    begin
        if reset = '1' then
            q_internal <= '0';
        elsif rising_edge(clk) then
            if t = '1' then
                q_internal <= not q_internal;
            end if;
        end if;
    end process;

    q <= q_internal;
end architecture behavioral;",vhdl;vhdl-latches-flip-flop-types;code,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=vhdl&lesson=vhdl-latches-flip-flop-types,lesson:vhdl/vhdl-latches-flip-flop-types,code
777,Digital Logic Design,VHDL,Latches & Flip-Flop Types in VHDL — T flip-flop [535c2c64],"Notice all three flip-flops share the same skeleton: a process sensitive to clk and reset, an asynchronous reset branch, and an elsif rising_edge(clk) branch containing the actual next-state logic -- exactly matching the characteristic-equation table from the Digital Logic Design course, just written in VHDL′s process style instead of Verilog′s always block.",vhdl;vhdl-latches-flip-flop-types;lesson_passage,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=vhdl&lesson=vhdl-latches-flip-flop-types,lesson:vhdl/vhdl-latches-flip-flop-types,lesson_passage
778,Digital Logic Design,VHDL,Registers & Shift Registers in VHDL — A parallel-load register [7a12fb0c],"library ieee;
use ieee.std_logic_1164.all;

entity register_n is
    generic (WIDTH : integer := 8);
    port (
        clk, load : in  std_logic;
        d         : in  std_logic_vector(WIDTH-1 downto 0);
        q         : out std_logic_vector(WIDTH-1 downto 0)
    );
end entity register_n;

architecture behavioral of register_n is
begin
    process(clk)
    begin
        if rising_edge(clk) then
            if load = '1' then
                q <= d;
            end if;
        end if;
    end process;
end architecture behavioral;",vhdl;vhdl-registers-shift-registers;code,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=vhdl&lesson=vhdl-registers-shift-registers,lesson:vhdl/vhdl-registers-shift-registers,code
779,Digital Logic Design,VHDL,"Registers & Shift Registers in VHDL — SIPO shift register (serial-in, parallel-out) [2ba58c06]","entity sipo is
    generic (WIDTH : integer := 8);
    port (
        clk       : in  std_logic;
        serial_in : in  std_logic;
        q         : out std_logic_vector(WIDTH-1 downto 0)
    );
end entity sipo;

architecture behavioral of sipo is
    signal shift_reg : std_logic_vector(WIDTH-1 downto 0) := (others => '0');
begin
    process(clk)
    begin
        if rising_edge(clk) then
            shift_reg <= shift_reg(WIDTH-2 downto 0) & serial_in;
        end if;
    end process;

    q <= shift_reg;
end architecture behavioral;",vhdl;vhdl-registers-shift-registers;code,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=vhdl&lesson=vhdl-registers-shift-registers,lesson:vhdl/vhdl-registers-shift-registers,code
780,Digital Logic Design,VHDL,"Registers & Shift Registers in VHDL — SIPO shift register (serial-in, parallel-out) [6f7ad9dd]","The concatenation shift_reg(WIDTH-2 downto 0) & serial_in builds the new shifted value in one expression -- the VHDL equivalent of Verilog′s {q[WIDTH-2:0], serial_in}.",vhdl;vhdl-registers-shift-registers;lesson_passage,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=vhdl&lesson=vhdl-registers-shift-registers,lesson:vhdl/vhdl-registers-shift-registers,lesson_passage
781,Digital Logic Design,VHDL,Registers & Shift Registers in VHDL — Universal shift register [feaf5c80],"entity universal_shift_reg is
    generic (WIDTH : integer := 8);
    port (
        clk          : in  std_logic;
        mode         : in  std_logic_vector(1 downto 0);  -- 00=hold,01=shift right,10=shift left,11=load
        sin_r, sin_l : in  std_logic;
        d            : in  std_logic_vector(WIDTH-1 downto 0);
        q            : out std_logic_vector(WIDTH-1 downto 0)
    );
end entity universal_shift_reg;

architecture behavioral of universal_shift_reg is
    signal shift_reg : std_logic_vector(WIDTH-1 downto 0) := (others => '0');
begin
    process(clk)
    begin
        if rising_edge(clk) then
            case mode is
                when ""00""   => shift_reg <= shift_reg;
                when ""01""   => shift_reg <= sin_r & shift_reg(WIDTH-1 downto 1);
                when ""10""   => shift_reg <= shift_reg(WIDTH-2 downto 0) & sin_l;
                when ""11""   => shift_reg <= d;
                when others => shift_reg <= (others => 'X');
            end case;
        end if;
    end process;

    q <= shift_reg;
end architecture behavioral;",vhdl;vhdl-registers-shift-registers;code,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=vhdl&lesson=vhdl-registers-shift-registers,lesson:vhdl/vhdl-registers-shift-registers,code
782,Digital Logic Design,VHDL,Counters in VHDL — Free-running binary up counter [4452e66a],"library ieee;
use ieee.std_logic_1164.all;
use ieee.numeric_std.all;

entity up_counter is
    generic (WIDTH : integer := 4);
    port (
        clk, reset : in  std_logic;
        count      : out std_logic_vector(WIDTH-1 downto 0)
    );
end entity up_counter;

architecture behavioral of up_counter is
    signal count_internal : unsigned(WIDTH-1 downto 0) := (others => '0');
begin
    process(clk, reset)
    begin
        if reset = '1' then
            count_internal <= (others => '0');
        elsif rising_edge(clk) then
            count_internal <= count_internal + 1;
        end if;
    end process;

    count <= std_logic_vector(count_internal);
end architecture behavioral;",vhdl;vhdl-counters;code,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=vhdl&lesson=vhdl-counters,lesson:vhdl/vhdl-counters,code
783,Digital Logic Design,VHDL,Counters in VHDL — Free-running binary up counter [8ef4b1cd],"Counting is done on an internal unsigned signal (since + is only defined for numeric types, not directly on std_logic_vector), then converted back to std_logic_vector for the output port -- the same conversion pattern introduced in the VHDL Data Types lesson.",vhdl;vhdl-counters;lesson_passage,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=vhdl&lesson=vhdl-counters,lesson:vhdl/vhdl-counters,lesson_passage
784,Digital Logic Design,VHDL,Counters in VHDL — Up/down counter [889e8429],"entity updown_counter is
    generic (WIDTH : integer := 4);
    port (
        clk, reset : in  std_logic;
        up_down    : in  std_logic;   -- '1' = up, '0' = down
        count      : out std_logic_vector(WIDTH-1 downto 0)
    );
end entity updown_counter;

architecture behavioral of updown_counter is
    signal count_internal : unsigned(WIDTH-1 downto 0) := (others => '0');
begin
    process(clk, reset)
    begin
        if reset = '1' then
            count_internal <= (others => '0');
        elsif rising_edge(clk) then
            if up_down = '1' then
                count_internal <= count_internal + 1;
            else
                count_internal <= count_internal - 1;
            end if;
        end if;
    end process;

    count <= std_logic_vector(count_internal);
end architecture behavioral;",vhdl;vhdl-counters;code,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=vhdl&lesson=vhdl-counters,lesson:vhdl/vhdl-counters,code
785,Digital Logic Design,VHDL,Counters in VHDL — Mod-N (e.g. BCD/decade) counter [e2f9428a],"entity mod_n_counter is
    generic (WIDTH : integer := 4; N : integer := 10);
    port (
        clk, reset : in  std_logic;
        count      : out std_logic_vector(WIDTH-1 downto 0)
    );
end entity mod_n_counter;

architecture behavioral of mod_n_counter is
    signal count_internal : unsigned(WIDTH-1 downto 0) := (others => '0');
begin
    process(clk, reset)
    begin
        if reset = '1' then
            count_internal <= (others => '0');
        elsif rising_edge(clk) then
            if count_internal = N - 1 then
                count_internal <= (others => '0');
            else
                count_internal <= count_internal + 1;
            end if;
        end if;
    end process;

    count <= std_logic_vector(count_internal);
end architecture behavioral;",vhdl;vhdl-counters;code,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=vhdl&lesson=vhdl-counters,lesson:vhdl/vhdl-counters,code
786,Digital Logic Design,VHDL,Counters in VHDL — Mod-N (e.g. BCD/decade) counter [79533035],Setting the N generic to 10 gives the same BCD decade counter from the Digital Logic Design course: it counts 0 through 9 and then wraps back to 0.,vhdl;vhdl-counters;lesson_passage,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=vhdl&lesson=vhdl-counters,lesson:vhdl/vhdl-counters,lesson_passage
787,Digital Logic Design,VHDL,Carry-Lookahead Adders & Generate Statements in VHDL — Computing carries in parallel [1211e676],"library ieee;
use ieee.std_logic_1164.all;

entity cla_adder is
    generic (WIDTH : integer := 4);
    port (
        a, b : in  std_logic_vector(WIDTH-1 downto 0);
        cin  : in  std_logic;
        sum  : out std_logic_vector(WIDTH-1 downto 0);
        cout : out std_logic
    );
end entity cla_adder;

architecture structural of cla_adder is
    signal p, g : std_logic_vector(WIDTH-1 downto 0);
    signal c    : std_logic_vector(WIDTH downto 0);
begin
    p <= a xor b;   -- propagate
    g <= a and b;   -- generate

    c(0) <= cin;

    carry_stage: for i in 0 to WIDTH-1 generate
        c(i+1) <= g(i) or (p(i) and c(i));
    end generate carry_stage;

    sum  <= p xor c(WIDTH-1 downto 0);
    cout <= c(WIDTH);
end architecture structural;",vhdl;vhdl-carry-lookahead-generate;code,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=vhdl&lesson=vhdl-carry-lookahead-generate,lesson:vhdl/vhdl-carry-lookahead-generate,code
788,Digital Logic Design,VHDL,Carry-Lookahead Adders & Generate Statements in VHDL — Computing carries in parallel [ee6b1cd2],"Because every c(i+1) is an independent concurrent signal assignment inside the generate statement, rather than a signal threaded through instantiated full-adder entities, a synthesis tool is free to expand and optimize the whole carry chain into a shallow tree of gates -- the same idea as the carry-lookahead adder from the Digital Logic Design course, and the same trade-off (more gates, much less delay) shown in its Verilog implementation.",vhdl;vhdl-carry-lookahead-generate;lesson_passage,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=vhdl&lesson=vhdl-carry-lookahead-generate,lesson:vhdl/vhdl-carry-lookahead-generate,lesson_passage
789,Digital Logic Design,VHDL,Carry-Lookahead Adders & Generate Statements in VHDL — generate statements beyond adders [56cc325e],"The same for ... generate pattern used here and in the Ripple-Carry Adders lesson scales to building any regular, repeated structure parametrically -- an N-bit ring counter, an array of registers, or a tree of comparators -- without writing out each stage by hand. VHDL also supports if ... generate, which conditionally includes or excludes a block of the architecture at compile time based on a generic value, useful for optional debug logic or alternate implementations selected by a generic.",vhdl;vhdl-carry-lookahead-generate;lesson_passage,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=vhdl&lesson=vhdl-carry-lookahead-generate,lesson:vhdl/vhdl-carry-lookahead-generate,lesson_passage
790,Digital Logic Design,VHDL,Finite State Machines in VHDL — Encoding states with an enumerated type [f00fcbd0],"Rather than hard-coding state values as bit patterns, VHDL lets you define a dedicated enumerated type listing the state names directly -- the compiler (and the synthesis tool) chooses the actual bit encoding, which is both clearer to read and less error-prone than managing the encoding by hand.",vhdl;vhdl-fsm-design;lesson_passage,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=vhdl&lesson=vhdl-fsm-design,lesson:vhdl/vhdl-fsm-design,lesson_passage
791,Digital Logic Design,VHDL,Finite State Machines in VHDL — Encoding states with an enumerated type [d2155f59],"type state_type is (S_IDLE, S_GOT1, S_GOT10, S_MATCH);
signal state, next_state : state_type;",vhdl;vhdl-fsm-design;code,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=vhdl&lesson=vhdl-fsm-design,lesson:vhdl/vhdl-fsm-design,code
792,Digital Logic Design,VHDL,"Finite State Machines in VHDL — Moore sequence detector for ""101"" [2f43086c]","This is the same Moore-machine sequence detector introduced in the Digital Logic Design and Verilog courses, following the identical three-part structure: combinational next-state logic, a clocked state register, and a combinational output equation.",vhdl;vhdl-fsm-design;lesson_passage,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=vhdl&lesson=vhdl-fsm-design,lesson:vhdl/vhdl-fsm-design,lesson_passage
793,Digital Logic Design,VHDL,"Finite State Machines in VHDL — Moore sequence detector for ""101"" [3793d5e2]","library ieee;
use ieee.std_logic_1164.all;

entity seq_detector_101_moore is
    port (
        clk, reset : in  std_logic;
        bit_in     : in  std_logic;
        y          : out std_logic
    );
end entity seq_detector_101_moore;

architecture behavioral of seq_detector_101_moore is
    type state_type is (S_IDLE, S_GOT1, S_GOT10, S_MATCH);
    signal state, next_state : state_type;
begin
    -- next-state logic (combinational)
    process(state, bit_in)
    begin
        case state is
            when S_IDLE =>
                if bit_in = '1' then next_state <= S_GOT1; else next_state <= S_IDLE; end if;
            when S_GOT1 =>
                if bit_in = '1' then next_state <= S_GOT1; else next_state <= S_GOT10; end if;
            when S_GOT10 =>
                if bit_in = '1' then next_state <= S_MATCH; else next_state <= S_IDLE; end if;
            when S_MATCH =>
                if bit_in = '1' then next_state <= S_GOT1; else next_state <= S_GOT10; end if;
        end case;
    end process;

    -- state register (sequential)
    process(clk, reset)
    begin
        if reset = '1' then
            state <= S_IDLE;
        elsif rising_edge(clk) then
            state <= next_state;
        end if;
    end process;

    -- Moore output: depends only on state
    y <= '1' when state = S_MATCH else '0';
end architecture behavioral;",vhdl;vhdl-fsm-design;code,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=vhdl&lesson=vhdl-fsm-design,lesson:vhdl/vhdl-fsm-design,code
794,Digital Logic Design,VHDL,"Finite State Machines in VHDL — Moore sequence detector for ""101"" [4fe45e34]","Because state_type is an enumeration with exactly four possible values, the case statement above already covers every case -- unlike the std_logic_vector-based examples earlier in this course, no when others branch is needed here.",vhdl;vhdl-fsm-design;lesson_passage,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=vhdl&lesson=vhdl-fsm-design,lesson:vhdl/vhdl-fsm-design,lesson_passage
795,Digital Logic Design,VHDL,Finite State Machines in VHDL — Mealy version [bf9fcd74],"A Mealy machine produces its output on the transition, which in VHDL means the output equation reads the current state and the current input, instead of state alone:",vhdl;vhdl-fsm-design;lesson_passage,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=vhdl&lesson=vhdl-fsm-design,lesson:vhdl/vhdl-fsm-design,lesson_passage
796,Digital Logic Design,VHDL,Finite State Machines in VHDL — Mealy version [557a88aa],"With this change, S_MATCH is no longer needed as a separate state at all -- exactly the same Moore-vs-Mealy trade-off the Digital Logic Design and Verilog courses describe for this same detector.",vhdl;vhdl-fsm-design;lesson_passage,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=vhdl&lesson=vhdl-fsm-design,lesson:vhdl/vhdl-fsm-design,lesson_passage
797,Digital Logic Design,VHDL,Modeling Memory in VHDL — Modeling a ROM [ecf4d1c8],"VHDL models memory as an array type -- a signal (or constant) whose elements are themselves vectors, indexed like a small lookup table.",vhdl;vhdl-memory-modeling;lesson_passage,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=vhdl&lesson=vhdl-memory-modeling,lesson:vhdl/vhdl-memory-modeling,lesson_passage
798,Digital Logic Design,VHDL,Modeling Memory in VHDL — Modeling a ROM [81aa033a],"library ieee;
use ieee.std_logic_1164.all;
use ieee.numeric_std.all;

entity rom_16x8 is
    port (
        addr : in  std_logic_vector(3 downto 0);
        data : out std_logic_vector(7 downto 0)
    );
end entity rom_16x8;

architecture behavioral of rom_16x8 is
    type rom_array is array (0 to 15) of std_logic_vector(7 downto 0);
    constant ROM_DATA : rom_array := (
        0 => x""00"",
        1 => x""01"",
        2 => x""04"",
        others => x""00""
    );
begin
    data <= ROM_DATA(to_integer(unsigned(addr)));
end architecture behavioral;",vhdl;vhdl-memory-modeling;code,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=vhdl&lesson=vhdl-memory-modeling,lesson:vhdl/vhdl-memory-modeling,code
799,Digital Logic Design,VHDL,Modeling Memory in VHDL — Modeling a ROM [f27edbf2],"to_integer(unsigned(addr)) converts the address bus into a plain integer usable as an array index -- VHDL arrays are indexed by integers (or another discrete type), not directly by std_logic_vector. The x""00"" syntax is VHDL′s hexadecimal literal notation, equivalent to 8'h00 in Verilog.",vhdl;vhdl-memory-modeling;lesson_passage,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=vhdl&lesson=vhdl-memory-modeling,lesson:vhdl/vhdl-memory-modeling,lesson_passage
800,Digital Logic Design,VHDL,Modeling Memory in VHDL — Modeling a RAM [48beacfd],"entity ram_16x8 is
    port (
        clk          : in  std_logic;
        write_enable : in  std_logic;
        addr         : in  std_logic_vector(3 downto 0);
        data_in      : in  std_logic_vector(7 downto 0);
        data_out     : out std_logic_vector(7 downto 0)
    );
end entity ram_16x8;

architecture behavioral of ram_16x8 is
    type ram_array is array (0 to 15) of std_logic_vector(7 downto 0);
    signal mem : ram_array := (others => (others => '0'));
begin
    process(clk)
    begin
        if rising_edge(clk) then
            if write_enable = '1' then
                mem(to_integer(unsigned(addr))) <= data_in;
            end if;
        end if;
    end process;

    data_out <= mem(to_integer(unsigned(addr)));   -- combinational read
end architecture behavioral;",vhdl;vhdl-memory-modeling;code,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=vhdl&lesson=vhdl-memory-modeling,lesson:vhdl/vhdl-memory-modeling,code
801,Digital Logic Design,VHDL,Modeling Memory in VHDL — Modeling a RAM [e57db4ff],"This is the same combinational-read, synchronous-write style introduced in the Verilog course′s memory-modeling lesson -- the write happens only on a clock edge with write_enable high, while the read is a plain concurrent assignment that always reflects whatever addr currently points to.",vhdl;vhdl-memory-modeling;lesson_passage,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=vhdl&lesson=vhdl-memory-modeling,lesson:vhdl/vhdl-memory-modeling,lesson_passage
802,Digital Logic Design,VHDL,Testbenches & Verification in VHDL — Why testbenches [e5572374],"Just as in the Verilog course, every circuit in this course needs to be checked against its truth table or state diagram before anyone trusts it in real hardware. A VHDL testbench is a separate entity with no ports at all (since it has nothing to connect to outside itself), containing a process that drives the design under test and checks its response.",vhdl;vhdl-testbenches-verification;lesson_passage,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=vhdl&lesson=vhdl-testbenches-verification,lesson:vhdl/vhdl-testbenches-verification,lesson_passage
803,Digital Logic Design,VHDL,Testbenches & Verification in VHDL — A simple testbench for the full adder [97529f8a],"library ieee;
use ieee.std_logic_1164.all;

entity full_adder_tb is
end entity full_adder_tb;

architecture sim of full_adder_tb is
    signal a, b, cin, sum, cout : std_logic;
begin
    dut : entity work.full_adder
        port map (a => a, b => b, cin => cin, sum => sum, cout => cout);

    process
    begin
        a <= '0'; b <= '0'; cin <= '0'; wait for 10 ns;
        a <= '0'; b <= '1'; cin <= '0'; wait for 10 ns;
        a <= '1'; b <= '1'; cin <= '0'; wait for 10 ns;
        a <= '1'; b <= '1'; cin <= '1'; wait for 10 ns;

        assert false report ""Testbench finished"" severity note;
        wait;
    end process;
end architecture sim;",vhdl;vhdl-testbenches-verification;code,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=vhdl&lesson=vhdl-testbenches-verification,lesson:vhdl/vhdl-testbenches-verification,code
804,Digital Logic Design,VHDL,Testbenches & Verification in VHDL — A simple testbench for the full adder [1e3c1fce],"wait for 10 ns; advances simulation time, similar to Verilog′s #10 delay, letting the combinational logic settle before the next input change. The final bare wait; (with no condition) pauses the process forever once the test is done, preventing it from looping back to the beginning and re-running.",vhdl;vhdl-testbenches-verification;lesson_passage,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=vhdl&lesson=vhdl-testbenches-verification,lesson:vhdl/vhdl-testbenches-verification,lesson_passage
805,Digital Logic Design,VHDL,Testbenches & Verification in VHDL — Self-checking with assert [1159e1f1],"Rather than requiring a human to read through a waveform or a log, assert lets a testbench check its own results and report a clear failure automatically:",vhdl;vhdl-testbenches-verification;lesson_passage,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=vhdl&lesson=vhdl-testbenches-verification,lesson:vhdl/vhdl-testbenches-verification,lesson_passage
806,Digital Logic Design,VHDL,Testbenches & Verification in VHDL — Self-checking with assert [f63ecd9c],"process
begin
    a <= '1'; b <= '1'; cin <= '0'; wait for 10 ns;
