865,Electronics,Arduino,Libraries & the Arduino Ecosystem — Commonly used built-in libraries [d1caa468],Libraries bundled with the Arduino IDE | Library | Purpose | Servo.h | Generates the precise pulse timing hobby servos need | Wire.h | I2C communication (see the Communication Protocols lesson) | SPI.h | SPI communication (see the Communication Protocols lesson) | EEPROM.h | Reading and writing the microcontroller′s non-volatile EEPROM memory,arduino;libraries-and-the-arduino-ecosystem;lesson_passage,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=arduino&lesson=libraries-and-the-arduino-ecosystem,lesson:arduino/libraries-and-the-arduino-ecosystem,lesson_passage
866,Electronics,Arduino,"Libraries & the Arduino Ecosystem — Classes and objects, briefly [8729e76b]","Most libraries expose a class -- Servo, for example -- rather than a plain function. Creating a variable of that type (Servo myServo;) makes an object, and calling methods on it (myServo.attach(9), myServo.write(90)) is how the library is actually used. It is entirely possible to use Arduino productively while treating this as ""the syntax the library documentation shows me,"" without first learning object-oriented programming from scratch.",arduino;libraries-and-the-arduino-ecosystem;lesson_passage,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=arduino&lesson=libraries-and-the-arduino-ecosystem,lesson:arduino/libraries-and-the-arduino-ecosystem,lesson_passage
867,Electronics,Arduino,Communication Protocols — Talking to other devices [e70cd0cf],"Microcontrollers rarely work alone -- they need to talk to sensors, displays, and other boards. The three most common protocols on Arduino are Serial (UART), I2C, and SPI.",arduino;arduino-communication-protocols;lesson_passage,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=arduino&lesson=arduino-communication-protocols,lesson:arduino/arduino-communication-protocols,lesson_passage
868,Electronics,Arduino,Communication Protocols — Talking to other devices [f7f0f59b],"Serial vs. I2C vs. SPI | Protocol | Wires | Typical speed | Devices per bus | Typical use | Serial (UART) | 2 (TX, RX) | Up to ~1 Mbps | 1-to-1 | USB debugging, GPS modules, Bluetooth modules | I2C | 2 (SDA, SCL) | 100 kHz-400 kHz typical | Many (addressed) | Sensors, small displays, low-speed peripherals | SPI | 4 (MOSI, MISO, SCK, CS) | Several MHz+ | Many (one CS pin each) | SD cards, fast displays, high-speed sensors",arduino;arduino-communication-protocols;lesson_passage,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=arduino&lesson=arduino-communication-protocols,lesson:arduino/arduino-communication-protocols,lesson_passage
869,Electronics,Arduino,Communication Protocols — Example: reading an I2C sensor [188936b1],"#include <Wire.h>

void setup() {
    Wire.begin();
    Serial.begin(9600);
}

void loop() {
    Wire.requestFrom(0x68, 1);   // request 1 byte from device at address 0x68
    if (Wire.available()) {
        byte data = Wire.read();
        Serial.println(data);
    }
    delay(100);
}",arduino;arduino-communication-protocols;code,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=arduino&lesson=arduino-communication-protocols,lesson:arduino/arduino-communication-protocols,code
870,Electronics,Arduino,Interrupts — Why interrupts [1cbb29d7],"Polling a pin inside loop() only checks it whenever loop() happens to reach that line -- a fast or brief event (a button press, an encoder pulse, an incoming signal edge) can be missed entirely if the rest of loop() is busy doing something else at that moment. An interrupt instead pauses whatever the processor is doing and immediately runs a dedicated function the moment a specified event occurs.",arduino;interrupts-in-arduino;lesson_passage,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=arduino&lesson=interrupts-in-arduino,lesson:arduino/interrupts-in-arduino,lesson_passage
871,Electronics,Arduino,Interrupts — attachInterrupt() [2b0b32e7],"volatile bool buttonPressed = false;

void setup() {
    pinMode(2, INPUT_PULLUP);
    attachInterrupt(digitalPinToInterrupt(2), onButtonPress, FALLING);
}

void onButtonPress() {
    buttonPressed = true;
}

void loop() {
    if (buttonPressed) {
        buttonPressed = false;
        // handle the button press here, outside the interrupt itself
    }
}",arduino;interrupts-in-arduino;code,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=arduino&lesson=interrupts-in-arduino,lesson:arduino/interrupts-in-arduino,code
872,Electronics,Arduino,Interrupts — attachInterrupt() [08e81ebd],"Only certain pins support external interrupts (commonly just two on smaller AVR boards), and the trigger condition -- RISING, FALLING, or CHANGE -- determines which edge of the signal fires the interrupt.",arduino;interrupts-in-arduino;lesson_passage,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=arduino&lesson=interrupts-in-arduino,lesson:arduino/interrupts-in-arduino,lesson_passage
873,Electronics,Arduino,Interrupts — Rules for writing an interrupt service routine (ISR) [99306288],"Keep it as short as possible -- normal program execution, and any other interrupts, are paused while it runs.",arduino;interrupts-in-arduino;lesson_passage,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=arduino&lesson=interrupts-in-arduino,lesson:arduino/interrupts-in-arduino,lesson_passage
874,Electronics,Arduino,Interrupts — Rules for writing an interrupt service routine (ISR) [4fc42c9c],"Any variable shared between an ISR and the rest of the program must be declared volatile, so the compiler does not wrongly assume it never changes and optimize away a check of its value.",arduino;interrupts-in-arduino;lesson_passage,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=arduino&lesson=interrupts-in-arduino,lesson:arduino/interrupts-in-arduino,lesson_passage
875,Electronics,Arduino,Interrupts — Rules for writing an interrupt service routine (ISR) [0d2d9131],The usual pattern is to have the ISR do the absolute minimum -- typically just setting a flag or a variable -- and let loop() do the real work once it next checks that flag.,arduino;interrupts-in-arduino;lesson_passage,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=arduino&lesson=interrupts-in-arduino,lesson:arduino/interrupts-in-arduino,lesson_passage
876,Electronics,Arduino,Interrupts — Debouncing a mechanical button [6d274b93],"A mechanical switch does not transition cleanly -- it ""bounces,"" producing several rapid, spurious transitions over a few milliseconds as the contacts physically settle. Both interrupt-driven and polled button code typically need debouncing: ignoring further changes for a short window (often 10-50 ms) after the first detected transition.",arduino;interrupts-in-arduino;lesson_passage,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=arduino&lesson=interrupts-in-arduino,lesson:arduino/interrupts-in-arduino,lesson_passage
877,Electronics,Arduino,Timers & Non-Blocking Timing — The problem with delay() [51a40d37],"delay() is easy to use but blocks everything -- no other code runs, no pins are read, no other timed events can happen, for the entire duration of the delay. A sketch that needs to blink an LED, read a sensor, and check a button all at their own independent rates cannot do all three with delay() alone.",arduino;timers-and-non-blocking-timing;lesson_passage,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=arduino&lesson=timers-and-non-blocking-timing,lesson:arduino/timers-and-non-blocking-timing,lesson_passage
878,Electronics,Arduino,Timers & Non-Blocking Timing — millis() and the standard non-blocking pattern [2c8cbe40],"millis() returns the number of milliseconds since the board was last reset, and keeps counting in the background regardless of what loop() is doing. Comparing it against a saved timestamp lets a sketch check ""has enough time passed yet?"" without ever blocking:",arduino;timers-and-non-blocking-timing;lesson_passage,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=arduino&lesson=timers-and-non-blocking-timing,lesson:arduino/timers-and-non-blocking-timing,lesson_passage
879,Electronics,Arduino,Timers & Non-Blocking Timing — millis() and the standard non-blocking pattern [e05d3ac3],"unsigned long lastBlinkTime = 0;
const unsigned long blinkInterval = 500;   // ms
bool ledState = false;

void loop() {
    unsigned long now = millis();
    if (now - lastBlinkTime >= blinkInterval) {
        lastBlinkTime = now;
        ledState = !ledState;
        digitalWrite(LED_PIN, ledState);
    }

    // ... other code runs every loop() iteration, completely unaffected ...
}",arduino;timers-and-non-blocking-timing;code,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=arduino&lesson=timers-and-non-blocking-timing,lesson:arduino/timers-and-non-blocking-timing,code
880,Electronics,Arduino,Timers & Non-Blocking Timing — millis() and the standard non-blocking pattern [bff6d56b],"Using subtraction (now - lastBlinkTime) rather than comparing absolute times directly is a deliberate habit: millis() eventually overflows and wraps back to 0 after about 49.7 days, and unsigned subtraction still produces the correct elapsed time across that wraparound, while comparing absolute timestamps directly would not.",arduino;timers-and-non-blocking-timing;lesson_passage,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=arduino&lesson=timers-and-non-blocking-timing,lesson:arduino/timers-and-non-blocking-timing,lesson_passage
881,Electronics,Arduino,Timers & Non-Blocking Timing — micros() for finer resolution [af07e2f9],"micros() works the same way but returns microseconds instead of milliseconds, useful for timing that needs finer resolution than a millisecond -- at the cost of overflowing much sooner, after about 70 minutes.",arduino;timers-and-non-blocking-timing;lesson_passage,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=arduino&lesson=timers-and-non-blocking-timing,lesson:arduino/timers-and-non-blocking-timing,lesson_passage
882,Electronics,Arduino,Timers & Non-Blocking Timing — State machines: scaling up the same idea [72507c86],"The same ""check the time, act, remember the new timestamp"" pattern generalizes into a state machine, where a variable tracks which phase of a multi-step timed sequence the program is currently in (e.g. a traffic light cycling through red, green, and yellow) -- the same concept, and often the same state-diagram design process, as the Finite State Machines lesson in the Digital Logic Design course, just implemented in software with millis() in place of a hardware clock signal.",arduino;timers-and-non-blocking-timing;lesson_passage,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=arduino&lesson=timers-and-non-blocking-timing,lesson:arduino/timers-and-non-blocking-timing,lesson_passage
883,Electronics,Arduino,Reading Sensors — Digital sensors and pull-up/pull-down resistors [470eb583],"A simple digital sensor (a button, a magnetic door switch, many PIR motion sensors) just needs to present a clean HIGH or LOW to a digital input pin. An unconnected (""floating"") digital input reads unpredictably, picking up electrical noise -- a pull-up or pull-down resistor guarantees a known default state when the sensor is not actively driving the pin.",arduino;reading-sensors;lesson_passage,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=arduino&lesson=reading-sensors,lesson:arduino/reading-sensors,lesson_passage
884,Electronics,Arduino,Reading Sensors — Digital sensors and pull-up/pull-down resistors [02be1076],"pinMode(BUTTON_PIN, INPUT_PULLUP);   // most AVR boards have this built in -- no external resistor needed

void loop() {
