885,Electronics,Arduino,Reading Sensors — Digital sensors and pull-up/pull-down resistors [2c98aaf4],INPUT_PULLUP enables an internal resistor that pulls the pin HIGH by default; wiring a button between the pin and ground then pulls it LOW only while pressed -- which is why the pressed condition reads LOW rather than HIGH with this common wiring style.,arduino;reading-sensors;lesson_passage,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=arduino&lesson=reading-sensors,lesson:arduino/reading-sensors,lesson_passage
886,Electronics,Arduino,Reading Sensors — Analog sensors [82139b9f],"analogRead() reads a voltage between 0 and the board′s reference voltage (5 V or 3.3 V, depending on the board) and returns a number from 0 to 1023 (a 10-bit reading on most AVR boards).",arduino;reading-sensors;lesson_passage,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=arduino&lesson=reading-sensors,lesson:arduino/reading-sensors,lesson_passage
887,Electronics,Arduino,Reading Sensors — Analog sensors [84ffd031],"Common analog sensors | Sensor | What it varies | Typical wiring | Potentiometer | Resistance, mechanically, as a knob turns | A simple voltage divider between power and ground, with the wiper going to an analog pin | LDR (light-dependent resistor) | Resistance, based on light level | Voltage divider with a fixed resistor | Thermistor | Resistance, based on temperature | Voltage divider with a fixed resistor",arduino;reading-sensors;lesson_passage,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=arduino&lesson=reading-sensors,lesson:arduino/reading-sensors,lesson_passage
