892,Electronics,Arduino,Driving Actuators — Why a microcontroller pin usually cannot drive a motor directly [04ccc579],"An Arduino pin can typically supply only about 20-40 mA safely, and the whole board′s regulator has limits too -- nowhere near enough for a DC motor, solenoid, or anything but the smallest LED. Anything that draws significant current needs to be switched by a separate device that the microcontroller merely controls, not powers directly.",arduino;driving-actuators-with-arduino;lesson_passage,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=arduino&lesson=driving-actuators-with-arduino,lesson:arduino/driving-actuators-with-arduino,lesson_passage
893,Electronics,Arduino,Driving Actuators — LEDs: current limiting [0fe18731],"An LED without a series resistor will draw far more current than it or the driving pin can safely handle. A resistor sized with Ohm′s Law (from the Electricity & Electronics course), using the LED′s forward voltage and a safe target current (often 10-20 mA), protects both the LED and the pin.",arduino;driving-actuators-with-arduino;lesson_passage,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=arduino&lesson=driving-actuators-with-arduino,lesson:arduino/driving-actuators-with-arduino,lesson_passage
894,Electronics,Arduino,Driving Actuators — Switching higher-current loads: transistors and MOSFETs [f3278105],"A small signal transistor or MOSFET, driven by a digital pin, can switch a much larger current than the pin itself could -- the pin only needs to supply the small base/gate current (or, for a MOSFET, essentially no steady current at all) needed to turn the switch fully on.",arduino;driving-actuators-with-arduino;lesson_passage,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=arduino&lesson=driving-actuators-with-arduino,lesson:arduino/driving-actuators-with-arduino,lesson_passage
895,Electronics,Arduino,Driving Actuators — Switching higher-current loads: transistors and MOSFETs [efedebeb],"digitalWrite(motorControlPin, HIGH);   // turns the transistor/MOSFET fully on,
                                         // which switches the actual motor current",arduino;driving-actuators-with-arduino;code,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=arduino&lesson=driving-actuators-with-arduino,lesson:arduino/driving-actuators-with-arduino,code
896,Electronics,Arduino,Driving Actuators — Switching higher-current loads: transistors and MOSFETs [26573971],"A flyback diode across any motor, relay coil, or solenoid is essential: switching off a suddenly de-energized inductive load (see the Inductors & Inductance lesson in the Electricity & Electronics course) generates a voltage spike that can destroy the switching transistor without one.",arduino;driving-actuators-with-arduino;lesson_passage,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=arduino&lesson=driving-actuators-with-arduino,lesson:arduino/driving-actuators-with-arduino,lesson_passage
897,Electronics,Arduino,Driving Actuators — DC motors and H-bridges [1e6ca755],"Controlling a DC motor′s direction, not just its on/off state, needs an H-bridge -- a circuit of four switches described in detail in the Robotics course′s Motor Control lesson. Motor driver modules and ICs (like the L298N or DRV8833) package an H-bridge behind a simple two- or three-pin interface that digitalWrite() and analogWrite() can drive directly.",arduino;driving-actuators-with-arduino;lesson_passage,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=arduino&lesson=driving-actuators-with-arduino,lesson:arduino/driving-actuators-with-arduino,lesson_passage
898,Electronics,Arduino,Driving Actuators — Servo motors [a9ac0d51],"As covered in the PWM lesson, hobby servos are controlled with the Servo library rather than plain PWM duty cycle:",arduino;driving-actuators-with-arduino;lesson_passage,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=arduino&lesson=driving-actuators-with-arduino,lesson:arduino/driving-actuators-with-arduino,lesson_passage
