900,Electronics,Arduino,Driving Actuators — Relays [d6a2124e],"A relay uses a small electromagnet to physically open or close a separate, electrically isolated switch contact -- useful for controlling AC mains-powered devices (lamps, appliances) from a low-voltage microcontroller pin, since the microcontroller side never directly touches the higher, more dangerous voltage.",arduino;driving-actuators-with-arduino;lesson_passage,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=arduino&lesson=driving-actuators-with-arduino,lesson:arduino/driving-actuators-with-arduino,lesson_passage
901,Electronics,Arduino,EEPROM & Persistent Storage — Why EEPROM [70f52955],"SRAM (where regular variables live) loses its contents the instant power is removed. EEPROM (Electrically Erasable Programmable Read-Only Memory) is a small block of memory that keeps its contents even with no power at all -- useful for settings, calibration values, or counters that need to survive a reset or power loss.",arduino;eeprom-and-persistent-storage;lesson_passage,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=arduino&lesson=eeprom-and-persistent-storage,lesson:arduino/eeprom-and-persistent-storage,lesson_passage
902,Electronics,Arduino,EEPROM & Persistent Storage — Reading and writing EEPROM [9cd0918a],"#include <EEPROM.h>

void setup() {
    EEPROM.write(0, 42);           // write the byte 42 to EEPROM address 0
    byte value = EEPROM.read(0);   // read it back
}",arduino;eeprom-and-persistent-storage;code,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=arduino&lesson=eeprom-and-persistent-storage,lesson:arduino/eeprom-and-persistent-storage,code
903,Electronics,Arduino,EEPROM & Persistent Storage — Reading and writing EEPROM [15f879fa],"For anything larger than a single byte -- an int, a float, or a whole struct -- the library also provides EEPROM.put() and EEPROM.get(), which handle the multi-byte layout automatically.",arduino;eeprom-and-persistent-storage;lesson_passage,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=arduino&lesson=eeprom-and-persistent-storage,lesson:arduino/eeprom-and-persistent-storage,lesson_passage
904,Electronics,Arduino,EEPROM & Persistent Storage — EEPROM has a limited lifespan [352d5306],"A typical AVR EEPROM cell is rated for roughly 100,000 write cycles before it becomes unreliable -- reads are unlimited, but repeatedly writing the same address (for example, every single time through loop()) can wear it out surprisingly quickly. EEPROM.update() writes a value only if it is actually different from what is already stored, avoiding wasted write cycles when the value has not changed.",arduino;eeprom-and-persistent-storage;lesson_passage,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=arduino&lesson=eeprom-and-persistent-storage,lesson:arduino/eeprom-and-persistent-storage,lesson_passage
905,Electronics,Arduino,EEPROM & Persistent Storage — Other options for larger or more frequent storage [6c8dd2bc],"Storage options beyond built-in EEPROM | Option | When to reach for it | SD card module | Logging large amounts of data, or data that changes frequently | External I2C/SPI EEPROM or FRAM chip | Need more storage than the built-in EEPROM, or (FRAM) far more write cycles | Flash-based storage (e.g. via a filesystem library) | Boards without a traditional EEPROM peripheral",arduino;eeprom-and-persistent-storage;lesson_passage,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=arduino&lesson=eeprom-and-persistent-storage,lesson:arduino/eeprom-and-persistent-storage,lesson_passage
906,Electronics,Arduino,Power Management & Sleep Modes — Why power matters [5e8197c2],"A board plugged into a wall adapter can ignore power consumption almost entirely. A battery-powered project cannot: a sensor node that draws a few milliamps continuously might run for days, while the same node put to sleep between readings and waking only briefly can often run for months or years on the same battery.",arduino;power-management-and-sleep-modes;lesson_passage,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=arduino&lesson=power-management-and-sleep-modes,lesson:arduino/power-management-and-sleep-modes,lesson_passage
907,Electronics,Arduino,Power Management & Sleep Modes — Where the power goes [330cfac3],"Typical current draw by activity (illustrative, board- and code-dependent) | State | Typical current | Actively running, radio/peripherals on | Tens of milliamps | Actively running, idle CPU | Several milliamps to tens of milliamps | Deep sleep mode | Microamps or less, depending on the board",arduino;power-management-and-sleep-modes;lesson_passage,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=arduino&lesson=power-management-and-sleep-modes,lesson:arduino/power-management-and-sleep-modes,lesson_passage
908,Electronics,Arduino,Power Management & Sleep Modes — Sleep modes [ddf3507d],"Most microcontrollers support several sleep modes that trade wake-up latency and which peripherals stay active against how little power is drawn. On AVR boards, the avr/sleep.h header provides several modes, from a light ""idle"" sleep (CPU stopped, timers and interrupts still running) down to ""power down"" (almost everything stopped, woken only by specific external events like a pin-change interrupt or the watchdog timer).",arduino;power-management-and-sleep-modes;lesson_passage,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=arduino&lesson=power-management-and-sleep-modes,lesson:arduino/power-management-and-sleep-modes,lesson_passage
909,Electronics,Arduino,Power Management & Sleep Modes — Sleep modes [ac77faf1],"#include <avr/sleep.h>

void enterSleep() {
    set_sleep_mode(SLEEP_MODE_PWR_DOWN);
    sleep_enable();
    sleep_mode();     // execution pauses here until an interrupt wakes the board
    sleep_disable();
}",arduino;power-management-and-sleep-modes;code,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=arduino&lesson=power-management-and-sleep-modes,lesson:arduino/power-management-and-sleep-modes,code
910,Electronics,Arduino,Power Management & Sleep Modes — The watchdog timer [13322687],"A watchdog timer can do two different jobs: waking a sleeping board on a schedule (since deep sleep otherwise stops even the normal millis() timer), and acting as a safety net that resets the board automatically if the main program ever hangs and fails to ""check in"" (reset the watchdog) within an expected interval.",arduino;power-management-and-sleep-modes;lesson_passage,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=arduino&lesson=power-management-and-sleep-modes,lesson:arduino/power-management-and-sleep-modes,lesson_passage
911,Electronics,Arduino,Power Management & Sleep Modes — Practical low-power habits [4bc8d4a9],"Turn off peripherals (sensors, radios, displays) when they are not actively needed, rather than leaving them powered continuously.",arduino;power-management-and-sleep-modes;lesson_passage,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=arduino&lesson=power-management-and-sleep-modes,lesson:arduino/power-management-and-sleep-modes,lesson_passage
912,Electronics,Arduino,Power Management & Sleep Modes — Practical low-power habits [57698510],"Reduce clock speed if the application does not need full processing speed, since power draw generally scales with clock frequency.",arduino;power-management-and-sleep-modes;lesson_passage,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=arduino&lesson=power-management-and-sleep-modes,lesson:arduino/power-management-and-sleep-modes,lesson_passage
913,Electronics,Arduino,Power Management & Sleep Modes — Practical low-power habits [2a2a1694],"Choose components (sensors, voltage regulators) rated for low quiescent current when battery life matters.",arduino;power-management-and-sleep-modes;lesson_passage,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=arduino&lesson=power-management-and-sleep-modes,lesson:arduino/power-management-and-sleep-modes,lesson_passage
914,Electronics,Arduino,Debugging & Best Practices — Serial.print() as a debugging tool [f49a9ae7],"void setup() {
    Serial.begin(9600);
}

void loop() {
    int reading = analogRead(A0);
    Serial.print(""Sensor reading: "");
    Serial.println(reading);
    delay(500);
}",arduino;debugging-and-best-practices;code,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=arduino&lesson=debugging-and-best-practices,lesson:arduino/debugging-and-best-practices,code
915,Electronics,Arduino,Debugging & Best Practices — Serial.print() as a debugging tool [a2ab7de6],"Without a debugger attached (which most Arduino setups do not have), printing values to the Serial Monitor is the primary way to see what a sketch is actually doing -- confirm a variable′s value, confirm a function was actually called, or confirm the timing between two events.",arduino;debugging-and-best-practices;lesson_passage,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=arduino&lesson=debugging-and-best-practices,lesson:arduino/debugging-and-best-practices,lesson_passage
916,Electronics,Arduino,Debugging & Best Practices — Common Arduino bugs [cf35558c],"Frequent beginner mistakes | Symptom | Likely cause | Whole sketch appears frozen or unresponsive | A blocking delay(), or a while loop waiting on a condition that never becomes true | A variable shared with an interrupt seems to update itself randomly, or a flag set inside an interrupt never seems to be noticed | The variable was not declared volatile | Sketch runs fine briefly, then crashes or behaves erratically | Running out of SRAM, often from excessive String concatenation | Digital input reads randomly HIGH and LOW with nothing connected | A floating input pin with no pull-up/pull-down resistor | analogWrite() has no visible effect | Using a pin that does not support PWM (missing the ~ marking)",arduino;debugging-and-best-practices;lesson_passage,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=arduino&lesson=debugging-and-best-practices,lesson:arduino/debugging-and-best-practices,lesson_passage
917,Electronics,Arduino,Debugging & Best Practices — Checking memory usage [cf48f876],"The Arduino IDE reports program storage (Flash) and dynamic memory (SRAM) usage after every compile. An Uno has only 2 KB of SRAM total, shared between global variables, the call stack, and anything the String class or other dynamic allocation needs -- a sketch that is close to that limit is at real risk of unpredictable crashes as available memory shrinks further at runtime.",arduino;debugging-and-best-practices;lesson_passage,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=arduino&lesson=debugging-and-best-practices,lesson:arduino/debugging-and-best-practices,lesson_passage
918,Electronics,Arduino,Debugging & Best Practices — Code organization habits that scale [56c969ac],"Give pins and constants meaningful names with const int, rather than scattering unexplained numbers (""magic numbers"") through the code.",arduino;debugging-and-best-practices;lesson_passage,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=arduino&lesson=debugging-and-best-practices,lesson:arduino/debugging-and-best-practices,lesson_passage
919,Electronics,Arduino,Debugging & Best Practices — Code organization habits that scale [5258f1a1],Break repeated logic out into functions instead of copy-pasting the same block repeatedly.,arduino;debugging-and-best-practices;lesson_passage,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=arduino&lesson=debugging-and-best-practices,lesson:arduino/debugging-and-best-practices,lesson_passage
920,Electronics,Arduino,Debugging & Best Practices — Code organization habits that scale [d318e14c],Keep loop() non-blocking (see the Timers & Non-Blocking Timing lesson) so the sketch stays responsive as more features are added.,arduino;debugging-and-best-practices;lesson_passage,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=arduino&lesson=debugging-and-best-practices,lesson:arduino/debugging-and-best-practices,lesson_passage
921,Electronics,Arduino,Debugging & Best Practices — Code organization habits that scale [1c9fd8dc],"Comment the why behind a non-obvious choice, not the what a line already makes clear from its own code.",arduino;debugging-and-best-practices;lesson_passage,https://munsakad.github.io/Hardware-Engineering-Education-Hub/pages/course.html?slug=arduino&lesson=debugging-and-best-practices,lesson:arduino/debugging-and-best-practices,lesson_passage
922,Electronics,Worked calculations,LED current limiting: case 01,"For an ideal 3.3 V supply, an assumed LED forward drop of 2 V and a target current of 2 mA, R=(Vs−Vf)/I=(3.3−2)/0.002=650 ohms. Resistor dissipation is I²R=0.0026 W. Choose the next suitable standard resistance, then recheck current and power at supply and LED tolerances; verify the actual LED datasheet.",worked_example;led_current_limiting,https://github.com/munsakad/DMHardware-Chat-Bot/blob/main/expand_knowledge.py,calculation:LED current limiting,generated_worked_example
923,Electronics,Worked calculations,Unloaded voltage divider: case 01,"With a 5 V input, upper resistor R1=1000 ohms and lower resistor R2=1000 ohms, the unloaded output is Vout=Vin×R2/(R1+R2)=2.5 V. The Thevenin output resistance is R1||R2=500 ohms. This assumes ideal resistors and no load; a finite load parallels R2 and reduces the output.",worked_example;unloaded_voltage_divider,https://github.com/munsakad/DMHardware-Chat-Bot/blob/main/expand_knowledge.py,calculation:Unloaded voltage divider,generated_worked_example
924,Electronics,Worked calculations,RC low-pass cutoff: case 01,"For an ideal first-order RC low-pass with R=1000 ohms and C=1 nF, fc=1/(2πRC)=1.5915e+05 Hz and the time constant RC=1e-06 s. The amplitude at fc is 1/√2 of the low-frequency amplitude (about −3.01 dB). Source resistance and load impedance alter the real response.",worked_example;rc_low-pass_cutoff,https://github.com/munsakad/DMHardware-Chat-Bot/blob/main/expand_knowledge.py,calculation:RC low-pass cutoff,generated_worked_example
925,Electronics,Worked calculations,Capacitor step settling: case 01,An initially discharged ideal capacitor of 1 nF charges through 1000 ohms from a 3.3 V step. The time constant is τ=RC=1e-06 s. Reaching 99% of the final voltage takes t=−RC ln(0.01)=4.6052e-06 s. This assumes a first-order circuit with no additional load or current limit.,worked_example;capacitor_step_settling,https://github.com/munsakad/DMHardware-Chat-Bot/blob/main/expand_knowledge.py,calculation:Capacitor step settling,generated_worked_example
926,PCB Design,Worked calculations,Open-drain bus rise time: case 01,"For an open-drain bus with pull-up resistance 1000 ohms and estimated capacitance 50 pF, the first-order 30%–70% rise time is tr=ln(0.7/0.3)RC≈0.8473RC=42.36 ns. At 3.3 V, ideal low-state pull-up current is about 3.3 mA. Check the protocol timing and the device's sink-current and VOL limits; this estimate does not certify compliance.",worked_example;open-drain_bus_rise_time,https://github.com/munsakad/DMHardware-Chat-Bot/blob/main/expand_knowledge.py,calculation:Open-drain bus rise time,generated_worked_example
927,Electronics,Worked calculations,Ideal ADC quantization: case 01,"An ideal 8-bit ADC with a 1.1 V reference has 2^8=256 quantization bins. Using the ideal bin-width convention, one LSB=Vref/2^N=0.00429688 V. This is quantization spacing, not guaranteed accuracy. Reference error, noise, input acquisition, offset and gain error can dominate; consult the converter's transfer-function convention.",worked_example;ideal_adc_quantization,https://github.com/munsakad/DMHardware-Chat-Bot/blob/main/expand_knowledge.py,calculation:Ideal ADC quantization,generated_worked_example
928,Robotics,Worked calculations,Encoder speed estimate: case 01,"An encoder produces 100 counted edges per revolution at the chosen decoding mode. If 50 edges are measured in 0.1 s, speed=edges/(edges-per-revolution×interval)=5 rev/s, or 300 RPM. Use counts per revolution after quadrature decoding, not a vendor's ambiguous PPR label, and account for any gearbox ratio.",worked_example;encoder_speed_estimate,https://github.com/munsakad/DMHardware-Chat-Bot/blob/main/expand_knowledge.py,calculation:Encoder speed estimate,generated_worked_example
929,Digital Logic Design,Worked calculations,Counter event frequency: case 01,"A 16 MHz timer clock divided by prescaler 1 drives a counter that emits an event after counting from 0 through 99 inclusive. There are TOP+1=100 ticks per event, so fevent=16000000/(1×100)=160000 Hz. A pin toggled once per event has half this square-wave frequency. Verify the timer mode, register width and clock source for actual hardware.",worked_example;counter_event_frequency,https://github.com/munsakad/DMHardware-Chat-Bot/blob/main/expand_knowledge.py,calculation:Counter event frequency,generated_worked_example
930,Electronics,Worked calculations,LED current limiting: case 02,"For an ideal 5 V supply, an assumed LED forward drop of 2 V and a target current of 2 mA, R=(Vs−Vf)/I=(5−2)/0.002=1500 ohms. Resistor dissipation is I²R=0.006 W. Choose the next suitable standard resistance, then recheck current and power at supply and LED tolerances; verify the actual LED datasheet.",worked_example;led_current_limiting,https://github.com/munsakad/DMHardware-Chat-Bot/blob/main/expand_knowledge.py,calculation:LED current limiting,generated_worked_example
