1007,Electronics,Worked calculations,Ideal ADC quantization: case 11,"An ideal 12-bit ADC with a 3.3 V reference has 2^12=4096 quantization bins. Using the ideal bin-width convention, one LSB=Vref/2^N=0.000805664 V. This is quantization spacing, not guaranteed accuracy. Reference error, noise, input acquisition, offset and gain error can dominate; consult the converter's transfer-function convention.",worked_example;ideal_adc_quantization,https://github.com/munsakad/DMHardware-Chat-Bot/blob/main/expand_knowledge.py,calculation:Ideal ADC quantization,generated_worked_example
1008,Robotics,Worked calculations,Encoder speed estimate: case 11,"An encoder produces 500 counted edges per revolution at the chosen decoding mode. If 250 edges are measured in 0.1 s, speed=edges/(edges-per-revolution×interval)=5 rev/s, or 300 RPM. Use counts per revolution after quadrature decoding, not a vendor's ambiguous PPR label, and account for any gearbox ratio.",worked_example;encoder_speed_estimate,https://github.com/munsakad/DMHardware-Chat-Bot/blob/main/expand_knowledge.py,calculation:Encoder speed estimate,generated_worked_example
1009,Digital Logic Design,Worked calculations,Counter event frequency: case 11,"A 16 MHz timer clock divided by prescaler 64 drives a counter that emits an event after counting from 0 through 999 inclusive. There are TOP+1=1000 ticks per event, so fevent=16000000/(64×1000)=250 Hz. A pin toggled once per event has half this square-wave frequency. Verify the timer mode, register width and clock source for actual hardware.",worked_example;counter_event_frequency,https://github.com/munsakad/DMHardware-Chat-Bot/blob/main/expand_knowledge.py,calculation:Counter event frequency,generated_worked_example
1010,Electronics,Worked calculations,LED current limiting: case 12,"For an ideal 12 V supply, an assumed LED forward drop of 2 V and a target current of 8 mA, R=(Vs−Vf)/I=(12−2)/0.008=1250 ohms. Resistor dissipation is I²R=0.08 W. Choose the next suitable standard resistance, then recheck current and power at supply and LED tolerances; verify the actual LED datasheet.",worked_example;led_current_limiting,https://github.com/munsakad/DMHardware-Chat-Bot/blob/main/expand_knowledge.py,calculation:LED current limiting,generated_worked_example
1011,Electronics,Worked calculations,Unloaded voltage divider: case 12,"With a 5 V input, upper resistor R1=10000 ohms and lower resistor R2=6800 ohms, the unloaded output is Vout=Vin×R2/(R1+R2)=2.024 V. The Thevenin output resistance is R1||R2=4048 ohms. This assumes ideal resistors and no load; a finite load parallels R2 and reduces the output.",worked_example;unloaded_voltage_divider,https://github.com/munsakad/DMHardware-Chat-Bot/blob/main/expand_knowledge.py,calculation:Unloaded voltage divider,generated_worked_example
1012,Electronics,Worked calculations,RC low-pass cutoff: case 12,"For an ideal first-order RC low-pass with R=47000 ohms and C=100 nF, fc=1/(2πRC)=33.863 Hz and the time constant RC=0.0047 s. The amplitude at fc is 1/√2 of the low-frequency amplitude (about −3.01 dB). Source resistance and load impedance alter the real response.",worked_example;rc_low-pass_cutoff,https://github.com/munsakad/DMHardware-Chat-Bot/blob/main/expand_knowledge.py,calculation:RC low-pass cutoff,generated_worked_example
